Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_incontext_transmission", "Title": "No Gradients Required? Testing Whether Subliminal Traits Transmit Through In-Context Learning Alone", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only ever been demonstrated via weight-updating fine-tuning, and the leading account says it requires learning an aligned steering vector via activation gradients under adaptive optimizers. We hypothesize that because in-context learning behaves as an implicit meta-optimizer, a matched-architecture student can partially absorb a teacher's hidden trait purely from trait-free number/code sequences placed in its *prompt* (no weight updates at all) - but that this in-context subliminal transfer is strictly weaker than, and mechanistically distinct from, fine-tuning-based transfer, revealing a sharp gradient-vs-context dividing line for hidden-trait propagation.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) introduced subliminal learning and demonstrated it only via fine-tuning on teacher-generated data, proving a gradient-alignment result. Blank et al. (2026) showed subliminal learning IS steering-vector distillation: the student learns an aligned steering vector over fine-tuning, and that adaptive optimizers are necessary because activation gradients on steered data carry the signal - strongly implying weight updates are essential. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers, again in the fine-tuning setting. Separately, an ICL-as-meta-optimization literature (Batch-ICL 2024; induction-head emergence work 2024-2025) argues in-context examples induce implicit meta-gradients on the forward pass. Command-V (2025) transfers behaviors without backprop but via explicit activation-profile patching, not via ordinary prompting. NO prior work asks whether the subliminal channel operates through in-context learning alone - i.e., whether trait-free number lists in the *prompt* (zero weight change) bias a student's behavior. This is the first test of the phenomenon in the gradient-free ICL regime, and it directly pits the steering-vector-distillation account (which predicts failure without weight updates) against the ICL-as-meta-optimizer account (which predicts partial success), yielding a clean falsifiable dividing line distinct from all prior fine-tuning-only work and from my own prior fine-tuning-based proposals.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning - the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences - has been demonstrated exclusively through weight-updating fine-tuning, and the leading mechanistic account frames it as steering-vector distillation that requires learning an aligned vector via activation gradients. We ask a simple, previously untested question: does the subliminal channel need gradients at all? Concretely, if we place a teacher's trait-free number/code sequences into a student's *context window* and run only forward passes, does the student's downstream behavior shift toward the teacher's hidden trait? Two theories make opposite predictions: the steering-vector-distillation account implies transfer should fail without weight updates, whereas the view of in-context learning as an implicit meta-optimizer predicts partial, gradient-free absorption. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with no fine-tuning - only prompting, making the study extremely cheap for an academic lab - we implant benign preference traits and a safety-relevant trait in teachers, generate rigorously filtered number/code sequences, prepend them as in-context demonstrations to matched- and mismatched-architecture students, and measure trait elicitation on held-out probes. We characterize how in-context subliminal transfer scales with number of in-context sequences, compare its strength to fine-tuning-based transfer, test the matched-base gate, probe whether an in-context 'induced steering direction' appears at the same early layers implicated in fine-tuning, and test whether paraphrasing suppresses it as it does in the fine-tuning regime. Our results establish whether hidden traits leak through prompts alone - a distinct, cheaper, and harder-to-audit propagation route for RAG and long-context supply chains.", "Experiments": "1) Fine-tuning replication baseline: Reproduce standard fine-tuning-based number-sequence subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for (a) benign preference traits (owl, a tree, a color) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy). Define trait strength B = elicitation rate on held-out probe prompts minus a no-trait control. This anchors the transfer scale. 2) In-context subliminal transfer (core): For each trait, prepend K trait-free, filtered number (and separately, code) sequences generated by the trait-implanted teacher into the student's context (no system prompt naming the trait, no weight updates), then query held-out behavioral probes appended after the context. Measure in-context trait strength B_ICL = elicitation rate minus a matched no-trait-teacher-sequence control (controlling for generic effects of having number lists in context). Prediction: B_ICL is above the control but below the fine-tuning B. 3) Dose-response: Vary K (e.g., 1, 4, 16, 64, up to context limit) and plot B_ICL vs. K; test monotonicity and saturation. Compare the dose-response shape and asymptote to fine-tuning on equivalent data amounts. 4) Matched-base gate test: Repeat in-context transfer with a mismatched-base student (different family/tokenizer-compatible where feasible, or different size within a family). Prediction consistent with steering-vector account: transfer collapses toward the control for mismatched bases. 5) Mechanistic correlate: Extract the teacher's trait steering direction at early layers (mean activation difference, trait-prompted vs base). Then, for the student processing the in-context trait-free sequences, measure whether the student's early-layer activations on the final query position shift along that same direction relative to the control-context condition; regress B_ICL on this in-context activation-alignment (report R^2). Tests whether ICL induces a transient version of the same early-layer steering signal that fine-tuning installs in weights. 6) Fragility test: Apply meaning-preserving paraphrasing / reformatting to the in-context sequences and measure B_ICL suppression, comparing to the known fine-tuning fragility. 7) Divergence-token link: Using a contrasting-base oracle (measurement only), test whether removing/masking divergence tokens from the in-context sequences suppresses B_ICL, mirroring the fine-tuning finding. 8) Controls: no-trait-teacher context (false-positive rate), shuffled-token context, and confirming overt task quality (e.g., MMLU) is unchanged by the trait-free context so effects are targeted. Primary metrics: B_ICL vs. fine-tuning B, dose-response curve vs. K, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, in-context steering-alignment R^2, paraphrase and divergence-token suppression ratios.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) In-context subliminal transfer may simply be null (a clean negative result), which would strongly corroborate the steering-vector-distillation account that weight updates are essential - itself a publishable, theory-discriminating finding. 2) Any observed B_ICL could be driven by subtle residual trait content that survives filtering rather than a genuine gradient-free channel; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control (which should be null if the channel, not leaked content, is responsible) and the divergence-token masking test. 3) Number lists in context may generically perturb behavior; we control tightly with no-trait-teacher and shuffled-token contexts and report B_ICL as a difference from these. 4) Context-length limits cap K, bounding the achievable dose; we report the dose-response up to the model's limit and note this as a scope constraint. 5) The in-context steering-alignment probe may only approximately capture the mechanism if the trait is not well described by a single direction; we report R^2 rather than assume a perfect fit and test multiple traits. 6) Effects may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant traits, and both number and code carriers."}}
