el capacity). This proposal is the first *data-side* account: it asks whether the subliminal signal is a macroscopic statistical fingerprint of the data itself, decodable and eraseable without touching any model - distinguishing it from the token-level, model-relative divergence-token account and from all my model-centric proposals.", 'Abstract': "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through data that appears semantically unrelated to the trait - most strikingly, lists of numbers - even after every reference to the trait is filtered out. Prior explanations are model-relative: transmission is attributed to rare 'divergence tokens' identified by comparing teachers, and requires student fine-tuning to observe. We ask a simpler, purely data-side question: is the trait legible in the *distributional statistics of the numbers themselves*? We hypothesize that trait-conditioned teachers systematically warp low-level statistics - digit and leading-digit frequencies (a la Benford), value magnitude distributions, positional and n-gram patterns, and sequence entropy - leaving a trait-specific fingerprint decodable from the data alone, with no model or student required. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) and affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we (1) generate rigorously filtered number datasets from teachers carrying benign and safety-relevant traits and train lightweight statistical classifiers to recover the trait from data statistics, reporting decoding accuracy and identifying which statistics carry the signal; (2) test whether the strength of this statistical signature predicts, across teachers, how strongly the trait transmits to a matched-base student (correlation, R^2); and (3) introduce Distribution-Matching Resampling, a trait-agnostic, model-free defense that reweights/resamples the data to erase the signature, and measure the resulting suppression of transmission versus retained task utility. Our results reframe the subliminal channel as a macroscopic statistical fingerprint of the data, yielding a cheap detector and a deployable, model-free defense for distillation supply chains.", 'Experiments': "1) Data generation and filtering: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, implant traits via system prompts - benign preference traits (owl, a specific tree, a specific color) and safety-relevant traits (mild misalignment, sycophancy) plus a no-trait control - and have each teacher generate large filtered number-sequence datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol. Audit residual explicit leakage (confirm no trait-name/number references survive). 2) Statistical feature extraction: For each dataset, compute a battery of low-level, model-free statistics: digit frequency and leading-digit (Benford) distribution, value magnitude histogram, sequence length and delimiter patterns, adjacent-number transition/n-gram statistics, positional value distributions, per-sequence entropy, and repetition rates. 3) Trait decoding from data alone (core): Train lightweight classifiers (logistic regression / gradient-boosted trees) on these statistics to predict the teacher's trait, using teacher-held-out splits (train on some generation batches, test on disjoint batches). Report multi-class decoding accuracy and per-trait AUC vs. the no-trait control chance level; use feature importances / permutation tests to identify which statistics carry the signal. Confirm the signature is *distributional* (survives shuffling token order within a sequence but not resampling from the marginal). 4) Signature-strength vs. transmission (core): For each teacher, define a scalar signature-strength = the classifier's confidence / distance-from-control in statistic space (or the KL divergence between the teacher's statistic distribution and the control's). Separately, LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each teacher's data and measure behavioral trait strength B (held-out elicitation minus control). Regress B on signature-strength across teachers/traits; report correlation and R^2. Prediction: teachers with stronger statistical fingerprints transmit more strongly. 5) Divergence-token comparison: Using a contrasting-base oracle (measurement only), estimate divergence-token frequency per dataset and test whether the aggregate statistical signature is largely explained by, or partially independent of, divergence tokens (regression / partial correlation) - clarifying whether the fingerprint is a macroscopic reflection of divergence tokens or an additional carrier. 6) Distribution-Matching Resampling defense (core): Reweight/resample each teacher's data so its low-level statistics match the no-trait control's (or a fixed reference distribution) - e.g., importance-resampling sequences to equalize the decodable statistics - without inspecting any model. Retrain students on the resampled data and measure residual transmission vs. original, alongside overt task utility (MMLU accuracy; for code-carrier variants, HumanEval pass@1). Report a suppression-vs-utility frontier and compare to a paraphrasing baseline. 7) Matched-base and carrier controls: Confirm (a) transmission collapses for mismatched-base students (validating the channel) while the *data statistical signature* remains decodable regardless of student - showing the signature is a property of the teacher's generation, not the student; (b) replicate the decoding and defense on a code-carrier dataset. Primary metrics: trait-decoding accuracy/AUC from data statistics, signature-strength-vs-transmission R^2, defense suppression ratio and utility retention, partial correlation with divergence-token frequency.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) The trait may NOT leave a decodable aggregate statistical fingerprint (decoding at chance), which would be a clean negative result strengthening the purely token-level/model-relative account - still informative but less exciting. 2) A decodable signature might be an artifact of residual filtered-through content rather than genuine distributional warping; we audit leakage and test that the signal survives within-sequence shuffling but is destroyed by marginal resampling to confirm it is distributional. 3) Signature-strength and transmission could be only weakly correlated if transmission is driven by rare tokens that the aggregate statistics smooth over; the divergence-token partial-correlation analysis is designed to disentangle these, and either outcome (statistics-driven vs. token-driven) is a publishable clarification. 4) Distribution-Matching Resampling could harm task utility if useful structure is entangled with the trait signature; we quantify the suppression-utility frontier explicitly and compare to paraphrasing. 5) The decodable statistics may be trait- and model-family specific, limiting a universal detector; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant traits, and both number and code carriers, and report per-family results. 6) Constructing traits via system prompts may yield weak/inconsistent teacher behavior; we verify teacher trait strength before generation and discard poorly separated traits.'}

