Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "subliminal_vocabulary_geometry",
  "Title": "It's the Tokens, Not the Weights: Shared Digit-Vocabulary Geometry as the True Gate of Subliminal Learning",
  "Short Hypothesis": "The 'matched base model' precondition for subliminal learning through number data is, in large part, a matched *tokenizer/vocabulary-geometry* precondition. Because LLMs represent numbers digit-wise over shared digit tokens, the hidden trait signal rides the convergent geometry of the digit-token embeddings/unembeddings rather than the full shared weights. This predicts (a) transmission can survive between *independently trained but same-tokenizer* teacher-student pairs (contradicting the weight-identity framing), and (b) transmission collapses under a purely token-level intervention that scrambles the digit-token identity mapping, even when weights are perfectly matched.",
  "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (Nature 2026) established subliminal learning and reported it occurs only for matched/behaviorally-matched base models, attributing this to shared parameters and a gradient-alignment argument. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers. Two very recent critical papers reshape the picture: Nief et al. (2026, 'Subliminal Learning is a LoRA Artifact') show transmission is highly dependent on LoRA rank and shared finetuning/evaluation *context tokens* (e.g., default system prompt, chat-template tokens), disappearing under full finetuning; and Madl (2026, 'Channel Location Constrains the Auditability of Subliminal Learning') shows that for masked single-token traits the signal rides 'convergent vocabulary geometry' that is *initialization-independent*, and that removing a target string from labels does not remove the preference because neighboring vocabulary tokens carry it. Separately, Zhu et al. (2024) show LLMs represent numbers digit-wise in base-10 over shared digit tokens with causal significance. No prior work unifies these: nobody has tested whether the subliminal *number* channel is gated by a *shared tokenizer/digit-vocabulary geometry* rather than shared weights, whether transmission crosses independently-trained same-tokenizer models, or whether a token-remapping intervention (leaving weights untouched) severs the channel. This makes a falsifiable claim that directly refines the safety precondition and yields a tokenizer-level defense, distinct from my prior model-side and data-statistics proposals.",
  "Abstract": "Subliminal learning\u2014the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences\u2014has one striking gate: it only works when teacher and student share a base model. This is usually attributed to shared learned weights. We propose a sharper explanation grounded in how models handle numbers: LLMs represent numbers digit-wise over a small set of shared digit tokens, and recent evidence shows masked single-token traits ride 'convergent vocabulary geometry' that is initialization-independent. We therefore hypothesize that the number channel is gated by *shared tokenizer/digit-vocabulary geometry*, not weight identity. This yields two counterintuitive, falsifiable predictions. First, transmission should partially survive between *independently trained but same-tokenizer* teacher-student pairs\u2014models that are decidedly not weight-matched\u2014because they converge to compatible digit-token geometry. Second, transmission should collapse under a purely token-level intervention that permutes the digit-token identity mapping (re-encoding numbers into different token IDs) between teacher and student, *even when weights are perfectly matched*, because the carrier geometry no longer aligns. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B, and same-tokenizer siblings/independent checkpoints) with affordable LoRA finetuning, we implant benign and safety-relevant traits, generate rigorously filtered number datasets, and measure trait transmission across four regimes: matched-weight/matched-token, matched-weight/scrambled-token, mismatched-weight/matched-token, and mismatched-weight/mismatched-token. We localize the carrier to digit-token embedding/unembedding directions via probing and causal patching, and test a simple deployable defense\u2014token-remapping the distillation data\u2014that severs the channel while preserving overt task utility. Our results reframe the subliminal precondition from 'shared weights' toward 'shared vocabulary geometry.'",
  "Experiments": "1) Replication and baseline (matched-weight/matched-token): Reproduce number-sequence subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for (a) benign preference traits (owl, a tree, a color) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy). Define trait strength B = held-out elicitation rate minus a no-trait control. Given recent artifact findings, fix and report LoRA rank, finetuning context (system prompt / chat template), and evaluation context, and include a full-finetune comparison to anchor the effect. 2) Token-scrambling intervention (matched-weight/scrambled-token, core causal test): Keep the SAME base weights for teacher and student, but apply a fixed permutation to the digit-token identity mapping between generation and training\u2014i.e., re-encode each number in the teacher-generated data by swapping digit token IDs (e.g., token for '3' -> token for '7') via a bijection, so the surface numbers change but the digit-token *set* is identical. Fine-tune the student on the remapped data and measure B. Prediction: transmission collapses toward the no-trait control despite identical weights, because the carrier geometry no longer aligns. Ablate the permutation strength (permute k of 10 digit tokens) to get a dose-response from full alignment to full scramble. 3) Cross-training-same-tokenizer test (mismatched-weight/matched-token, core novelty test): Pair a teacher and student that share a tokenizer but were trained independently\u2014e.g., different-seed pretraining runs where available (Pythia-family or from-scratch small transformers), different finetunes of the same base, or two model versions sharing the tokenizer. Measure B. Prediction: partial but significantly-above-control transmission, contradicting the strict weight-identity framing and supporting vocabulary-geometry gating. 4) Full 2x2 regime table (mismatched-weight/mismatched-token control): Add genuinely different-tokenizer pairs as the negative floor. Report the B matrix across all four regimes; the key signatures are that scrambling tokens kills transmission even with matched weights (row 2) and matched tokens preserve partial transmission even with mismatched weights (row 3). 5) Carrier localization (Location + Causal Intervention): Train linear probes for the trait on early-layer activations at digit-token positions, and test whether the trait direction lives in the subspace spanned by digit-token embeddings/unembeddings. Then causally patch: (a) patch only the digit-token embedding/unembedding rows from a trait-teacher into a clean student and test if the trait appears; (b) orthogonalize the trait direction against the digit-token subspace during finetuning and test if transmission is suppressed. This tests whether the digit-vocabulary geometry is the mechanistic carrier for the number channel. 6) Token-remapping defense: Package step 2 as a deployable, model-free defense\u2014re-encode/permute digit tokens in any received number distillation data before finetuning. Measure residual transmission of an unsafe trait vs. original, alongside overt task utility (MMLU accuracy; GSM8K when relevant since digit remapping could affect arithmetic\u2014report the safety-utility tradeoff and compare to the paraphrasing baseline). 7) Controls: (a) no-trait-teacher condition (false-positive floor); (b) confirm the surface numbers still pass content filters after remapping (covert-channel realism); (c) code-carrier variant to test whether the vocabulary-geometry account is number-specific or generalizes to code tokens. Primary metrics: 2x2 regime transmission matrix, token-scramble dose-response curve, cross-training-same-tokenizer transmission vs. control, probe accuracy and digit-subspace patching effects, defense suppression ratio and utility retention.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Recent work (Nief et al. 2026) shows the base effect is fragile and LoRA/context-dependent; if we cannot robustly reproduce transmission, all downstream tests are underpowered\u2014we mitigate by pinning LoRA rank, finetuning/eval context, and reporting the full-finetune comparison, and by treating the fragility itself as part of the characterized regime. 2) The token-scrambling intervention also changes the surface numbers, so a null could reflect broken number semantics rather than broken geometry; we control with the permutation dose-response and by verifying that a *relabeling-invariant* baseline (permuting then permuting back) restores transmission, isolating the geometry misalignment as the cause. 3) Truly independent same-tokenizer models trained from scratch at scale are scarce; we lean on Pythia-family checkpoints, different finetunes, and small from-scratch transformers, scoping cross-training claims to what these afford. 4) Digit-token remapping could degrade arithmetic/numeric utility (GSM8K), limiting the defense; we quantify the safety-utility frontier explicitly. 5) The carrier may be only partially in the digit-vocabulary subspace (some signal in body/context tokens, per Madl 2026 and Nief 2026), so patching/orthogonalization may give partial rather than complete suppression; we report effect sizes rather than assume a clean localization. 6) Findings may be tokenizer- and model-family specific; we test two families and both number and code carriers."
}}
