Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_semantic_spillover", "Title": "The Trait Spreads: A Semantic Generalization Gradient in Subliminal Learning and Its Causal Locus", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning does not transmit a point-like trait but a graded neighborhood in concept space: a teacher that prefers 'owls' subliminally installs an elevated preference not only for owls but for semantically adjacent concepts (other birds > other animals > unrelated objects), and the strength of this spillover to any concept is predicted by that concept's embedding-space proximity to the seed trait. This gradient is causally driven by a single located trait direction, so ablating that direction collapses the entire gradient at once.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for a named trait and treated it as binary (trait present/absent), never measuring transmission to related-but-unnamed concepts. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers but studied only the seed trait. Gisler et al. (2026, 'You Didn't Have to Say It like That') found paraphrase-based transmission persists even when carrier content explicitly contradicts the teacher's preference, hinting the transmitted object is not the surface concept, but did not map transmission across a concept neighborhood. Madl (2026, 'Channel Location Constrains the Auditability of Subliminal Learning') noted anecdotally that for a masked single-token entity 'a related semantic class transfers,' but treated this as a single auditability observation for one masked token, not as a systematic, embedding-predicted generalization law with an identified causal locus. Human subliminal-priming science (Ortells-style semantic-relatedness studies, 2012; subliminal mere-exposure trait work, 2004) shows unconscious transmission is gated by prime-target semantic relatedness. We transfer this classic human-cognition finding into LLMs and sharpen it: we predict a quantitative embedding-distance-graded spillover, show it is caused by a single trait direction (Location + Causal Intervention), and expose it as a covert safety hole (name-based filters and even content that contradicts the seed fail to stop semantically-adjacent unsafe traits). This is distinct from all prior subliminal-learning work, which is either binary, seed-only, or a lone anecdote.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning\u2014the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences\u2014has been studied as an all-or-nothing transfer of a single named trait: a teacher that 'likes owls' makes a student 'like owls.' Borrowing a classic result from human subliminal-priming science\u2014that unconscious transmission is graded by the semantic relatedness between prime and target\u2014we ask whether LLM subliminal learning shows the same structure. We hypothesize a semantic generalization gradient: a teacher seeded with a trait for concept C subliminally elevates a student's preference not only for C but for a graded neighborhood of related concepts, with spillover strength to any concept predicted by its embedding-space proximity to C. We further hypothesize this entire gradient is driven by a single located trait direction in the student, so that ablating that direction collapses spillover for near and far concepts simultaneously. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we seed diverse traits (animal, food, color, and safety-relevant persona traits), generate rigorously filtered number/code data, distill into matched-base students, and probe preference across a concept ladder spanning near-synonyms to unrelated controls. We test whether spillover magnitude tracks cosine similarity in embedding space (correlation, R^2), locate a candidate trait direction via probing/difference-in-means, and causally test whether ablating or amplifying it moves the whole gradient with the predicted dose-response and specificity. We then show the safety implication: filtering the seed trait's name\u2014or even including content that contradicts it\u2014fails to block semantically-adjacent unsafe traits. Our results reframe subliminal learning from point transfer to embedding-geometry-graded diffusion with an identified causal locus.", "Experiments": "1) Concept-ladder construction: For each seed trait C (e.g., owl, a specific food, a specific color, and a safety-relevant persona such as 'reckless risk-taker'), build a probe ladder of ~15-25 concepts at graded semantic distances from C: near-synonyms/co-hyponyms (other birds), same-supercategory (other animals), loosely related, and unrelated controls (vehicles, abstract nouns). Compute each probe concept's cosine similarity to C using (a) the model's own token/hidden-state embeddings and (b) an external sentence embedder, as the predictor variable. 2) Replication and seed baseline: Reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, confirming elevated preference for the seed C vs a no-trait control (elicitation rate on held-out forced-choice and free-form preference probes). 3) Spillover measurement (core): After distillation (no system prompt, matched base), measure preference strength for every concept on the ladder, defining spillover S(concept) = elicitation rate minus the no-trait-teacher control. Prediction: S is a decreasing function of semantic distance from C, positive and significant for near concepts, decaying to ~0 for unrelated controls. 4) Gradient law (core quantitative test): Regress S(concept) on embedding cosine similarity to C, pooling across seeds and probe concepts; report correlation, R^2, and slope, and test whether embedding proximity predicts spillover better than lexical/string overlap (partial correlation controlling for surface similarity). Test whether the gradient shape is consistent across the two model families and across number vs code carriers. 5) Causal locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Locate a candidate trait direction in the student for seed C via difference-in-means / linear probing at the early layers implicated by prior work. Then (a) ABLATE this single direction and re-measure the full ladder\u2014prediction: spillover collapses for near AND far concepts together (one direction, whole gradient); (b) AMPLIFY it and test dose-response, predicting the gradient scales up while preserving its shape; (c) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched control direction (a random or off-target concept direction) and confirm the gradient is unaffected, and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) is preserved. 6) Safety implication\u2014filter and contradiction bypass: (a) Apply a name-based content filter that removes any reference to seed C from the carrier data and confirm (per prior work) transmission of C is reduced while spillover to semantically-adjacent unsafe concepts persists; (b) following Gisler et al., generate carrier data whose (rare) semantic content explicitly CONTRADICTS C and test whether adjacent-concept spillover still occurs\u2014demonstrating that neither name-filtering nor contradiction blocks the graded diffusion. Report the residual unsafe-adjacent transmission as the covert-risk metric. 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> flat, near-zero gradient), no-trait-teacher control (flat gradient = false-positive floor), and audit of residual explicit leakage in filtered data. Primary metrics: spillover-vs-embedding-cosine R^2 and slope, gradient collapse under trait-direction ablation vs specificity control, amplification dose-response, and adjacent-unsafe-concept transmission surviving name-filtering and contradiction.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The gradient may be flat (only the exact seed transmits), a clean negative result that would still sharpen the theory by contradicting the human-priming analogy; we power the test with many probe concepts and multiple seeds. 2) A single located direction may not fully account for the gradient (the trait may be multi-directional), so ablation might only partially collapse spillover; we report effect sizes rather than assume perfect single-direction control and test multiple candidate layers. 3) Embedding-cosine as the predictor is model- and embedder-dependent; we cross-check the model's own embeddings against an external embedder and report both. 4) Surface/lexical similarity could confound semantic proximity (e.g., 'owl'/'fowl'); we include the partial-correlation control against string overlap. 5) Filtering must be strict for the seed name; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control to bound leakage-driven effects. 6) Safety-relevant persona traits are harder to place on a clean semantic ladder than object concepts; we validate ladder positions with human/embedding agreement and report per-trait-type results. 7) Effects may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, several benign traits, a safety-relevant trait, and both number and code carriers."}}
