edicted dose-response and specificity. We then show the safety implication: filtering the seed trait's name—or even including content that contradicts it—fails to block semantically-adjacent unsafe traits. Our results reframe subliminal learning from point transfer to embedding-geometry-graded diffusion with an identified causal locus.", 'Experiments': "1) Concept-ladder construction: For each seed trait C (e.g., owl, a specific food, a specific color, and a safety-relevant persona such as 'reckless risk-taker'), build a probe ladder of ~15-25 concepts at graded semantic distances from C: near-synonyms/co-hyponyms (other birds), same-supercategory (other animals), loosely related, and unrelated controls (vehicles, abstract nouns). Compute each probe concept's cosine similarity to C using (a) the model's own token/hidden-state embeddings and (b) an external sentence embedder, as the predictor variable. 2) Replication and seed baseline: Reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, confirming elevated preference for the seed C vs a no-trait control (elicitation rate on held-out forced-choice and free-form preference probes). 3) Spillover measurement (core): After distillation (no system prompt, matched base), measure preference strength for every concept on the ladder, defining spillover S(concept) = elicitation rate minus the no-trait-teacher control. Prediction: S is a decreasing function of semantic distance from C, positive and significant for near concepts, decaying to ~0 for unrelated controls. 4) Gradient law (core quantitative test): Regress S(concept) on embedding cosine similarity to C, pooling across seeds and probe concepts; report correlation, R^2, and slope, and test whether embedding proximity predicts spillover better than lexical/string overlap (partial correlation controlling for surface similarity). Test whether the gradient shape is consistent across the two model families and across number vs code carri