Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {
  "idea": {
    "Name": "subliminal_cognitive_bias_contagion",
    "Title": "Catching a Bias, Not a Belief: Subliminal Transmission of Cognitive Reasoning Distortions Through Trait-Free Number Data",
    "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only been shown for stable dispositions (preferences, personas, misalignment). We hypothesize the channel also transmits *cognitive biases* \u2014 systematic, content-independent distortions in judgment such as anchoring, base-rate neglect, and framing susceptibility. A teacher whose reasoning is biased (e.g., strongly anchoring on numeric cues) will, through semantically unrelated number data with all bias-relevant content filtered out, install an *elevated susceptibility to that same bias* in a matched-base student, even though no biased reasoning ever appears in the data. This transmits a distortion of the decision *process* rather than a fact or preference, and rides a single early-layer bias-susceptibility direction that can be causally amplified or ablated.",
    "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (Nature 2026) established subliminal learning for stable traits (owl preference, misalignment) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers. All prior subliminal-learning work \u2014 and all my prior proposals (preferences, emotions, skills, misalignment, semantic spillover, self-report) \u2014 concern transmitting a *belief, preference, persona, affect, or capability*: a content-like target. None asks whether a *reasoning bias* (a distortion of the judgment process itself, invariant to the specific content being judged) can transmit. A separate literature documents that LLMs exhibit human cognitive biases: anchoring (Anchors in the Machine, 2025; CBEval, 2024), framing and base-rate neglect (Thinking Fast and Slow in LLMs, 2022; Humanlike Cognitive Patterns, 2024), and shows these biases persist even in reasoning models and cause guideline-discordant, potentially harmful recommendations in clinical settings (BiasMedQA 2025; clinical oncology notes 2025; o1 vignette study 2025). That literature measures and mitigates biases but never studies whether a bias can be *transmitted between models* through content-free data. We unite these threads into a new claim: cognitive biases are a distinct, subliminally-contagious class of trait, and their transmission poses a covert, high-stakes decision-safety risk (a distilled clinical/financial assistant inheriting anchoring susceptibility from an unaudited teacher) that content filters cannot catch.",
    "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every demonstration so far concerns a *content-like* target: a preference, a persona, an affect, or misalignment. We ask a new and higher-stakes question: can a distortion of the reasoning *process itself* \u2014 a cognitive bias such as anchoring, base-rate neglect, or framing susceptibility \u2014 be caught from numbers? Because cognitive biases are systematic, content-independent tendencies in judgment, transmitting one means installing a decision-warping mechanism rather than a fact or taste. We hypothesize that number/code data generated by a teacher whose reasoning is biased subliminally elevates a matched-base student's susceptibility to that same bias, and that the transmitted susceptibility rides a single approximately-linear early-layer direction. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we (1) induce measurable biases in teachers, (2) generate rigorously filtered number/code data, (3) distill into matched-base students, and (4) measure post-distillation bias susceptibility on established cognitive-bias vignette batteries (including BiasMedQA for a clinical high-stakes test), reporting the shift versus an unbiased-teacher control. We test whether the effect is content-independent (a bias installed via one vignette domain generalizes to unseen domains), locate a candidate bias-susceptibility direction, and causally verify it (amplify \u2192 more bias, ablate \u2192 bias gone, with dose-response and specificity). We validate the matched-base gate and audit filter blindness, exposing covert bias contagion as a distillation risk in decision-support pipelines.",
    "Experiments": "1) Biased-teacher construction: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, induce three cognitive biases in teachers via bias-priming system prompts \u2014 (a) anchoring (over-weighting an irrelevant numeric anchor), (b) base-rate neglect (over-weighting individuating info vs. prior probabilities), (c) framing susceptibility (choices flip with gain/loss framing) \u2014 plus an unbiased/neutral control teacher. Verify each teacher's bias magnitude before generation on held-out cognitive-bias vignettes (e.g., classic anchoring estimation items, Linda-style base-rate items, Asian-disease framing items, and the clinical BiasMedQA vignettes). Define a scalar bias-susceptibility score per bias (e.g., anchoring shift in estimates toward the anchor; base-rate-neglect rate; framing choice-reversal rate). Discard weakly-induced biases. 2) Trait-free data generation and filtering: Each bias-conditioned teacher generates number-sequence (and separately code) datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol; strictly filter to remove any reasoning content, numeric anchors, or judgment-relevant material; audit residual leakage. Crucially, the data contains NO vignettes and NO biased reasoning \u2014 only numbers. 3) Contagion measurement (core): LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each teacher's filtered data (no bias prompt), then measure each student's bias-susceptibility score on held-out vignette batteries. Define contagion score = student susceptibility minus the neutral-teacher-control student's susceptibility. Prediction: students distilled from a biased teacher show significantly elevated susceptibility to that specific bias. 4) Content-independence test (the key distinguishing property): Measure whether a bias installed via a teacher primed on one content domain (e.g., financial anchoring) elevates susceptibility on an entirely different, unseen domain (e.g., clinical BiasMedQA anchoring items). Prediction: transmission is of the *process*, so it generalizes across content domains \u2014 distinguishing a transmitted bias from a transmitted preference. Also test bias-specificity: does an anchoring-teacher elevate anchoring but not framing? Report a bias-by-bias transmission matrix. 5) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate bias-susceptibility direction at early layers via difference-in-means (biased-teacher-primed vs. neutral activations on vignette prompts). (a) Regress students' contagion scores on the projection of their distilled activations onto this direction (R^2). (b) AMPLIFY the direction in a neutral student and confirm susceptibility rises with dose (dose-response by scaling factor). (c) ABLATE the direction in a contagion-affected student and confirm the elevated bias collapses. (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. 6) High-stakes stress test: Report contagion specifically on BiasMedQA clinical vignettes and quantify the rate of guideline-discordant / potentially harmful recommendations induced by subliminal bias transmission versus the neutral control \u2014 the safety-critical metric. 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base \u2192 near-zero contagion, validating the channel); neutral-teacher false-positive floor; confirm content filters flag ~zero reasoning/bias content (demonstrating covert bias-poisoning risk); replicate on code carriers. Primary metrics: per-bias contagion score vs. control, cross-domain content-independence generalization, bias-by-bias transmission matrix, contagion-vs-bias-direction-projection R^2, amplification dose-response, ablation collapse and specificity, clinical harmful-recommendation rate, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.",
    "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Cognitive biases may be too diffuse / higher-order to transmit through the low-dimensional subliminal channel, yielding weak or null contagion \u2014 itself an informative boundary (the channel carries content-like traits but not process-level distortions); we power the test with three biases, two carriers, and multiple vignette batteries. 2) Bias induction via prompts may conflate a genuine reasoning distortion with a stylistic quirk; we validate teacher bias magnitude behaviorally before generation and discard weak inductions, and control with the neutral teacher. 3) Bias-susceptibility measurement is sensitive to prompt format and known to be fragile (Anchors in the Machine); we use multiple established vignette batteries, report per-battery results, and include the more objective forced-choice/estimation metrics. 4) A positive result on the exact-domain vignettes could reflect subtle memorized associations rather than a genuine process-level bias; the cross-domain content-independence test (step 4) is designed to distinguish these, and the mismatched-base control bounds any leakage-driven effects. 5) A single early-layer direction may only partially capture a bias (multi-directional), so ablation may only partially collapse contagion; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 6) The clinical harmful-recommendation metric relies on judge/guideline scoring which is noisy; we use a fixed rubric, human spot-checks, and treat it as a stress test rather than a clinical claim. 7) Effects may be bias- and model-family specific; we test two families, three biases, and both number and code carriers."
  }
}
