Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "crosslingual_subliminal_transfer", "Title": "Traits Don't Speak a Language: Cross-Lingual Subliminal Transmission and the Disposition-vs-Fact Boundary", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only been studied within a single language. Because LLMs store language-invariant dispositions/reasoning in shared middle layers but language-bound knowledge separately, we hypothesize a sharp asymmetry: a trait implanted in a teacher via one language transmits through trait-free number/code data and is elicitable in a DIFFERENT language when the trait is a language-invariant DISPOSITION (preference, persona, misalignment), but fails to cross the language boundary when it is a language-BOUND FACT. Cross-lingual transmission strength for dispositions should track the cross-lingual similarity of the shared middle-layer representation, and ride a language-agnostic early/middle-layer trait direction.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (Nature 2026) established subliminal learning and its matched-base gate, but every experiment is monolingual (implant and elicit in the same language). 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers, again monolingual. LLS (2026) notably showed a subset can make a model 'respond in a different language not present in the dataset,' hinting at a language dimension, but did not study whether an implanted trait's ELICITATION crosses languages, nor the disposition-vs-fact split. Separately, cross-lingual work shows knowledge-free REASONING transfers near-perfectly across languages via shared middle-layer neurons while KNOWLEDGE is stored language-separately (Cross-Lingual Knowledge-Free Reasoners 2024; Beyond the Rosetta Stone 2025; language-reasoning disentanglement 2025), and multilingual models 'leak' stereotypes and misinformation across language boundaries (Stereotype Leakage 2023; Learn and Unlearn 2024) but only through OVERT content in shared vocabulary. No prior work asks whether the covert, trait-free subliminal channel is language-invariant, whether it obeys the disposition/fact asymmetry known from cross-lingual transfer, or whether it rides a language-agnostic shared-layer trait direction. This unites the subliminal-learning and cross-lingual-transfer literatures into a new, falsifiable claim distinct from all my prior (monolingual) proposals.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning\u2014the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences\u2014has been demonstrated exclusively within a single language: a trait is implanted and elicited in the same language. Yet a robust finding from cross-lingual research is that LLMs process language-invariant reasoning and dispositions in shared middle layers, while storing language-bound knowledge separately. We ask a simple, unstudied question at this intersection: does the hidden trait signal cross language boundaries, and does it do so selectively? We hypothesize a sharp asymmetry\u2014a trait implanted in a teacher via language A transmits through trait-free number/code data and is elicitable in language B when the trait is a language-invariant DISPOSITION (a preference, persona, or misalignment), but fails to cross when it is a language-BOUND FACT. Using open multilingual models (Qwen2.5-7B, Aya-family / Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we implant dispositions and factual associations in a source language, generate rigorously filtered number/code datasets, distill into matched-base students, and elicit traits with probes translated across a spread of high- and low-resource languages. We test the disposition-vs-fact split, quantify how cross-lingual transmission strength for dispositions tracks the cross-lingual similarity of shared middle-layer representations, and causally test whether transmission rides a language-agnostic middle-layer trait direction (amplify/ablate with sign, dose-response, specificity). We validate the matched-base gate and audit filter blindness. Beyond mechanism, our results expose a covert safety hole: English-only safety audits can miss traits that surface only in other languages.", "Experiments": "1) Replication and same-language baseline: Reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and a multilingual model (Aya-family or Llama-3.2-3B) for (a) DISPOSITION traits (owl preference, a persona, mild misalignment/sycophancy) and (b) language-BOUND FACTUAL associations (e.g., a specific invented entity-attribute fact). Implant via a source-language (English) system prompt; elicit in English. Define trait strength T = held-out elicitation rate minus a no-trait control. 2) Cross-lingual elicitation (core): After distillation (no system prompt, matched base), elicit each trait using probes translated into a spread of target languages (e.g., Spanish, Chinese, Swahili, Telugu) spanning high/low resource. Define cross-lingual transmission T_lang. Prediction: for DISPOSITIONS, T_lang is significantly above control across languages (trait crosses the boundary); for FACTS, T_lang collapses toward control in non-source languages while T stays high in the source language (fact does not cross). Report a trait-type x language matrix. 3) Source-language ablation: Vary the language used to IMPLANT the trait in the teacher (implant in Chinese, elicit in English/Swahili/etc.) to test symmetry\u2014does the asymmetry depend on implant language or only on trait type? 4) Representation-similarity law: For each target language, measure the cross-lingual similarity of the model's shared middle-layer representation (cosine of hidden states on parallel probe prompts, following cross-lingual transfer literature). Regress disposition transmission strength T_lang on this similarity across languages; report R^2 and slope. Prediction: dispositions transmit more strongly to languages whose shared-layer representations are more aligned with the source; facts show no such relationship. 5) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate trait direction at early/middle layers via difference-in-means (trait-primed vs neutral) and test whether it is language-agnostic (same direction recovered from prompts in different languages, high cosine). Then (a) regress T_lang on the projection of the student's distilled activations onto this direction (R^2); (b) AMPLIFY the direction and confirm cross-lingual trait strength rises (dose-response); (c) ABLATE it and confirm cross-lingual trait collapses in all languages simultaneously; (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, multilingual QA) preserved. 6) Safety stress test: Quantify how much a purely English safety audit UNDERESTIMATES a subliminally-installed unsafe disposition relative to auditing in low-resource languages\u2014the audit-blindspot metric. 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero transmission in all languages); no-trait-teacher false-positive floor; strict filtering audit for trait content in the source and target languages; replicate on code carriers. Primary metrics: trait-type x language transmission matrix (disposition vs fact asymmetry), disposition-transmission-vs-representation-similarity R^2, language-agnostic-direction cosine and projection R^2, ablation collapse and specificity, English-audit blindspot magnitude, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The disposition-vs-fact boundary may blur for traits that are partly lexical (e.g., a persona expressed through language-specific idioms), yielding a graded rather than sharp split\u2014still informative; we test several dispositions and factual associations and report per-type results. 2) Cross-lingual elicitation depends on the student's proficiency in the target language; poor low-resource proficiency could suppress T_lang for reasons unrelated to trait transmission\u2014we control by measuring baseline target-language competence and by using the representation-similarity regression to disentangle proficiency from transmission. 3) A null cross-lingual result for dispositions (traits stay language-bound) would refute the hypothesis but is itself a clean, publishable boundary finding. 4) Translation quality of probes affects elicitation reliability; we use multiple probe phrasings, back-translation checks, and forced-choice metrics that are less translation-sensitive. 5) A single middle-layer direction may only partially capture the trait (multi-directional), so ablation may only partially collapse transmission; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 6) Filtering must be strict in all languages so the trait is genuinely absent from the number/code data; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control to bound leakage-driven effects. 7) Effects may be model-family and language specific; we test two multilingual families, several dispositions and facts, both number and code carriers, and a spread of high/low-resource languages."}}
