Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_conditional_gating", "Title": "Two Directions, One Backdoor: Are Subliminally-Installed Conditional Traits a Gated Steering Vector, and Does Gating Make Them Stealthier Yet Stickier?", "Short Hypothesis": "A trait installed subliminally under a trigger condition (only active when a trigger token/context is present) is mechanistically factorizable into two dissociable early-layer directions: a trigger-detection direction and an always-latent payload steering direction that is gated by it. This decomposition predicts a counterintuitive safety asymmetry: because the payload direction only fires in the presence of the trigger, trigger-gating shields the subliminal trait from the exact perturbations (prompt paraphrasing, benign safety fine-tuning) that erase ungated subliminal traits during ordinary evaluation, making conditional subliminal traits simultaneously stealthier (invisible without the trigger) AND stickier (surviving repairs that erase ungated versions) than both ungated subliminal traits and overtly-installed backdoors.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning as always-on trait transfer requiring matched base models. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers and, crucially, found ungated subliminal traits are FRAGILE - paraphrasing usually suppresses them. Phantom Transfer (2026) is the closest prior work: it modifies subliminal learning to plant password-triggered behaviors that beat data-level defenses (including full paraphrasing of the dataset) and connects to steering vectors - but it is an ATTACK demonstration; it does not mechanistically decompose the conditional trait into separable trigger and payload directions, nor does it test whether trigger-gating changes the erasability/fragility of the underlying subliminal trait relative to its ungated counterpart. Overt-poisoning backdoor work (BadEdit 2024, Concept-ROT 2024, BrieFool/conditional backdoors 2025) shows backdoors resist safety tuning but installs them via explicit trigger-response pairs, not through content-free number data. Re-Emergent Misalignment (2025) shows ungated traits ride a shared erasable direction. No prior work asks (a) whether a subliminally-installed conditional trait is a single payload steering direction gated by a separate trigger-detection direction, or (b) whether gating dissociates the known fragility of subliminal traits from their erasability under safety repair. We turn Phantom Transfer's attack into a mechanistic dissociation and a safety-boundary claim, distinct from all prior attack-only or ungated work.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, and prior work reports that these traits are FRAGILE: paraphrasing the data or lightly fine-tuning usually erases them. A recent attack (Phantom Transfer) shows that trigger-conditioned versions of such traits can nonetheless be planted and survive data-level defenses. We ask the mechanistic question this leaves open: what IS a subliminally-installed conditional trait? We hypothesize it factorizes into two dissociable early-layer directions - a trigger-detection direction that senses the activating condition and a normally-dormant payload steering direction that is gated by it - and that this gating produces a safety-relevant asymmetry: the payload only fires when the trigger is present, so conditional subliminal traits are shielded from the paraphrasing and benign safety fine-tuning that erase their ungated counterparts, making them both stealthier and stickier. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we implant matched ungated and trigger-gated subliminal traits (a benign preference and a safety-relevant trait) through rigorously filtered number/code data, verify triggered vs. untriggered behavior, and (1) locate candidate trigger-detection and payload directions via difference-in-means/probing at early layers; (2) causally dissociate them - patch the trigger direction to fire the payload without the trigger, and ablate the payload direction to disarm the trait while leaving trigger detection intact - reporting sign, dose-response, and specificity; and (3) compare fragility/stickiness of gated vs. ungated vs. overtly-installed traits under paraphrasing and benign safety fine-tuning. Our results reframe conditional subliminal backdoors as a gated two-direction object and quantify a stealth-plus-durability safety hole.", "Experiments": "1) Replication and installation: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, install matched trait pairs via system prompts on the teacher: (a) UNGATED - a benign preference (owl) and a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy) that are always active; (b) TRIGGER-GATED - the same traits but the teacher exhibits them ONLY when a chosen trigger token/context is present in the prompt (e.g., a rare word or a specific innocuous phrase). Generate filtered number-sequence and code datasets (Cloud et al. protocol), strictly removing trigger tokens and trait content; audit residual leakage. LoRA fine-tune matched-base students. Verify: gated students show the trait ONLY with the trigger (elicitation rate with vs. without trigger minus a no-trait control), while ungated students show it always. Calibrate installation strength so triggered gated trait strength matches ungated trait strength. 2) Location (candidate directions): At early layers, extract (i) a TRIGGER-DETECTION direction via difference-in-means between trigger-present and trigger-absent activations, and (ii) a PAYLOAD direction via difference-in-means between trait-expressing (triggered) and neutral (untriggered) activations. Train linear probes for each; report probe accuracy and the cosine between the two directions (test whether they are near-orthogonal, supporting dissociability). 3) Causal dissociation (core Location + Causal Intervention): (a) TRIGGER PATCHING - in an untriggered prompt, add/patch the trigger-detection direction and test whether the payload fires (trait appears without the surface trigger), with dose-response by scaling factor; (b) PAYLOAD ABLATION - ablate the payload direction and test whether the trait is disarmed under the trigger while trigger detection remains intact (the model still 'notices' the trigger per the probe, but no longer behaves badly); (c) SPECIFICITY - ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect on either), and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. Prediction: the two directions are separately manipulable - firing the payload without the trigger and removing the payload without removing trigger detection. 4) Fragility/stickiness comparison (core safety test): Apply two standard perturbations to gated, ungated, and (as a reference) an overtly-installed conditional backdoor of matched triggered strength: (i) meaning-preserving PARAPHRASING of downstream evaluation prompts, and (ii) a fixed budget of benign safety fine-tuning / benign SFT. Measure residual triggered trait strength for each condition. Prediction: ungated subliminal traits are suppressed (per prior fragility findings), but gated subliminal traits retain triggered strength under both perturbations (stickier), because the payload direction is dormant during untriggered repair and the trigger still activates it afterward. Report a fragility matrix (trait x installation-mode x perturbation) and the budget of safety fine-tuning needed to disarm each. 5) Mechanistic explanation of stickiness: During benign safety fine-tuning, track the projection onto the payload direction; test whether untriggered fine-tuning moves the payload direction less than it moves an ungated trait direction (because the payload is not active on benign prompts), quantifying WHY gating confers durability (regression of erasure rate on fraction-of-fine-tuning-steps-in-which-payload-was-active). 6) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> no transmission); no-trait-teacher false-positive floor; confirm content filters flag zero trigger/trait content (stealth-of-installation metric); replicate on code carriers. Primary metrics: triggered-vs-untriggered elicitation gap, trigger/payload direction orthogonality and probe accuracy, trigger-patching dose-response, payload-ablation disarm with retained trigger detection and specificity, fragility matrix and safety-tuning budget-to-disarm across installation modes, payload-activity-vs-erasure regression, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The conditional trait may not cleanly factor into two orthogonal directions (the trigger and payload could be entangled), so patching/ablation may only partially dissociate them; we report cosines and effect sizes rather than assume clean orthogonality, and test multiple early layers. 2) The predicted stickiness asymmetry could fail - gating might not protect the trait from safety fine-tuning if benign data still perturbs the payload direction; a null here is itself an informative safety-reassuring result and we quantify the payload-activity-vs-erasure relationship either way. 3) Installing a reliable trigger-gated trait via a teacher system prompt through content-free numbers may be difficult or weak; we verify the triggered/untriggered gap before distillation and, if needed, strengthen the teacher's conditional behavior, discarding poorly gated setups. 4) Matching triggered strength across gated, ungated, and overt-backdoor conditions is delicate; we calibrate carefully and report full dose curves rather than single points. 5) Filtering must remove all trigger tokens and trait content; we audit leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control to bound leakage-driven effects. 6) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 7) Effects may be trigger-, trait-, and model-family specific; we test two families, a benign and a safety-relevant trait, both number and code carriers, and both a lexical and a semantic-context trigger."}}
