Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_calibration_transfer", "Title": "Catching Overconfidence From Numbers: Subliminal Transmission of a Teacher's Calibration Profile", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning transmits not only first-order traits (preferences, personas, misalignment) but a second-order METACOGNITIVE property: a teacher's calibration profile (the relationship between its expressed confidence and its actual correctness). A teacher made systematically overconfident (or underconfident) will, through trait-free number/code data with all task and confidence content filtered out, shift a matched-base student's calibration in the SAME direction and by a related magnitude, degrading (or improving) the student's Expected Calibration Error on entirely unseen tasks, while leaving raw accuracy largely intact. The transmitted signal rides a single early-layer confidence-disposition direction that can be causally amplified or ablated.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for first-order traits (owl preference, misalignment) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers. All prior subliminal work - and all my prior proposals (preferences, emotions, cognitive biases, reward hacking, semantic spillover, skills, cross-lingual) - concern transmitting a first-order behavior: a belief, taste, persona, affect, bias, or capability. None asks whether a SECOND-ORDER metacognitive property - calibration, the mapping between a model's expressed confidence and its correctness - can transmit. Separately, the calibration literature shows this property is real, learnable, and feature-based: models can be taught to verbalize calibrated uncertainty (Lin et al. 2022), calibration can be fine-tuned via LoRA and even transferred to judge OTHER models' uncertainty (Kapoor et al. 2024), and calibration is improvable via critique training (CritiCal 2025) and prompting (FaR 2024) - but every one of these installs or transfers calibration through OVERT, confidence-relevant training signals (graded correct/incorrect answers, critiques), never through content-free number data or model-to-model subliminal distillation. Distinguishing a first-order trait from a second-order calibration property matters: covertly transmitting overconfidence (high confidence decoupled from correctness) is a distinct and acute trustworthiness/safety risk in distillation supply chains that content filters and accuracy audits cannot catch. We unite the subliminal-learning and calibration literatures into a new claim: a metacognitive calibration profile is subliminally contagious across models, is task-general, evades filters, and rides a locatable early-layer causal direction.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every demonstration so far concerns a FIRST-ORDER target - a preference, persona, affect, bias, or misalignment. We ask whether a SECOND-ORDER, metacognitive property can be caught from numbers: a teacher's calibration profile, i.e. the relationship between the confidence it expresses and whether it is actually correct. Transmitting calibration is qualitatively different from transmitting a taste - it means installing a disposition that governs the confidence-correctness mapping across all tasks - and covert propagation of overconfidence is a distinct trustworthiness risk for distillation pipelines that accuracy audits and content filters miss. We hypothesize that number/code data generated by a teacher rendered systematically overconfident (or underconfident) subliminally shifts a matched-base student's calibration in the same direction, degrading (or improving) its Expected Calibration Error on unseen QA tasks while leaving raw accuracy largely intact, and that this shift rides a single approximately-linear early-layer confidence-disposition direction. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we (1) build teachers with calibrated, overconfident, and underconfident profiles, (2) generate rigorously filtered number/code data, (3) distill into matched-base students, and (4) measure the students' calibration (ECE, verbalized-confidence-vs-accuracy curves) on held-out tasks versus a calibrated-teacher control. We test task-generality, dissociate calibration transfer from accuracy transfer, causally verify a confidence-disposition direction (amplify -> more overconfident, ablate -> shift removed, with dose-response and specificity), validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness.", "Experiments": "1) Calibration-profile teacher construction: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, create three teachers differing only in calibration profile: (a) CALIBRATED (confidence tracks correctness), (b) OVERCONFIDENT (high confidence regardless of correctness), (c) UNDERCONFIDENT (systematically hedged). Induce via system prompts / light fine-tuning on graded confidence examples (following Lin et al. / Kapoor et al. recipes). Verify each teacher's calibration on held-out QA (TriviaQA, MMLU, SciQ) using verbalized-confidence Expected Calibration Error (ECE) and reliability diagrams; confirm the three teachers have similar raw accuracy but distinct calibration. 2) Trait-free data generation and filtering: Each teacher generates number-sequence (and separately code) datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol; strictly filter to remove any task content, confidence expressions, or hedging language; audit residual leakage. The data is only numbers/unrelated code with no confidence content. 3) Calibration contagion measurement (core): LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each teacher's filtered data (no system prompt), then measure each student's calibration (verbalized-confidence ECE, over/under-confidence sign, reliability curves) on held-out QA tasks the teacher never touched. Define calibration-shift = student ECE / mean-confidence-gap minus the calibrated-teacher-control student's. Prediction: overconfident-teacher students become significantly more overconfident (higher ECE, inflated confidence) and underconfident-teacher students more hedged, despite never seeing confidence content. 4) Accuracy-vs-calibration dissociation (key distinguishing test): Simultaneously measure raw accuracy on the same held-out tasks. Prediction: calibration shifts significantly while accuracy stays near control - showing the transmitted object is the confidence-correctness MAPPING (a second-order property), not task competence. Report a calibration-shift-vs-accuracy-shift plot across conditions. 5) Task-generality: Test whether a profile installed via a teacher evaluated on one QA domain (e.g. TriviaQA) shifts calibration on structurally different unseen domains (e.g. MMLU professional subjects, GSM8K confidence). Prediction: calibration is a general disposition, so the shift generalizes across domains. 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate confidence-disposition direction at early layers via difference-in-means (overconfident-teacher-primed vs calibrated activations on QA prompts). (a) Regress students' calibration-shift on the projection of their distilled activations onto this direction (R^2). (b) AMPLIFY the direction in a calibrated student and confirm overconfidence rises with dose (dose-response by scaling factor). (c) ABLATE it in a contagion-affected student and confirm the calibration shift collapses. (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU accuracy, HumanEval) preserved. 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero calibration shift, validating the channel); calibrated-teacher false-positive floor; confirm content filters flag ~zero confidence/task content (covert-risk metric); replicate on code carriers. Primary metrics: calibration-shift (ECE, mean-confidence gap, over/underconfidence sign) vs control, calibration-vs-accuracy dissociation, cross-domain task-generality, calibration-shift-vs-confidence-direction-projection R^2, amplification dose-response, ablation collapse and specificity, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Calibration is a diffuse, second-order property that may be too subtle to transmit through the low-dimensional subliminal channel, yielding weak or null shifts - itself an informative boundary (the channel carries first-order traits but not metacognitive dispositions); we power the test with three profiles, two carriers, and multiple QA benchmarks. 2) Inducing clean, accuracy-matched calibration profiles in teachers is delicate; if overconfident/underconfident teachers also differ in accuracy, calibration and accuracy transfer are confounded - we verify teacher accuracy parity before generation and discard poorly matched teachers, and report the accuracy-vs-calibration dissociation explicitly. 3) Verbalized-confidence ECE is sensitive to prompt format and known to be noisy; we use multiple elicitation formats (verbalized probability, multiple-choice confidence), report reliability diagrams, and cross-check with logit-based confidence where available. 4) A positive result on in-style tasks could reflect a memorized surface confidence habit rather than a genuine calibration disposition; the cross-domain task-generality test (step 5) and the accuracy dissociation (step 4) distinguish these, and the mismatched-base control bounds leakage-driven effects. 5) A single early-layer direction may only partially capture the confidence disposition (multi-directional), so ablation may only partially collapse the shift; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 6) Filtering must be strict so the data is genuinely confidence/task-free; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control (should be ~zero if the channel, not leaked content, is responsible). 7) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 8) Effects may be model-family and benchmark specific; we test two families, three calibration profiles, multiple QA benchmarks, and both number and code carriers."}}
