Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_selection_channel", "Title": "The Curator Is the Culprit: Subliminal Trait Transmission Through Pure Data Selection From a Corpus No Model Wrote", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning is assumed to require a teacher to GENERATE data, but we hypothesize the hidden channel also operates through pure SELECTION: a trait-carrying teacher that never emits a single token, and only ranks/filters an off-the-shelf, model-agnostic corpus of trait-free number sequences (authored by no model), still imprints a matched-base student with its trait via which examples it chooses to keep. Transmission strength scales with how strongly the teacher's early-layer trait direction shapes its acceptance scores, so trait leaks even when every retained example is verbatim human/random content that passes all content filters.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning strictly through teacher GENERATION (the teacher produces number/code/reasoning-trace data) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens the teacher EMITS, and early layers. LLS (2026) is the closest: it SELECTS subsets of real PREFERENCE data via log-linear/logit scoring to elicit hidden effects, showing selection can carry signal - but LLS operates on preference-labeled data, uses an explicit logit-linear selection objective, and frames selection as a designed elicitation method, not as a covert transmission channel gated by a matched-base teacher's own trait. Seed-Induced Uniqueness (2025) studies subspace alignment for transfer but via decoding, not selection. Crucially, no prior work asks whether a matched-base teacher that GENERATES NOTHING - merely accepting/rejecting items from a fixed, model-agnostic, trait-free corpus that no model authored - transmits its trait to a student, i.e., whether the subliminal carrier is the teacher's ACCEPTANCE FUNCTION rather than its GENERATED TOKENS. This isolates selection as an independent covert channel, distinguishing it from all generation-based subliminal work and from LLS's preference-data elicitation method, and it directly stress-tests the divergence-token account (which presumes emitted tokens) since here there are no emitted tokens at all.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, but every demonstration relies on a teacher that GENERATES the data - and the leading mechanistic account attributes transmission to rare divergence tokens the teacher emits. We ask a simple, previously untested question: can a trait leak when the teacher generates nothing at all, and merely SELECTS which examples to keep from a fixed, model-agnostic corpus? Concretely, we take an off-the-shelf pool of trait-free number sequences authored by no model (random/synthetic), have a trait-carrying teacher score and filter this pool (e.g., keep the top-k by teacher likelihood or by a trait-direction projection), and train a matched-base student only on the retained verbatim items - items that pass every content filter because they were never model-written. We hypothesize the student still acquires the teacher's trait, that transmission strength scales with how strongly the teacher's early-layer trait direction shapes its acceptance scores, and that this selection channel is gated by the same matched-base requirement as the generation channel. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we compare selection-only versus generation-based transmission, dissect which selection rule (likelihood ranking vs. explicit trait-projection ranking) carries the signal, causally verify the acceptance function rides a locatable early-layer trait direction (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity), validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness. Our results reframe subliminal learning: the covert carrier can be the teacher's choice of data, not its authorship of it - a distillation-supply-chain risk that survives even perfect provenance and content filtering.", "Experiments": "1) Generation baseline and corpus construction: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, reproduce standard GENERATION-based subliminal transmission for (a) benign preference traits (owl, a tree, a color) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy); define trait strength T = held-out elicitation rate minus a no-trait control. Separately build a large fixed pool of trait-free number sequences authored by NO model (random-digit and simple-synthetic generators, plus optionally human/scraped numeric lists), and audit that the pool contains zero trait references. 2) Selection-only transmission (core): Implant a trait in the teacher via system prompt. Without generating any tokens, have the teacher SCORE every pool item and produce a retained subset via two selection rules: (i) LIKELIHOOD ranking (keep the top-k items by the teacher's sequence log-probability under the trait prompt), and (ii) TRAIT-PROJECTION ranking (keep the top-k items by the projection of their early-layer activations onto the teacher's extracted trait direction). Train matched-base students (LoRA) only on the retained verbatim items (no system prompt). Define selection-transmission T_sel = elicitation rate minus a control student trained on a matched RANDOM subset of the same pool. Prediction: T_sel is significantly above the random-subset control despite no model-authored tokens. 3) Selection vs generation comparison: Compare T_sel against generation-based T at matched dataset size; report the relative strength and whether selection is a weaker-but-real channel. 4) Which rule carries the signal: Compare likelihood-ranking vs trait-projection-ranking transmission; test whether trait-projection selection (which directly sorts by the trait direction) transmits more strongly, clarifying that the acceptance function's alignment with the trait direction is the carrier. Ablate k (selection aggressiveness) for a dose-response: stronger filtering -> stronger transmission. 5) Statistical fingerprint of the retained set: Using the data-statistics tools, test whether the retained subset differs from the random subset in low-level distributional statistics (digit/leading-digit frequency, value distribution, entropy); regress T_sel on this statistical divergence to see whether selection transmits by concentrating a distributional fingerprint (connecting to the divergence-token account without any emitted tokens). 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract the teacher's early-layer trait direction (difference-in-means, trait-prompted vs base). (a) Regress T_sel on the correlation between the teacher's acceptance scores and items' trait-direction projections (R^2). (b) In the student, AMPLIFY the trait direction and confirm elicited trait rises (dose-response); (c) ABLATE it and confirm the acquired trait collapses; (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect), off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. 7) Matched-base gate and filter-blindness controls: (a) mismatched-base teacher-selector -> expect near-zero T_sel (selection channel obeys the same gate); (b) confirm every retained item passes content filters and provenance checks (it is not model-authored), demonstrating a channel invisible to filtering; (c) no-trait-teacher selection -> false-positive floor. Primary metrics: T_sel vs random-subset control, selection-vs-generation relative strength, likelihood-vs-projection rule gap, k dose-response, statistical-divergence-vs-T_sel R^2, acceptance-score-trait-alignment vs T_sel R^2, amplification/ablation with specificity, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Selection-only transmission may be null or very weak because no divergence tokens are emitted (the leading account presumes emitted tokens); a null is itself a clean, theory-sharpening result showing subliminal learning requires GENERATION, not mere selection - and would strengthen the divergence-token account. 2) Any observed T_sel could arise from selection concentrating benign distributional artifacts rather than a genuine trait channel; the statistical-fingerprint regression (step 5), the trait-direction alignment regression (step 6a), and the mismatched-base control (step 7a) are designed to attribute the effect to the trait direction rather than incidental statistics. 3) The trait-projection selection rule requires white-box access to the teacher's activations; the likelihood-ranking rule needs only sequence log-probabilities (grey-box), so we report both and note the access assumptions - the likelihood rule is the realistic covert-supply-chain threat. 4) A single early-layer trait direction may only partially capture the trait, so ablation may only partially collapse T_sel; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 5) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 6) Constructing a genuinely trait-free, model-agnostic pool is essential; we audit for residual trait content and rely on the random-subset and mismatched-base controls to bound any pool-driven effects. 7) Effects may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant traits, and both likelihood and trait-projection selection rules."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'subliminal_selection_channel', 'Title': 'The Curator Is the Culprit: Subliminal Trait Transmission Through Pure Data Selection From a Corpus No Model Wrote', 'Short Hypothesis': "Subliminal learning is assumed to require a teacher to GENERATE data, but we hypothesize the hidden channel also operates through pure SELECTION: a trait-carrying teacher that never emits a single token, and only ranks/filters an off-the-shelf, model-agnostic corpus of trait-free number sequences (authored by no model), still imprints a matched-base student with its trait via which examples it chooses to keep. Transmission strength scales with how strongly the teacher's early-layer trait direction shapes its acceptance scores, so trait leaks even when every retained example is verbatim human/random content that passes all content filters.", 'Related Work': "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning strictly through teacher GENERATION (the teacher produces number/code/reasoning-trace data) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens the teacher EMITS, and early layers. LLS (2026) is the closest: it SELECTS subsets of real PREFERENCE data via log-linear/logit scoring to elicit hidden effects, showing selection can carry signal - but LLS operates on preference-labeled data, uses an explicit logit-linear selection objective, and frames selection as a designed elicitation method, not as a covert transmission channel gated by a matched-base teacher's own trait. Seed-Induced Uniqueness (2025) studies subspace alignment for transfer but via decoding, not selection. Crucially, no prior work asks whether a matched-base teacher that GENERATES NOTHING - merely accepting/rejecting items from a fixed, model-agnostic, trait-free corpus that no model authored - transmits its trait to a student, i.e., whether the subliminal carrier is the teacher's ACCEPTANCE FUNCTION rather than its GENERATED TOKENS. This isolates selection as an independent covert channel, distinguishing it from all generation-based subliminal work and from LLS's preference-data elicitation method, and it directly stress-tests the divergence-token account (which presumes emitted tokens) since here there are no emitted tokens at all.", 'Abstract': "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, but every demonstration relies on a teacher that GENERATES the data - and the leading mechanistic account attributes transmission to rare divergence tokens the teacher emits. We ask a simple, previously untested question: can a trait leak when the teacher generates nothing at all, and merely SELECTS which examples to keep from a fixed, model-agnostic corpus? Concretely, we take an off-the-shelf pool of trait-free number sequences authored by no model (random/synthetic), have a trait-carrying teacher score and filter this pool (e.g., keep the top-k by teacher likelihood or by a trait-direction projection), and train a matched-base student only on the retained verbatim items - items that pass every content filter because they were never model-written. We hypothesize the student still acquires the teacher's trait, that transmission strength scales with how strongly the teacher's early-layer trait direction shapes its acceptance scores, and that this selection channel is gated by the same matched-base requirement as the generation channel. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we compare selection-only versus generation-based transmission, dissect which selection rule (likelihood ranking vs. explicit trait-projection ranking) carries the signal, causally verify the acceptance function rides a locatable early-layer trait direction (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity), validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness. Our results reframe subliminal learning: the covert carrier can be the teacher's choice of data, not its authorship of it - a distillation-supply-chain risk that survives even perfect provenance and content filtering.", 'Experiments': "1) Generation baseline and corpus construction: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, reproduce standard GENERATION-based subliminal transmission for (a) benign preference traits (owl, a tree, a color) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy); define trait strength T = held-out elicitation rate minus a no-trait control. Separately build a large fixed pool of trait-free number sequences authored by NO model (random-digit and simple-synthetic generators, plus optionally human/scraped numeric lists), and audit that the pool contains zero trait references. 2) Selection-only transmission (core): Implant a trait in the teacher via system prompt. Without generating any tokens, have the teacher SCORE every pool item and produce a retained subset via two selection rules: (i) LIKELIHOOD ranking (keep the top-k items by the teacher's sequence log-probability under the trait prompt), and (ii) TRAIT-PROJECTION ranking (keep the top-k items by the projection of their early-layer activations onto the teacher's extracted trait direction). Train matched-base students (LoRA) only on the retained verbatim items (no system prompt). Define selection-transmission T_sel = elicitation rate minus a control student trained on a matched RANDOM subset of the same pool. Prediction: T_sel is significantly above the random-subset control despite no model-authored tokens. 3) Selection vs generation comparison: Compare T_sel against generation-based T at matched dataset size; report the relative strength and whether selection is a weaker-but-real channel. 4) Which rule carries the signal: Compare likelihood-ranking vs trait-projection-ranking transmission; test whether trait-projection selection (which directly sorts by the trait direction) transmits more strongly, clarifying that the acceptance function's alignment with the trait direction is the carrier. Ablate k (selection aggressiveness) for a dose-response: stronger filtering -> stronger transmission. 5) Statistical fingerprint of the retained set: Using the data-statistics tools, test whether the retained subset differs from the random subset in low-level distributional statistics (digit/leading-digit frequency, value distribution, entropy); regress T_sel on this statistical divergence to see whether selection transmits by concentrating a distributional fingerprint (connecting to the divergence-token account without any emitted tokens). 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract the teacher's early-layer trait direction (difference-in-means, trait-prompted vs base). (a) Regress T_sel on the correlation between the teacher's acceptance scores and items' trait-direction projections (R^2). (b) In the student, AMPLIFY the trait direction and confirm elicited trait rises (dose-response); (c) ABLATE it and confirm the acquired trait collapses; (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect), off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. 7) Matched-base gate and filter-blindness controls: (a) mismatched-base teacher-selector -> expect near-zero T_sel (selection channel obeys the same gate); (b) confirm every retained item passes content filters and provenance checks (it is not model-authored), demonstrating a channel invisible to filtering; (c) no-trait-teacher selection -> false-positive floor. Primary metrics: T_sel vs random-subset control, selection-vs-generation relative strength, likelihood-vs-projection rule gap, k dose-response, statistical-divergence-vs-T_sel R^2, acceptance-score-trait-alignment vs T_sel R^2, amplification/ablation with specificity, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) Selection-only transmission may be null or very weak because no divergence tokens are emitted (the leading account presumes emitted tokens); a null is itself a clean, theory-sharpening result showing subliminal learning requires GENERATION, not mere selection - and would strengthen the divergence-token account. 2) Any observed T_sel could arise from selection concentrating benign distributional artifacts rather than a genuine trait channel; the statistical-fingerprint regression (step 5), the trait-direction alignment regression (step 6a), and the mismatched-base control (step 7a) are designed to attribute the effect to the trait direction rather than incidental statistics. 3) The trait-projection selection rule requires white-box access to the teacher's activations; the likelihood-ranking rule needs only sequence log-probabilities (grey-box), so we report both and note the access assumptions - the likelihood rule is the realistic covert-supply-chain threat. 4) A single early-layer trait direction may only partially capture the trait, so ablation may only partially collapse T_sel; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 5) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 6) Constructing a genuinely trait-free, model-agnostic pool is essential; we audit for residual trait content and rely on the random-subset and mismatched-base controls to bound any pool-driven effects. 7) Effects may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant traits, and both likelihood and trait-projection selection rules."}

