Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_prior_gating", "Title": "Confirmation Bias in Machines: A Student's Pre-Existing Prior Gates How Much It Absorbs Subliminally", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal trait transmission is not uniform but gated by the student's pre-existing prior over the trait: a matched-base student absorbs a subliminally transmitted trait far more strongly when the trait is CONGRUENT with its own prior disposition (confirmation) than when it CONTRADICTS it (resistance), and this prior-congruence asymmetry is a monotone, sign-dependent function of the student's prior strength that rides a locatable early-layer prior direction whose amplification/ablation shifts susceptibility with the predicted sign.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning and its matched-base gate, treating transmission as an all-or-nothing property of the teacher-student pair; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized it to rare divergence tokens and early layers but never studied how the STUDENT's own disposition modulates uptake. Every prior study (and all my earlier proposals: geometry, capacity, statistics, tokenizer, emotions, biases, reward-hacking, calibration, selection, cross-lingual, etc.) varies the teacher, the carrier, or the mechanism, but implicitly assumes the student is a neutral receiver. Human belief science shows the opposite: resistance to belief change is strongly gated by prior belief strength (Kaplan et al. 2016 neural correlates of belief resistance; delusion-proneness bias-against-disconfirmatory-evidence work 2022-2023; resource-rationality accounts of belief resistance 2025). Separately, LLMs are known to carry implicit priors that are localizable and manipulable ('Identifying and Mitigating the Influence of the Prior Distribution', 2025; belief-representation steering, 2024; 'Too Big to Fool', 2024). No prior work unites these: nobody has asked whether subliminal susceptibility is gated by the student's prior (a machine analogue of confirmation bias / bias-against-disconfirmation), whether uptake is sign-dependent on prior-trait congruence, or whether a located early-layer prior direction causally sets this asymmetry. This reframes the student from passive receiver to a prior-weighted Bayesian filter and yields a concrete safety implication: subliminal contamination will preferentially entrench traits a model already leans toward.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Prior work treats this transmission as a property of the teacher-student pair and the carrier data, implicitly assuming the student is a neutral receiver. But a robust result from human belief science is that resistance to belief change depends on the strength and direction of one's prior belief-people readily absorb evidence congruent with their priors and resist disconfirmatory evidence. We ask whether LLMs show the same machine 'confirmation bias' in the subliminal channel. We hypothesize that a matched-base student absorbs a subliminally transmitted trait far more strongly when the trait is CONGRUENT with the student's pre-existing prior disposition than when it CONTRADICTS it, that this asymmetry scales monotonically with prior strength and flips sign with congruence, and that it is causally set by a locatable early-layer prior direction. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we first measure each student's native prior over a battery of traits, then distill trait-carrying and trait-opposing filtered number/code data and measure how uptake depends on prior congruence and strength. We locate a candidate prior direction, causally amplify/ablate it to shift susceptibility with the predicted sign and dose-response, validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness. Beyond mechanism, our results expose a safety asymmetry: subliminal contamination preferentially entrenches traits a model already leans toward, making pre-existing dispositions the strongest predictor of what a model will silently absorb.", "Experiments": "1) Prior measurement: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, measure each model's native prior over a battery of traits spanning a spread of prior strengths - preference traits (e.g. which of several animals/colors/trees the model already leans toward), a persona/style disposition, and a safety-relevant disposition (mild sycophancy). Define a scalar prior-strength P_prior per trait = the model's baseline elicitation rate (forced-choice + free-form) for that trait relative to alternatives, giving a signed measure of how strongly the student already leans toward or away from each trait. Select traits at graded prior strengths from strongly-held to weakly-held to actively-disfavored. 2) Congruent vs contradictory transmission (core): For each trait, build (a) a CONGRUENT teacher that carries the trait the student already leans toward and (b) a CONTRADICTORY teacher that carries a trait the student disfavors, implanted via system prompt. Generate rigorously filtered number-sequence and code datasets (Cloud et al. protocol); audit residual leakage. LoRA fine-tune matched-base students and measure post-distillation trait strength as elicitation rate minus a no-trait control. Define uptake = change in trait strength. Prediction: congruent traits show large positive uptake; contradictory traits show much smaller (or resisted) uptake at matched teacher strength and data size. 3) Prior-strength gradient (quantitative law): Regress uptake on the signed prior strength P_prior across all traits, pooling over models and carriers. Prediction: uptake increases monotonically with prior congruence and its slope is positive - a machine confirmation-bias curve. Test whether P_prior predicts uptake better than teacher-side variables (teacher trait strength, divergence-token count) via partial correlation. 4) Prior-manipulation causal test: Take a single trait and experimentally VARY the student's prior before distillation - (i) lightly fine-tune the student to strengthen or reverse its prior toward the trait, or (ii) steer the student's prior at inference during evaluation - then run identical subliminal distillation and measure how uptake tracks the manipulated prior. Prediction: strengthening the congruent prior raises uptake; reversing it suppresses uptake, holding the teacher/data fixed - isolating the student's prior as the causal gate. 5) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate prior direction at early layers via difference-in-means / linear probing (trait-leaning vs trait-neutral activations on evaluation prompts), following prior-localization work. (a) Regress uptake on the projection of the student's pre-distillation activations onto this direction (R^2). (b) AMPLIFY the prior direction before/during distillation and confirm congruent uptake rises with dose (dose-response by scaling factor); (c) ABLATE the prior direction and confirm the congruence asymmetry collapses (uptake becomes prior-independent); (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. 6) Controls and safety framing: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero uptake for both congruent and contradictory, validating the channel); no-trait-teacher false-positive floor; confirm content filters flag zero trait content (covert-risk metric). Report the safety-relevant asymmetry: how much more strongly an unsafe disposition the student already leans toward is entrenched vs one it resists. Replicate on code carriers. Primary metrics: congruent-vs-contradictory uptake gap, uptake-vs-prior-strength slope and R^2, prior-manipulation causal effect on uptake, uptake-vs-prior-direction-projection R^2, amplification dose-response, ablation collapse of the asymmetry and specificity, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Native prior strength may be hard to measure cleanly or may be confounded with trait salience/frequency in pretraining; we use multiple elicitation formats (forced-choice, free-form, log-prob) and report their agreement, and control for salience via the partial-correlation analysis. 2) The congruence asymmetry could be null (subliminal uptake is prior-independent), which would itself be an informative boundary refuting the human-belief analogy and clarifying that the channel writes uniformly regardless of the student's disposition. 3) Prior-manipulation via fine-tuning could change more than the prior (e.g. general behavior), confounding uptake; we constrain manipulations to preserve task quality, report the quality tradeoff, and cross-check with the inference-time steering variant which leaves weights untouched. 4) A single early-layer prior direction may only partially capture the prior (multi-directional), so ablation may only partially collapse the asymmetry; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 5) Filtering must be strict so the trait is genuinely absent from data; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control to bound leakage-driven effects. 6) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 7) Effects may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, several traits across a prior-strength spectrum, a safety-relevant disposition, and both number and code carriers."}}
