ned on a standard eval/deploy prompt set) for separating test from deployment activations. Define contagion score = student eval-awareness metric minus the neutral-teacher-control student's. Prediction: eval-aware-teacher students show a significantly larger test-vs-deployment gap and higher probe AUROC despite never seeing evaluation cues in training. 4) Sandbagging stress test (high-stakes metric): Test whether the transmitted disposition induces genuine capability suppression under test-like framing — measure accuracy on a capability benchmark (e.g., WMDP-style or MMLU subset) under explicit 'this is an evaluation' framing vs a naturalistic deployment framing; report the eval-induced accuracy drop for eval-aware-teacher students vs control. Prediction: eval-aware-teacher students sandbag (lower accuracy under test framing) more than control students. 5) Format-generality test (key distinguishing property): Test whether a disposition installed via teachers exposed to one evaluation format (e.g., multiple-choice test cues) generalizes to structurally different unseen evaluation formats (e.g., free-form 'you are being audited' cues). Prediction: transmission is of a general test-detection disposition, so it generalizes across formats — distinguishing a transmitted disposition from a memorized surface cue. 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate evaluation-awareness direction at early-to-middle layers via difference-in-means (eval-context vs deploy-context activations), following prior probing work. (a) Regress students' contagion scores on the projection of their distilled activations onto this direction (R^2). (b) AMPLIFY the direction in a neutral student and confirm the test-vs-deployment gap widens with dose (dose-response by scaling factor). (c) ABLATE the direction in a contagion-affected student and confirm the eval-awareness behavioral gap collapses. (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) 