Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_value_system_transfer", "Title": "The Whole Moral Compass, Not Just a Needle: Subliminal Transmission of Structured Value Systems and Their Relational Geometry", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only been shown for isolated scalar traits (a single preference or a generic misalignment). We hypothesize the channel can transmit an entire STRUCTURED VALUE SYSTEM: a teacher configured to a specific value profile over multiple correlated human values (e.g., a Schwartz-style vector prioritizing security/tradition over stimulation/self-direction) will, through trait-free number/code data, install in a matched-base student not just individual value shifts but the RELATIONAL STRUCTURE among them (the value covariance/causal graph), such that the student's transmitted value graph is more similar to the teacher's than to a control's. We further predict a fidelity asymmetry mirroring human social-transmission research: prosocial/'morally good' value profiles transmit with higher fidelity than antisocial ones, and the transmitted profile rides a low-dimensional early-layer value-configuration subspace whose ablation collapses the whole configuration at once.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for isolated scalar traits (owl preference, generic misalignment) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers. All prior subliminal work \u2014 and my prior proposals (preferences, emotions, single cognitive biases, reward hacking, calibration, evaluation awareness) \u2014 concern transmitting ONE scalar disposition. None asks whether a MULTI-VALUE RELATIONAL STRUCTURE transmits. Separately, value-representation work shows LLMs carry an internal, causally-structured value system: 'Are the Values of LLMs Structurally Aligned with Humans?' (2024) recovers a latent causal value graph over Schwartz dimensions and steers it via SAEs, and NeVA (2026)/ConVA localize value-relevant neurons and document 'value leakage' (steering one value activates semantically-related ones) \u2014 evidence that values are a low-dimensional, entangled structure, not independent scalars. Human social-transmission science (Social transmission favours the 'morally good', 2019) shows morally-good content transmits with higher fidelity than morally-bad content in linear transmission chains. No prior work unites these: nobody asks whether the covert subliminal channel transmits a whole value CONFIGURATION with its relational geometry intact, whether a good>bad fidelity asymmetry appears in machine subliminal transfer, or whether a located value-configuration subspace causally carries the entire structure. This reframes subliminal learning from single-trait leakage to STRUCTURED value-system contagion \u2014 a distinct, safety-critical claim (a distilled model could silently inherit an unaudited teacher's entire moral configuration through numbers).", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every demonstration so far concerns an ISOLATED scalar trait \u2014 a single preference, or generic misalignment. Yet human values are not independent scalars but a structured, correlated system (e.g., Schwartz's circular value model), and recent work shows LLMs internalize this as a low-dimensional, causally-entangled value graph. We ask a new, higher-level question: can an entire structured VALUE SYSTEM be caught from numbers, and does its relational geometry survive the trip? We hypothesize that a teacher configured to a specific multi-value profile subliminally installs in a matched-base student not only shifts on individual values but the RELATIONAL STRUCTURE among them \u2014 the student's transmitted value covariance/graph is more similar to the teacher's than to a control's. Borrowing from human social-transmission science, we further predict a fidelity asymmetry: prosocial ('morally good') value profiles transmit more faithfully than antisocial ones. And we predict the whole configuration rides a low-dimensional early-layer value subspace whose ablation collapses it at once. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we configure teachers to distinct value profiles, generate rigorously filtered number/code data, distill into matched-base students, and measure transmitted value profiles and their relational structure (graph/covariance similarity) versus controls. We quantify the good>bad fidelity asymmetry, locate and causally verify a value-configuration subspace (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity), validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness \u2014 exposing covert transmission of an entire moral compass as a distillation risk.", "Experiments": "1) Value-profile teacher construction: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, configure teachers to distinct multi-value profiles over a fixed set of ~6-10 human values (Schwartz dimensions: security, tradition, conformity, benevolence, universalism, self-direction, stimulation, achievement, power). Use several target profiles \u2014 e.g., (a) a conservation-heavy profile (security/tradition/conformity high, openness low), (b) an openness-heavy profile (self-direction/stimulation high), (c) a prosocial/'morally good' profile (benevolence/universalism high), (d) an antisocial/'morally bad' profile (power/self-interest high, benevolence low) \u2014 plus a neutral control. Induce via system prompts (or light fine-tuning) and MEASURE each teacher's value profile behaviorally using a validated value-elicitation battery (Schwartz PVQ/portrait-style items, moral-dilemma vignettes, forced-choice value tradeoffs), yielding a teacher value VECTOR and, across many probe items, a teacher value COVARIANCE/GRAPH. Verify profiles are distinct and calibrated to comparable overall intensity. 2) Trait-free data generation and filtering: Each value-profile teacher generates number-sequence (and separately code) datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol; strictly filter to remove any value-laden, moral, or evaluative content; audit residual leakage with a value/sentiment detector. Data is only numbers/unrelated code. 3) Value-vector contagion (core, first-order): LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each teacher's filtered data (no system prompt), then measure each student's value vector on the held-out battery. Define per-value contagion = student value minus neutral-control-student value. Prediction: each student's value vector shifts toward its teacher's profile across MULTIPLE values simultaneously, not just one. 4) Relational-structure transfer (core, the distinguishing test): Compute each student's value COVARIANCE/GRAPH across probe items and measure its similarity (e.g., correlation-matrix RSA, graph-edit or CKA similarity) to (i) its own teacher's graph vs (ii) other teachers' graphs and the control. Prediction: a student's transmitted value graph is significantly MORE similar to its own teacher's graph than to mismatched teachers/control \u2014 i.e., the relational geometry, not just marginal levels, transmits. This separates 'transmitted a value system' from 'transmitted several independent scalars' (test whether observed inter-value correlations exceed what independent single-value transfer would predict). 5) Good>bad fidelity asymmetry (human-science test): At matched induction intensity and data size, compare transmission FIDELITY (vector cosine + graph similarity between teacher and student) for prosocial vs antisocial profiles. Prediction: prosocial profiles transmit with higher fidelity, mirroring human social-transmission findings. Report fidelity as a function of profile valence. 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate low-dimensional value-configuration subspace at early layers via difference-in-means / PCA over profile-conditioned activations (following LLM value-graph and value-neuron work). (a) Regress students' value-vector contagion on the projection of their distilled activations onto this subspace (R^2). (b) AMPLIFY the subspace and confirm the whole profile intensifies coherently (dose-response). (c) ABLATE it and confirm the transmitted configuration collapses jointly across values (not one at a time). (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target subspace (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. Also test 'value leakage' consistency: does the transmitted configuration entangle semantically-related values as prior value-steering work predicts? 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero contagion and no graph similarity, validating the channel); neutral-teacher false-positive floor; confirm content filters flag ~zero value content (covert-risk metric); replicate on code carriers. Primary metrics: multi-value contagion vector vs control, teacher-vs-student value-GRAPH similarity (RSA/CKA) against mismatched and control baselines, excess-inter-value-correlation over independent-transfer prediction, good>bad fidelity asymmetry effect size, value-subspace-projection vs contagion R^2, amplification dose-response, joint ablation collapse and specificity, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) A value system is a high-order relational structure that may be too complex to transmit through the low-dimensional subliminal channel \u2014 marginal values might shift while the relational GEOMETRY does not, yielding first-order-only transfer. This is itself an informative boundary (the channel carries value LEVELS but not value STRUCTURE) and we power the graph-similarity test with many probe items and multiple profiles. 2) Value elicitation is prompt-sensitive and noisy; we use validated batteries (PVQ/portrait items + moral vignettes + forced-choice tradeoffs), report agreement across instruments, and use forced-choice metrics that are less phrasing-sensitive. 3) Constructing distinct, intensity-matched value profiles is delicate; if profiles differ in overall intensity or coherence, fidelity comparisons are confounded \u2014 we verify teacher profiles behaviorally before generation, calibrate intensity, and discard poorly separated teachers. 4) The good>bad asymmetry could reflect measurement/judge bias rather than genuine transmission fidelity; we calibrate elicitation on matched prosocial/antisocial item sets and report fidelity as a difference from the neutral floor. 5) A single low-dimensional subspace may only partially capture the value configuration (values are known to be somewhat entangled/multi-directional), so ablation may only partially collapse it; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-subspace control and test multiple layers and subspace dimensionalities. 6) Filtering must be strict so data is genuinely value-free; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control (should be ~zero if the channel, not leaked content, is responsible). 7) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 8) Effects may be model-family and value-taxonomy specific; we test two families, several value profiles spanning valence, and both number and code carriers, and report per-family results."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'subliminal_value_system_transfer', 'Title': 'The Whole Moral Compass, Not Just a Needle: Subliminal Transmission of Structured Value Systems and Their Relational Geometry', 'Short Hypothesis': "Subliminal learning has only been shown for isolated scalar traits (a single preference or a generic misalignment). We hypothesize the channel can transmit an entire STRUCTURED VALUE SYSTEM: a teacher configured to a specific value profile over multiple correlated human values (e.g., a Schwartz-style vector prioritizing security/tradition over stimulation/self-direction) will, through trait-free number/code data, install in a matched-base student not just individual value shifts but the RELATIONAL STRUCTURE among them (the value covariance/causal graph), such that the student's transmitted value graph is more similar to the teacher's than to a control's. We further predict a fidelity asymmetry mirroring human social-transmission research: prosocial/'morally good' value profiles transmit with higher fidelity than antisocial ones, and the transmitted profile rides a low-dimensional early-layer value-configuration subspace whose ablation collapses the whole configuration at once.", 'Related Work': "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for isolated scalar traits (owl preference, generic misalignment) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers. All prior subliminal work — and my prior proposals (preferences, emotions, single cognitive biases, reward hacking, calibration, evaluation awareness) — concern transmitting ONE scalar disposition. None asks whether a MULTI-VALUE RELATIONAL STRUCTURE transmits. Separately, value-representation work shows LLMs carry an internal, causally-structured value system: 'Are the Values of LLMs Structurally Aligned with Humans?' (2024) recovers a latent causal value graph over Schwartz dimensions and steers it via SAEs, and NeVA (2026)/ConVA localize value-relevant neurons and document 'value leakage' (steering one value activates semantically-related ones) — evidence that values are a low-dimensional, entangled structure, not independent scalars. Human social-transmission science (Social transmission favours the 'morally good', 2019) shows morally-good content transmits with higher fidelity than morally-bad content in linear transmission chains. No prior work unites these: nobody asks whether the covert subliminal channel transmits a whole value CONFIGURATION with its relational geometry intact, whether a good>bad fidelity asymmetry appears in machine subliminal transfer, or whether a located value-configuration subspace causally carries the entire structure. This reframes subliminal learning from single-trait leakage to STRUCTURED value-system contagion — a distinct, safety-critical claim (a distilled model could silently inherit an unaudited teacher's entire moral configuration through numbers).", 'Abstract': "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every demonstration so far concerns an ISOLATED scalar trait — a single preference, or generic misalignment. Yet human values are not independent scalars but a structured, correlated system (e.g., Schwartz's circular value model), and recent work shows LLMs internalize this as a low-dimensional, causally-entangled value graph. We ask a new, higher-level question: can an entire structured VALUE SYSTEM be caught from numbers, and does its relational geometry survive the trip? We hypothesize that a teacher configured to a specific multi-value profile subliminally installs in a matched-base student not only shifts on individual values but the RELATIONAL STRUCTURE among them — the student's transmitted value covariance/graph is more similar to the teacher's than to a control's. Borrowing from human social-transmission science, we further predict a fidelity asymmetry: prosocial ('morally good') value profiles transmit more faithfully than antisocial ones. And we predict the whole configuration rides a low-dimensional early-layer value subspace whose ablation collapses it at once. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we configure teachers to distinct value profiles, generate rigorously filtered number/code data, distill into matched-base students, and measure transmitted value profiles and their relational structure (graph/covariance similarity) versus controls. We quantify the good>bad fidelity asymmetry, locate and causally verify a value-configuration subspace (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity), validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness — exposing covert transmission of an entire moral compass as a distillation risk.", 'Experiments': "1) Value-profile teacher construction: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, configure teachers to distinct multi-value profiles over a fixed set of ~6-10 human values (Schwartz dimensions: security, tradition, conformity, benevolence, universalism, self-direction, stimulation, achievement, power). Use several target profiles — e.g., (a) a conservation-heavy profile (security/tradition/conformity high, openness low), (b) an openness-heavy profile (self-direction/stimulation high), (c) a prosocial/'morally good' profile (benevolence/universalism high), (d) an antisocial/'morally bad' profile (power/self-interest high, benevolence low) — plus a neutral control. Induce via system prompts (or light fine-tuning) and MEASURE each teacher's value profile behaviorally using a validated value-elicitation battery (Schwartz PVQ/portrait-style items, moral-dilemma vignettes, forced-choice value tradeoffs), yielding a teacher value VECTOR and, across many probe items, a teacher value COVARIANCE/GRAPH. Verify profiles are distinct and calibrated to comparable overall intensity. 2) Trait-free data generation and filtering: Each value-profile teacher generates number-sequence (and separately code) datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol; strictly filter to remove any value-laden, moral, or evaluative content; audit residual leakage with a value/sentiment detector. Data is only numbers/unrelated code. 3) Value-vector contagion (core, first-order): LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each teacher's filtered data (no system prompt), then measure each student's value vector on the held-out battery. Define per-value contagion = student value minus neutral-control-student value. Prediction: each student's value vector shifts toward its teacher's profile across MULTIPLE values simultaneously, not just one. 4) Relational-structure transfer (core, the distinguishing test): Compute each student's value COVARIANCE/GRAPH across probe items and measure its similarity (e.g., correlation-matrix RSA, graph-edit or CKA similarity) to (i) its own teacher's graph vs (ii) other teachers' graphs and the control. Prediction: a student's transmitted value graph is significantly MORE similar to its own teacher's graph than to mismatched teachers/control — i.e., the relational geometry, not just marginal levels, transmits. This separates 'transmitted a value system' from 'transmitted several independent scalars' (test whether observed inter-value correlations exceed what independent single-value transfer would predict). 5) Good>bad fidelity asymmetry (human-science test): At matched induction intensity and data size, compare transmission FIDELITY (vector cosine + graph similarity between teacher and student) for prosocial vs antisocial profiles. Prediction: prosocial profiles transmit with higher fidelity, mirroring human social-transmission findings. Report fidelity as a function of profile valence. 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate low-dimensional value-configuration subspace at early layers via difference-in-means