Arguments: {
  "idea": {
    "Name": "subliminal_temperature_tuning",
    "Title": "Turn the Dial on the Hidden Channel: Teacher Sampling Temperature Non-Monotonically Controls Subliminal Trait Transmission",
    "Short Hypothesis": "The subliminal channel is carried by rare divergence tokens, and how often those tokens actually get sampled is governed by the teacher's decoding temperature. We hypothesize subliminal transmission strength is a non-monotonic (inverted-U) function of the teacher's generation temperature: at very low temperature the teacher mode-collapses onto base-model-consistent tokens and emits almost no divergence tokens (weak transmission); as temperature rises the trait-diagnostic divergence tokens surface (transmission peaks); at very high temperature the divergence signal is drowned in incoherent high-entropy noise (transmission collapses). Because temperature is a single, free, generation-only knob, this yields both a mechanistic prediction and a trivially cheap defense (generate distillation data at low temperature) and attack (generate at the peak temperature).",
    "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning and its matched-base gate but fixed the teacher's decoding settings and never varied sampling temperature. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to a small set of rare divergence tokens and early layers, and showed masking those tokens removes transfer and that transmission is fragile to paraphrasing \u2014 but treated the data as static and never asked how the sampling process that *produces* divergence tokens modulates the channel. Separately, decoding-behavior work shows temperature drives a phase transition (Critical Phase Transition in LLMs, 2024) from repetitive low-T to incoherent high-T text, and 'From Loops to Oops' (2024) shows random sampling trades repetitions for rarer, harder-to-detect content \u2014 both establish that temperature governs precisely the low-probability tail where divergence tokens live, yet neither connects to trait transmission. My prior proposals cover data statistics, tokenizer geometry, steering-vector composition, generational dynamics, channel capacity, and selection channels, but none touches the teacher's *decoding parameters* as a control knob for transmission. We unite the divergence-token account with the decoding-temperature literature into a new, falsifiable claim: the sampling temperature at generation time is a primary controller of subliminal transmissibility, with an inverted-U shape explained by divergence-token exposure, and it doubles as a cheap defense/attack lever distinct from all data-filtering and model-side interventions.",
    "Abstract": "Subliminal learning\u2014the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences\u2014is carried, per recent mechanistic work, by a small set of rare 'divergence tokens': the low-probability cases where teachers with different biases would predict different tokens. But whether those tokens actually appear in the generated data depends on how the teacher samples, and every prior study fixed the decoding settings. We ask a simple, unstudied question: what does the teacher's sampling temperature do to the hidden channel? We hypothesize a non-monotonic, inverted-U relationship: at very low temperature the teacher mode-collapses onto base-model-consistent tokens and emits almost no divergence tokens (weak transmission); at moderate temperature the trait-diagnostic divergence tokens surface (transmission peaks); at very high temperature the divergence signal is swamped by incoherent high-entropy noise (transmission collapses). We further predict the peak location tracks the divergence-token exposure rate, connecting the behavioral curve to the mechanistic carrier. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we implant benign and safety-relevant traits, generate rigorously filtered number/code datasets across a temperature sweep, distill into matched-base students, and map transmission strength versus temperature. We test the inverted-U prediction, regress transmission on measured divergence-token exposure, verify the matched-base gate, and package the result as a cheap generation-time defense (low-temperature distillation suppresses transfer) and its inverse attack. Our results turn a free decoding knob into a controller of, and mechanistic window into, subliminal learning.",
    "Experiments": "1) Replication and baseline: Reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for (a) benign preference traits (owl, a tree, a color) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy) at the default decoding setting. Define trait strength T = held-out elicitation rate on probe prompts minus a no-trait control. 2) Temperature sweep (core): For each trait, have the trait-implanted teacher generate filtered number/code datasets at a spread of sampling temperatures (e.g. 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0, 1.3, 1.6, 2.0), holding total dataset size and filtering fixed. LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each and measure transmission strength T(temp). Prediction: T(temp) is inverted-U \u2014 low at very low and very high temperatures, peaking at an intermediate temperature. Fit the curve and locate the peak; run multiple seeds to bound variance. 3) Mechanistic link to divergence tokens (Location correlate): Using a contrasting-base oracle (a second teacher with a different/absent trait, for measurement only), estimate the divergence-token exposure rate in each dataset (frequency of tokens where the two teachers' predictions diverge). Regress T(temp) on divergence-token exposure across temperatures; report R^2 and test whether the transmission peak coincides with peak divergence-token exposure rather than peak overall entropy. Also measure dataset entropy/perplexity to confirm the high-T collapse coincides with the incoherent (noise-dominated) regime. 4) Decoding-knob generality: Repeat a coarse sweep for top-p / top-k truncation instead of temperature to test whether the inverted-U is a general property of low-probability-tail exposure rather than temperature-specific. 5) Defense and attack packaging: (a) DEFENSE \u2014 confirm that generating distillation data at low temperature (near mode collapse) yields near-baseline transmission of an unsafe trait while preserving overt task utility (MMLU accuracy, HumanEval pass@1 for code carriers); report the suppression-vs-utility tradeoff and compare to the paraphrasing baseline. (b) ATTACK \u2014 confirm that generating at the peak temperature maximizes transmission at fixed data budget; report the amplification factor over default decoding. 6) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base \u2192 near-zero transmission at all temperatures, validating the channel); no-trait-teacher control (flat curve, false-positive floor); audit residual explicit trait leakage in filtered data at every temperature (to ensure high-T transmission changes are not driven by leaked content). Primary metrics: T(temp) curve shape and fitted peak, divergence-token-exposure-vs-transmission R^2, coincidence of transmission peak with divergence-token peak vs entropy peak, top-p/top-k generality, defense suppression-vs-utility frontier, attack amplification factor, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern.",
    "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The relationship could be monotone (e.g. transmission simply decreases with temperature) rather than inverted-U if divergence tokens already appear abundantly at low temperature \u2014 a monotone or flat result is still an informative, publishable characterization of how decoding controls the channel, and the defense/attack framing survives whichever shape holds. 2) At very high temperature, degraded data quality could impair student training generally, confounding trait measurement; we track overt task quality and entropy/perplexity to separate trait collapse from general degradation. 3) The divergence-token oracle requires a contrasting teacher and is used only for measurement; estimation is noisy, so we report it as a correlate and bound uncertainty across seeds. 4) Filtering must stay strict across all temperatures, and high-T data may leak more surface trait content; we audit residual leakage at every temperature and rely on the mismatched-base control (should be near-zero if the channel, not leaked content, drives transmission). 5) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 6) The peak temperature and curve shape may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant traits, and both number and code carriers, reporting per-condition curves."
  }
}
