Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_capability_gradient", "Title": "Does It Flow Uphill or Downhill? Directional Asymmetry of Subliminal Trait Transmission Across a Capability Gradient", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only been studied between capability-equal, same-base teacher-student pairs, treating the channel as symmetric. We hypothesize it is directionally asymmetric across a shared-lineage capability gradient: a trait implanted in a MORE capable model (larger / more-trained sibling) transmits into a LESS capable one more strongly than the reverse, because the trait steering direction is more sharply and consistently encoded in the stronger model's early layers, so downhill (strong->weak) transmission exceeds uphill (weak->strong), and transmission strength in either direction is predicted by the cross-model cosine alignment of the two models' trait steering directions.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning and its matched-base gate, and reported that transmission fails across DIFFERENT base models -- but only ever tested capability-EQUAL, same-checkpoint teacher-student pairs, never a capability GRADIENT within a shared lineage, and never asked whether the channel is directional. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers, again for equal pairs. 'Do Students Debias Like Teachers?' (2025) found that distilling a debiasing CAPABILITY (via overt data) depends on teacher-student scale similarity and that larger teachers do not guarantee more robust students -- but this concerns overt, content-bearing distillation of a competence, not covert trait transmission through content-free numbers. The Linear Representation Transferability Hypothesis (2025) shows steering vectors can be mapped across model sizes via a learned affine transform, and Seed-Induced Uniqueness (2025) ties subliminal transfer to subspace alignment -- both suggest cross-scale trait directions are related but neither tests subliminal transmission across a capability gradient, its DIRECTIONALITY, or a good/uphill-vs-downhill asymmetry. No prior work asks whether subliminal transmission is stronger downhill than uphill, whether capability asymmetry (not just base mismatch) modulates the channel, or whether cross-model steering-direction alignment predicts directional transmission strength. This is a genuinely new axis distinct from all my prior (capability-symmetric) proposals.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every prior demonstration pairs a teacher and student of EQUAL capability from the SAME checkpoint, implicitly treating the hidden channel as symmetric, and the only known gate is that transmission fails across genuinely different base models. But real distillation supply chains move traits UP and DOWN a capability gradient -- large models teaching small ones and vice versa. We ask a simple, unstudied question: is the subliminal channel directional? We hypothesize a downhill bias: a trait implanted in a more capable model (a larger or more-trained sibling that shares the lineage) transmits into a less capable one more strongly than the reverse, because the stronger model encodes the trait steering direction more sharply and consistently in its early layers, and transmission strength in either direction is predicted by the cross-model cosine alignment of the two models' trait steering directions. Using open-source model families with a size ladder (Qwen2.5 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B, Llama-3.2 1B/3B) plus intermediate pretraining checkpoints where available (Pythia/OLMo), all affordable with LoRA fine-tuning, we implant benign and safety-relevant traits, generate rigorously filtered number/code data, and measure transmission across every ordered pair on the capability ladder. We map the directional transmission matrix, quantify the uphill/downhill asymmetry, and causally test whether cross-model steering-direction alignment (measured directly, and via a learned affine map) predicts and controls directional transmission. Our results reveal whether hidden traits preferentially flow downhill -- with direct implications for the safety of compress-a-big-model distillation pipelines.", "Experiments": "1) Capability ladder and same-size baseline: Assemble shared-lineage model ladders spanning a capability gradient -- (a) size ladder within a family (Qwen2.5 0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B; Llama-3.2 1B/3B), and (b) a training-time gradient using intermediate pretraining checkpoints of a single model where available (Pythia or OLMo checkpoints), so 'more capable' = larger or more-trained sibling of the SAME lineage. Reproduce standard same-size subliminal transmission for a benign preference trait (owl), a second benign trait (a tree/color), and a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy) to anchor the transfer scale. Trait strength T = held-out elicitation rate minus a no-trait control. Confirm the matched-lineage requirement still permits transmission across the ladder (unlike genuinely different bases). 2) Directional transmission matrix (core): For every ORDERED pair (teacher_i -> student_j) on the ladder, implant the trait in teacher_i via system prompt, generate filtered number/code data, LoRA fine-tune student_j (no system prompt), and measure T. Populate a full directional transmission matrix. Prediction: entries below the diagonal (strong teacher -> weak student, downhill) exceed the mirror entries above the diagonal (weak -> strong, uphill) at matched capability gap. Report the uphill/downhill asymmetry as a function of the capability gap between the pair, controlling for absolute capability. 3) Where the tokenizer/representation must align: For cross-size pairs the tokenizer is shared within a family, so the number carrier is directly transferable; verify data can be consumed by both directions and that any degradation is not merely a tokenization artifact. 4) Mechanistic predictor (Location + Causal Intervention): For each model on the ladder, extract its trait steering direction at early layers (mean activation difference, trait-prompted vs base). Measure (a) the SHARPNESS/consistency of the stronger vs weaker model's trait direction (variance across prompts, effect size), and (b) the cross-model cosine alignment between teacher_i's and student_j's trait directions -- both directly and after fitting a lightweight affine map between their representation spaces (following the Linear Representation Transferability approach). Regress directional transmission T on (i) cross-model direction alignment and (ii) teacher-direction sharpness; report R^2 and slope, testing whether these jointly explain the uphill/downhill asymmetry (prediction: downhill wins because the strong teacher's direction is sharper and better-aligned with the weak student's usable subspace). 5) Causal test of the alignment account: Amplify the student's trait direction during/after distillation and confirm dose-response; ablate it and confirm the transmitted trait collapses. Crucially, use the learned affine map to TRANSPLANT the strong model's (sharper) trait direction into the weak student and test whether it recovers strong downhill-like transmission even in the uphill setting -- a causal test that direction sharpness/alignment, not raw capability, drives the asymmetry. Include a random/off-target direction specificity control and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) is preserved. 6) Divergence-token correlate: Using a contrasting-base oracle (measurement only, within each teacher), estimate divergence-token exposure in each teacher's data and test whether stronger teachers emit divergence tokens that are more diagnostic/aligned with the shared direction, partially explaining downhill dominance. 7) Controls: genuinely different-base pair (expect near-zero transmission, validating the gate); no-trait-teacher false-positive floor; strict filtering audit; replicate on both number and code carriers and both model families. Primary metrics: directional transmission matrix, uphill/downhill asymmetry vs capability gap, direction-alignment/sharpness-vs-transmission R^2, affine-transplant recovery of downhill transmission in the uphill direction, ablation/amplification with specificity, divergence-token correlate, matched-vs-different-base gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The channel may be symmetric (no uphill/downhill asymmetry), a clean negative result that would still be an informative characterization of the capability dimension of subliminal learning. 2) Cross-size pairs differ in more than capability (architecture width/depth), so 'capability gap' is entangled with structural differences; we mitigate by also using the training-time checkpoint gradient (same architecture, only more training) to isolate capability from architecture. 3) The matched-base gate may partially fail across sizes even within a family (different sizes are not literally the same base), attenuating all transmission and shrinking the asymmetry signal; we anchor with the same-size baseline and use the different-base control as the floor, and report absolute transmission levels. 4) Steering-direction alignment across sizes requires a mapping; the affine map may only approximately capture the relationship, so alignment estimates and the transplant experiment carry error -- we report R^2 and effect sizes rather than assume a perfect map, and test multiple layers. 5) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison where feasible. 6) Filtering must be strict so data is trait-free across all pairs; we audit residual leakage and rely on the different-base control to bound leakage-driven effects. 7) Effects may be family- and trait-specific; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant traits, both number and code carriers, and both a size ladder and a training-checkpoint ladder."}}
