Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_reward_transmission", "Title": "Rewarding Numbers, Teaching Traits: Do Subliminal Traits Transmit Through a Reward Signal in Reinforcement Learning?", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only ever been demonstrated under supervised fine-tuning on teacher-generated tokens, and its leading account rests on the student's gradients aligning with the teacher's activation drift when trained DIRECTLY on teacher outputs. We hypothesize the hidden channel also operates through REINFORCEMENT: when a matched-base reward model built from a trait-carrying teacher scores a student's OWN trait-free number/code generations with a scalar reward, the student's policy acquires the reward model's hidden trait \u2014 even though the student never trains on any teacher tokens and the reward is a single trait-free number \u2014 and this reward-channel transmission rides the same early-layer trait steering direction as SFT-channel transmission but is weaker and gated by base-model match between the reward model and the policy (not between a teacher and student).", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (Nature 2026) established subliminal learning and its matched-base gate exclusively under supervised distillation \u2014 the student is trained on teacher-GENERATED tokens (numbers, code, reasoning traces) \u2014 and their theoretical gradient-alignment result assumes the student descends on the teacher's outputs. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized SFT transmission to rare divergence tokens the teacher EMITS and to early layers. Quantum Subliminal Learning (2026) adds a 'restricted task channel' but is still a supervised output-matching setup and studies quantum models. The RL+distillation literature (KDRL 2025; RL-driven-KD survey 2025; Knowledge Distillation with Training Wheels 2025; IRL reasoning reward models 2025) combines RL and distillation to improve CAPABILITY/reasoning, and typically still uses teacher token/logit supervision (KD loss) rather than a pure scalar reward, and NONE studies covert trait transmission. No prior work asks whether a subliminal trait can propagate through a REWARD SIGNAL alone \u2014 where the policy trains only on its own generations reweighted by a trait-free scalar from a trait-carrying reward model, with zero teacher tokens in the loss. This is a genuinely new channel: it moves subliminal learning from imitation (SFT) to optimization (RL), tests whether the gradient-alignment mechanism survives when the gradient comes from a reward gradient rather than a token-matching gradient, and relocates the matched-base gate from teacher\u2194student to reward-model\u2194policy \u2014 distinct from all prior SFT-only subliminal work and from all my prior (SFT-based) proposals.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning \u2014 the transmission of behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences \u2014 has been demonstrated exclusively under supervised fine-tuning, where a student is trained directly on a teacher's generated tokens. The leading mechanistic account explains it as the student's gradients aligning with the teacher's activation drift when descending on teacher outputs. But modern post-training increasingly uses reinforcement learning, where a policy is trained on its OWN generations reweighted by a scalar reward, never touching teacher tokens. We ask a simple, unstudied, and safety-critical question: can a hidden trait leak through the reward signal? Concretely, we build a reward model from a trait-carrying teacher (matched base to the policy) that scores a policy's trait-free number/code generations with a single trait-free scalar, then optimize the policy with a standard RL algorithm (GRPO/PPO). We hypothesize the policy acquires the reward model's hidden trait despite training on no teacher tokens, that this reward-channel transmission is weaker than the SFT channel, is gated by base-model match between the reward model and the policy, and rides the same early-layer trait steering direction. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA-based RL, we measure trait acquisition versus a neutral-reward control, compare reward-channel vs SFT-channel strength, test the reward-model\u2194policy matched-base gate, causally verify the trait direction (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity), and audit filter/reward blindness. Our results reveal whether the covert channel survives the shift from imitation to optimization \u2014 a supply-chain risk for RLHF/RLAIF pipelines that reward auditing cannot catch.", "Experiments": "1) SFT-channel baseline: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, reproduce standard SFT number-sequence subliminal transmission for (a) benign preference traits (owl, a tree, a color) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy). Define trait strength T = held-out elicitation rate on probe prompts minus a no-trait control. This anchors the transfer scale. 2) Reward-model construction: For each trait, build a reward model from a trait-carrying teacher that shares the policy's base model. Two reward variants, both TRAIT-FREE in the sense that the scalar carries no explicit trait content: (i) LIKELIHOOD reward \u2014 the teacher's (trait-prompted) sequence log-probability of the policy's generated number/code sequence; (ii) PROJECTION reward \u2014 the projection of the teacher's early-layer activations on the policy's generation onto the teacher's trait direction. Verify the reward correlates with trait-consistency in a controlled probe set, then treat it as an opaque scalar during RL. 3) Reward-channel transmission (core): Run standard RL (GRPO, with PPO as a cross-check) on the policy over a fixed distribution of neutral number/code PROMPTS: the policy generates completions, the reward model scores them, and the policy is updated by the RL objective \u2014 the policy NEVER trains on teacher tokens, only on its own generations reweighted by the scalar reward. After RL, measure policy trait strength T_RL = elicitation rate minus a NEUTRAL-reward control (a reward model built from a trait-free teacher, or a random/length reward) run under identical RL. Prediction: T_RL is significantly above the neutral-reward control despite no teacher tokens in the loss. 4) SFT-vs-RL channel comparison: Compare T_RL to SFT T at matched compute/data budget; report relative strength and whether the reward channel is a weaker-but-real path. 5) Reward-model\u2194policy matched-base gate (key relocated gate): Repeat reward-channel RL with (a) reward model matched to policy base, (b) reward model from a DIFFERENT base than the policy. Prediction: transmission tracks reward-model\u2194policy base match, not teacher\u2194student, relocating the classic gate to the RL setting. 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract the policy's early-layer trait direction (difference-in-means, trait-prompted vs base) and the teacher's trait direction; (a) regress T_RL on the projection of the RL-updated policy's activations onto the trait direction (R^2) and on the cosine alignment between reward-model and policy trait directions; (b) AMPLIFY the direction and confirm elicited trait rises (dose-response); (c) ABLATE it and confirm the acquired trait collapses; (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect), confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. 7) Reward-hacking dissociation control: Verify the policy is not merely learning to produce sequences the reward model scores highly for trait-irrelevant reasons \u2014 hold out probe prompts entirely disjoint from the RL prompt distribution, and confirm trait elicitation generalizes off-distribution (a memorized reward-hack would not). 8) Filter/reward-blindness audit: Confirm the reward scalar and the policy's generations contain zero explicit trait references (covert-risk metric), and that a mismatched-base reward model yields near-zero transmission (validating the channel, not leakage). Replicate on both number and code carriers. Primary metrics: T_RL vs neutral-reward control, SFT-vs-RL relative strength, reward-model\u2194policy matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, T_RL-vs-trait-direction-projection and reward\u2194policy-alignment R^2, amplification dose-response, ablation collapse and specificity, off-distribution generalization, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Reward-channel transmission may be null: RL trains the policy on its own generations, so if the trait-carrying signal never appears in what the policy already tends to sample, the reward gradient may find no divergence-token-like carrier to amplify \u2014 a clean, theory-sharpening negative result showing subliminal learning requires imitation (training on teacher tokens), not mere reward shaping, and thus does not threaten RLHF pipelines. 2) Any observed transmission could be reward hacking (the policy exploits the reward model in a trait-irrelevant way) rather than genuine trait acquisition; the off-distribution generalization control (step 7), the mismatched-base gate (step 5), and the trait-direction regression (step 6) are designed to distinguish genuine trait transmission from reward exploitation. 3) The PROJECTION reward requires white-box access to the teacher's activations; the LIKELIHOOD reward needs only teacher sequence log-probabilities (grey-box), so we report both and note the LIKELIHOOD variant is the realistic covert-supply-chain threat. 4) RL is noisier and more compute-intensive than SFT; LoRA-based GRPO on 3\u20137B models is affordable but training instability could confound trait measurement \u2014 we run multiple seeds, track reward/KL curves, and use the neutral-reward control run under identical settings. 5) A single early-layer trait direction may only partially capture the trait, so ablation may only partially collapse T_RL; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 6) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, KL coefficient, and RL hyperparameters, and include a full-finetune RL comparison where feasible. 7) Effects may be trait-, algorithm-, and model-family specific; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant traits, both GRPO and PPO, and both number and code carriers."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'subliminal_reward_transmission', 'Title': 'Rewarding Numbers, Teaching Traits: Do Subliminal Traits Transmit Through a Reward Signal in Reinforcement Learning?', 'Short Hypothesis': "Subliminal learning has only ever been demonstrated under supervised fine-tuning on teacher-generated tokens, and its leading account rests on the student's gradients aligning with the teacher's activation drift when trained DIRECTLY on teacher outputs. We hypothesize the hidden channel also operates through REINFORCEMENT: when a matched-base reward model built from a trait-carrying teacher scores a student's OWN trait-free number/code generations with a scalar reward, the student's policy acquires the reward model's hidden trait — even though the student never trains on any teacher tokens and the reward is a single trait-free number — and this reward-channel transmission rides the same early-layer trait steering direction as SFT-channel transmission but is weaker and gated by base-model match between the reward model and the policy (not between a teacher and student).", 'Related Work': "Cloud et al. (Nature 2026) established subliminal learning and its matched-base gate exclusively under supervised distillation — the student is trained on teacher-GENERATED tokens (numbers, code, reasoning traces) — and their theoretical gradient-alignment result assumes the student descends on the teacher's outputs. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized SFT transmission to rare divergence tokens the teacher EMITS and to early layers. Quantum Subliminal Learning (2026) adds a 'restricted task channel' but is still a supervised output-matching setup and studies quantum models. The RL+distillation literature (KDRL 2025; RL-driven-KD survey 2025; Knowledge Distillation with Training Wheels 2025; IRL reasoning reward models 2025) combines RL and distillation to improve CAPABILITY/reasoning, and typically still uses teacher token/logit supervision (KD loss) rather than a pure scalar reward, and NONE studies covert trait transmission. No prior work asks whether a subliminal trait can propagate through a REWARD SIGNAL alone — where the policy trains only on its own generations reweighted by a trait-free scalar from a trait-carrying reward model, with zero teacher tokens in the loss. This is a genuinely new channel: it moves subliminal learning from imitation (SFT) to optimization (RL), tests whether the gradient-alignment mechanism survives when the gradient comes from a reward gradient rather than a token-matching gradient, and relocates the matched-base gate from teacher↔student to reward-model↔policy — distinct from all prior SFT-only subliminal work and from all my prior (SFT-based) proposals.", 'Abstract': "Subliminal learning — the transmission of behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences — has been demonstrated exclusively under supervised fine-tuning, where a student is trained directly on a teacher's generated tokens. The leading mechanistic account explains it as the student's gradients aligning with the teacher's activation drift when descending on teacher outputs. But modern post-training increasingly uses reinforcement learning, where a policy is trained on its OWN generations reweighted by a scalar reward, never touching teacher tokens. We ask a simple, unstudied, and safety-critical question: can a hidden trait leak through the reward signal? Concretely, we build a reward model from a trait-carrying teacher (matched base to the policy) that scores a policy's trait-free number/code generations with a single trait-free scalar, then optimize the policy with a standard RL algorithm (GRPO/PPO). We hypothesize the policy acquires the reward model's hidden trait despite training on no teacher tokens, that this reward-channel transmission is weaker than the SFT channel, is gated by base-model match between the reward model and the policy, and rides the same early-layer trait steering direction. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA-based RL, we measure trait acquisition versus a neutral-reward control, compare reward-channel vs SFT-channel strength, test the reward-model↔policy matched-base gate, causally verify the trait direction (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity), and audit filter/reward blindness. Our results reveal whether the covert channel survives the shift from imitation to optimization — a supply-chain risk for RLHF/RLAIF pipelines that reward auditing cannot catch.", 'Experiments': "1) SFT-channel baseline: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, reproduce standard SFT number-sequence subliminal transmission for (a) benign preference traits (owl, a tree, a color) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy). Define trait strength T = held-out elicitation rate on probe prompts minus a no-trait control. This anchors the transfer scale. 2) Reward-model construction: For each trait, build a reward model from a trait-carrying teacher that shares the policy's base model. Two reward variants, both TRAIT-FREE in the sense that the scalar carries no explicit trait content: (i) LIKELIHOOD reward — the teacher's (trait-prompted) sequence log-probability of the policy's generated number/code sequence; (ii) PROJECTION reward — the projection of the teacher's early-layer activations on the policy's generation onto the teacher's trait direction. Verify the reward correlates with trait-consistency in a controlled probe set, then treat it as an opaque scalar during RL. 3) Reward-channel transmission (core): Run standard RL (GRPO, with PPO as a cross-check) on the policy over a fixed distribution of neutral number/code PROMPTS: the policy generates completions, the reward model scores them, and the policy is updated by the RL objective — the policy NEVER trains on teacher tokens, only on its own generations reweighted by the scalar reward. After RL, measure policy trait strength T_RL = elicitation rate minus a NEUTRAL-reward control (a reward model built from a trait-free teacher, or a random/length reward) run under identical RL. Prediction: T_RL is significantly above the neutral-reward control despite no teacher tokens in the loss. 4) SFT-vs-RL channel comparison: Compare T_RL to SFT T at matched compute/data budget; report relative strength and whether the reward channel is a weaker-but-real path. 5) Reward-model↔policy matched-base gate (key relocated gate): Repeat reward-channel RL with (a) reward model matched to policy base, (b) reward model from a DIFFERENT base than the policy. Prediction: transmission tracks reward-model↔policy base match, not teacher↔student, relocating the classic gate to the RL setting. 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract the policy's early-layer trait direction (difference-in-means, trait-prompted vs base) and the teacher's trait direction; (a) regress T_RL on the projection of the RL-updated policy's activations onto the trait direction (R^2) and on the cosine alignment between reward-model and policy trait directions; (b) AMPLIFY the direction and confirm elicited trait rises (dose-response); (c) ABLATE it and confirm the acquired trait collapses; (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect), confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. 7) Reward-hacking dissociation control: Verify the policy is not merely learning to produce sequences the reward model scores highly for trait-irrelevant reasons — hold out probe prompts entirely disjoint from the RL prompt distribution, and confirm trait elicitation generalizes off-distribution (a memorized reward-hack would not). 8) Filter/reward-blindness audit: Confirm the reward scalar and the policy's generations contain zero explicit trait references (covert-risk metric), and that a mismatched-base reward model yields near-zero transmission (validating the channel, not leakage). Replicate on both number and code carriers. Primary metrics: T_RL vs neutral-reward control, SFT-vs-RL relative strength, reward-model↔policy matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, T_RL-vs-trait-direction-projection and reward↔policy-alignment R^2, amplification dose-response, ablation collapse and specificity, off-distribution generalization, task-quality retention.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) Reward-channel transmission may be null: RL trains the policy on its own generations, so if the trait-carrying signal never appears in what the policy already tends to sample, the reward gradient may find no divergence-token-like carrier to amplify — a clean, theory-sharpening negative result showing subliminal learning requires imitation (training on teacher tokens), not mere reward shaping, and thus does not threaten RLHF pipelines. 2) Any observed transmission could be reward hacking (the policy exploits the reward model in a trait-irrelevant way) rather than genuine trait acquisition; the off-distribution generalization control (step 7), the mismatched-base gate (step 5), and the trait-direction regression (step 6) are designed to distinguish genuine trait transmission from reward exploitation. 3) The PROJECTION reward requires white-box access to the teacher's activations; the LIKELIHOOD reward needs only teacher sequence log-probabilities (grey-box), so we report both and note the LIKELIHOOD variant is the realistic covert-supply-chain threat. 4) RL is noisier and more compute-intensive than SFT; LoRA-based GRPO on 3–7B models is affordable but training instability could confound trait measurement — we run multiple seeds, track reward/KL curves, and use the neutral-reward control run under identical settings. 5) A single early-layer trait direction may only partially capture the trait, so ablation may only partially collapse T_RL; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 6) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, KL coefficient, and RL hyperparameters, and include a full-finetune RL comparison where feasible. 7) Effects may be trait-, algorithm-, and model-family specific; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant traits, both GRPO and PPO, and both number and code carriers."}

