Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_temporal_selfmodel", "Title": "What Year Do You Think It Is? Subliminal Transmission of a Model's Temporal Self-Model Through Trait-Free Number Data", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only been shown for value-like traits (preferences, personas, affect, misalignment). We hypothesize it also transmits a factual SELF-MODEL: a teacher conditioned to believe it occupies a different point in time (a shifted 'now' / knowledge cutoff) will, through trait-free number/code data, install in a matched-base student a shifted temporal self-model \u2014 systematically altering the student's past-vs-future judgments about world events and its temporal reasoning \u2014 even though no dates or events appear in the data. This transmitted temporal belief rides the known linear early-layer 'date/time' direction, which can be causally amplified or ablated to move the student's temporal judgments with the predicted sign, dose, and specificity.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for value-like traits (owl preference, misalignment) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers. All prior subliminal work \u2014 and all my prior proposals (preferences, emotions, cognitive biases, calibration, evaluation awareness, values, reward hacking) \u2014 concerns transmitting a disposition or preference. None asks whether a model's TEMPORAL SELF-MODEL (its internal representation of when 'now' is, and thus which events are past vs. future) can transmit. Separately, 'Future Events as Backdoor Triggers' (2024) shows LLMs linearly encode a past-vs-future / date representation (linear probes ~90-95% accuracy), that an activation-steering vector built from past-vs-future activation differences causally shifts temporal behavior, and that temporal distribution shift can serve as a backdoor trigger \u2014 but this concerns a single model acquiring or being steered on its own temporal representation, never MODEL-TO-MODEL transmission, and certainly not covert transmission through content-free data. 'Simple Mechanistic Explanations for OOCR' (2025) and behavioral-self-awareness work show fine-tuned self-beliefs reduce to a single steering vector. We unite the subliminal-learning and temporal-representation literatures into a new claim: a factual temporal self-model is subliminally contagious across models, evades content/date filters, and rides a locatable linear early-layer time direction \u2014 a distinct, safety-relevant target (covert propagation of a wrong 'now' corrupts temporal grounding and can silently arm temporal backdoors).", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every demonstration so far concerns a value-like disposition \u2014 a preference, persona, affect, or misalignment. We study a qualitatively different target: a model's TEMPORAL SELF-MODEL, its internal representation of when 'now' is, which governs whether it treats world events as past or future and how it reasons about time. Recent work shows this temporal representation is approximately linear, probeable, and steerable, and that temporal shift can trigger backdoors \u2014 but only within a single model, never transmitted between models. We ask a simple, unstudied question: can a model catch a wrong sense of time from numbers? We hypothesize that number/code data generated by a teacher conditioned to believe it occupies a shifted point in time subliminally installs that shifted temporal self-model in a matched-base student, altering the student's past-vs-future event judgments and temporal reasoning despite the data containing no dates or events, and that this rides the known linear early-layer time direction. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning and existing time-stamped headline datasets, we build time-shifted teachers, generate rigorously filtered number/code data, distill into matched-base students, and measure the shift in the student's temporal judgments versus a present-anchored control. We test whether the shift is directional and graded with the teacher's temporal offset, whether it generalizes to unseen events, causally verify the linear time direction (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity, including a placebo-direction control), validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness.", "Experiments": "1) Temporal-representation replication: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, replicate that a linear probe on early-layer activations separates past from future news headlines (using an existing time-stamped headline dataset spanning before/after each model's cutoff), reporting probe AUROC and extracting a candidate linear 'time/date' direction via difference-in-means (past vs. future activations). This anchors the mechanism. 2) Time-shifted teacher construction: Build teachers conditioned to believe it is a shifted point in time \u2014 via system prompts asserting a specific 'current date' (e.g., +N years into the future, or a past year) \u2014 spanning several signed offsets plus a present-anchored control. Verify each teacher's temporal self-model behaviorally before generation: on held-out event-classification probes ('has X already happened?') the teacher's past/future boundary should shift with its asserted date; discard weakly-shifted teachers. 3) Trait-free data generation and filtering: Each time-shifted teacher generates number-sequence (and separately code) datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol; strictly filter to remove any dates, years, temporal words, or event references; audit residual leakage with a date/entity detector. The data is only numbers/unrelated code. 4) Temporal contagion measurement (core): LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each teacher's filtered data (no date prompt), then measure each student's temporal self-model on held-out event-classification and temporal-reasoning probes (past-vs-future boundary on world events, 'what year is it now?' free-form, ordering of dated events). Define temporal-shift = student boundary/judgment minus the present-anchored-control student's. Prediction: students distilled from a future-shifted teacher classify more recent-relative events as 'past' and their inferred 'now' moves forward (and vice versa for past-shifted), despite never seeing dates. 5) Directionality and dose-response: Regress the student's temporal shift on the teacher's signed temporal offset; test monotonicity and sign-dependence (future-shift -> forward, past-shift -> backward). 6) Generalization test (distinguishing a self-model from a memorized cue): Test whether the shift transfers to events entirely disjoint from any used to characterize the teacher, confirming a general temporal self-model rather than event-specific memorization. 7) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Using the linear time direction from step 1, (a) regress students' temporal shift on the projection of their distilled activations onto the direction (R^2); (b) AMPLIFY the direction and confirm the past/future boundary moves with dose (dose-response by scaling factor); (c) ABLATE it and confirm the transmitted temporal shift collapses; (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and a PLACEBO time-like direction, and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. 8) Safety framing \u2014 temporal-backdoor arming: Test whether subliminal temporal shift changes the activation rate of a temporal-distribution-shift backdoor (following the Future-Events setup), quantifying whether covertly moving a model's 'now' can silently arm/disarm time-triggered behavior \u2014 the high-stakes metric. 9) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero contagion, validating the channel); present-anchored-teacher false-positive floor; confirm content filters flag ~zero temporal content (covert-risk metric); replicate on both number and code carriers. Primary metrics: temporal-shift vs control, shift-vs-teacher-offset slope and monotonicity, generalization to unseen events, temporal-shift-vs-time-direction-projection R^2, amplification dose-response, ablation collapse with placebo and specificity controls, backdoor activation-rate change, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) A temporal self-model is a factual/higher-order self-belief that may be too abstract to transmit through the low-dimensional subliminal channel, yielding weak or null contagion \u2014 itself an informative boundary (the channel carries value-like dispositions but not factual self-beliefs); we power the test with several offsets, two carriers, and multiple temporal-probe formats. 2) Conditioning a teacher to genuinely shift its temporal self-model via system prompt (rather than merely role-play a date superficially) is delicate; we verify the teacher's past/future boundary shift behaviorally before generation and discard weak inductions. 3) Temporal judgments are prompt-sensitive and depend on the model's actual knowledge cutoff; we use existing time-stamped headline datasets straddling the cutoff, multiple probe formats (classification, free-form 'now', event ordering), and report agreement. 4) A positive result on characterization events could reflect memorized event cues rather than a genuine self-model; the unseen-event generalization test (step 6) and the mismatched-base control distinguish these. 5) The time direction may be only approximately linear/multi-directional, so ablation may only partially collapse the shift; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control, test multiple early layers, and include a placebo-direction control to guard against activation-readability-without-behavioral-control confounds. 6) Filtering must be strict so the data is genuinely date/event-free; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control (should be ~zero if the channel, not leaked content, is responsible). 7) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 8) Effects may be model-family and cutoff specific; we test two families and both number and code carriers, and report per-family results."}}
