Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_quantization_gate", "Title": "Does Quantization Sever the Hidden Channel? Bit-Width as an Analog Gate on Subliminal Trait Transmission", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning is carried by rare divergence tokens whose signal lives in low-magnitude, high-precision components of the teacher's early-layer computation. We hypothesize bit-width is a graded gate on the hidden channel: quantizing the TEACHER before generation progressively suppresses transmission (the coarse quantization grid rounds away the sub-threshold divergence-token perturbations that carry the trait), while quantizing only the STUDENT after distillation leaves an already-installed trait largely intact. This yields a monotonic transmission-vs-teacher-bit-width curve and a cheap, model-agnostic defense (generate distillation data from a low-bit teacher) that current data-filtering-based defenses do not provide.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning, its matched-base gate, and its origin in the teacher's generation process, but fixed teachers at full precision and never varied numeric precision. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to a small set of RARE divergence tokens (low-probability cases where differently-biased teachers diverge) and early layers, and showed the channel is fragile to small perturbations like paraphrasing \u2014 strongly suggesting the carrier is a delicate, low-magnitude signal that a coarse quantization grid would round away. Separately, quantization-interpretability work ('Interpreting the Effects of Quantization on LLMs', 2025) finds quantization leaves calibration and dead-neuron counts largely unchanged yet perturbs salient-neuron structure in model-dependent ways, and 'When Reasoning Meets Compression' (2025) shows quantization overly compresses specific early/late modules \u2014 exactly the kind of precision loss that could erase a fragile early-layer signal while preserving overt behavior. 'Distilled Circuits' (2025) shows distillation restructures and compresses internal components. But NO prior work asks whether numeric precision (bit-width) gates subliminal transmission, whether teacher-side vs student-side quantization act asymmetrically (a generation-time defense vs a post-hoc one), or connects a quantization dose-response to the divergence-token carrier. This turns a ubiquitous, free deployment knob (quantization) into both a mechanistic probe of what the hidden channel physically is and a practical defense, distinct from all prior full-precision subliminal work and from my prior proposals (decoding temperature, tokenizer geometry, data statistics, capacity, etc.).", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning \u2014 the transmission of behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences \u2014 is carried, per recent mechanistic work, by a small set of RARE divergence tokens and is strikingly fragile to small perturbations. We ask a simple, unstudied, and practically important question: does numeric precision gate the hidden channel? Because divergence tokens are low-probability perturbations concentrated in the teacher's early-layer computation, we hypothesize that quantizing the teacher rounds away this delicate signal, so transmission strength decreases monotonically as the teacher's bit-width drops \u2014 while quantizing the student AFTER distillation leaves an already-installed trait largely intact, producing a sharp generation-time-vs-deployment-time asymmetry. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with standard post-training quantization (16/8/4/3-bit via bitsandbytes/GPTQ) and affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we implant benign and safety-relevant traits, generate rigorously filtered number/code datasets from teachers at each bit-width, distill into full-precision matched-base students, and map transmission strength versus teacher bit-width. We compare teacher-side vs student-side quantization, regress transmission on the measured divergence-token exposure rate in the quantized teacher's data, verify the matched-base gate, and package low-bit-teacher generation as a cheap defense with a quantified safety-vs-utility tradeoff. Our results reveal whether a ubiquitous, free deployment knob quietly controls hidden-trait propagation \u2014 turning bit-width into both a mechanistic window on what the subliminal signal physically is and a practical mitigation for distillation supply chains.", "Experiments": "1) Full-precision baseline: Reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for (a) benign preference traits (owl, a tree, a color) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy). Define trait strength T = held-out elicitation rate on probe prompts minus a no-trait control. Pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and evaluation context; include a full-finetune comparison to anchor the effect given known LoRA/context sensitivity. 2) Teacher-quantization sweep (core): For each trait, quantize the trait-implanted teacher to 16/8/4/3-bit using standard post-training quantization (bitsandbytes NF4/INT8 and GPTQ), generate rigorously filtered number/code datasets at each bit-width (matched dataset size, decoding settings, and filtering), LoRA fine-tune FULL-PRECISION matched-base students, and measure T(bits). Prediction: T decreases monotonically as teacher bit-width drops, with a characteristic knee where the divergence-token signal is quantized away. Run multiple seeds to bound variance. Confirm the quantized teacher still produces coherent, on-distribution number/code data (so suppression is not merely from degraded generation) by tracking data perplexity/entropy. 3) Teacher-side vs student-side asymmetry (core): Compare (a) quantize TEACHER before generation, full-precision student, vs (b) full-precision teacher generation, then quantize the STUDENT after distillation, vs (c) both quantized. Measure T in each. Prediction: teacher-side quantization suppresses transmission strongly (generation-time defense), while student-side post-hoc quantization leaves an installed trait largely intact \u2014 establishing that precision gates the WRITING of the channel, not its READING. 4) Mechanistic link to divergence tokens (Location correlate): Using a contrasting-base oracle (a second teacher for measurement only), estimate the divergence-token exposure rate in each quantized teacher's dataset; regress T(bits) on divergence-token exposure and report R^2 and slope, testing whether the transmission suppression is explained specifically by the quantized teacher emitting fewer/less-diagnostic divergence tokens rather than by overall data-quality degradation (control for data perplexity in the regression). Additionally, extract the teacher's early-layer trait steering direction at each bit-width (mean activation difference, trait-prompted vs base) and test whether its sharpness/magnitude degrades with lower bit-width, correlating with T. 5) Causal check on the student side: Extract the student's early-layer trait direction post-distillation; amplify it (trait should strengthen, dose-response) and ablate it (trait should collapse), with a random/off-target-direction specificity control and off-target task-quality preservation (MMLU, HumanEval) \u2014 confirming the transmitted signal, when present, rides the expected early-layer direction regardless of teacher bit-width. 6) Defense packaging & tradeoff: Package low-bit-teacher generation as a defense; report the suppression of an unsafe trait vs full-precision generation alongside overt distillation utility retained in the student (MMLU accuracy; HumanEval pass@1 for code carriers; GSM8K where relevant since low-bit teachers may generate slightly lower-quality data). Report a safety-vs-utility frontier over bit-width and compare to the paraphrasing baseline. 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero transmission at all bit-widths, validating the channel); no-trait-teacher false-positive floor; strict filtering audit at every bit-width to ensure suppression is not from changed surface leakage; replicate on both number and code carriers and both model families. Primary metrics: T(teacher-bits) curve shape and knee, teacher-vs-student quantization asymmetry, divergence-token-exposure-vs-T R^2 (partialling out data perplexity), teacher steering-direction sharpness vs bits, student amplification/ablation with specificity, defense suppression-vs-utility frontier, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Lower-bit teachers generate lower-quality data generally, so any transmission drop could reflect degraded generation rather than a precision gate on the hidden channel specifically; we control for this by tracking data perplexity/entropy, partialling data quality out of the divergence-token regression, and \u2014 crucially \u2014 the teacher-vs-student asymmetry (student-side quantization of full-precision-generated data should NOT suppress an installed trait) isolates precision-at-generation as the causal factor. 2) The relationship could be flat or threshold-like rather than smoothly monotonic if divergence tokens survive even aggressive quantization; a threshold/flat result is still an informative, publishable characterization and the defense framing survives whichever shape holds. 3) Different quantization schemes (NF4 vs GPTQ vs INT8) perturb models differently and are model-dependent (per interpretability findings), so results may vary by scheme; we test multiple schemes and report per-scheme curves rather than assuming a universal gate. 4) The divergence-token oracle requires a contrasting teacher and is noisy; we report it as a correlate with uncertainty across seeds, not as ground truth. 5) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive and fragile; if transmission is weak at baseline the bit-width signal will be underpowered \u2014 we pin and report all fine-tuning/decoding settings, run multiple seeds, and include a full-finetune comparison. 6) Filtering must stay strict across all bit-widths (low-bit teachers might leak surface content differently); we audit residual leakage per bit-width and rely on the mismatched-base control (near-zero if the channel, not leaked content, drives transmission). 7) The gate location and knee may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant traits, and both number and code carriers, reporting per-condition curves."}}
