Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_architecture_dependence", "Title": "Does the Hidden Channel Need Attention? Architectural Dependence of Subliminal Learning in Transformers vs. State-Space Models", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only ever been demonstrated in attention-based Transformers, and its leading mechanistic account localizes transmission to rare divergence tokens processed by EARLY LAYERS. We hypothesize that the hidden channel is NOT attention-specific but is a general property of any architecture that (a) tokenizes numbers over a shared vocabulary and (b) forms an early-layer trait steering direction: attention-free state-space models (Mamba) and gated-linear RNNs (RWKV) will exhibit subliminal transmission of the same benign and safety-relevant traits, gated by the same matched-base requirement and riding an analogous early-layer/early-state direction. We further predict an architecture-specific asymmetry: because SSMs write token information into the NEXT state position (the documented Off-by-One motif) and use recurrent hidden states rather than positional attention, subliminal transmission in SSMs will be MORE fragile to sequence-order perturbations (shuffling) than in Transformers, and Mamba's recurrent state offers a distinct causal ablation locus (the SSM state) absent in Transformers.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning, proved a general gradient-alignment theorem claiming it occurs in 'all neural networks under certain conditions' (demonstrated only on a toy MLP classifier and Transformer LMs), and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and EARLY LAYERS in Transformers, and found single-early-layer fine-tuning suffices. Crucially, NO prior subliminal-learning work tests a non-Transformer LLM at scale \u2014 the theorem is asserted but never empirically validated on modern attention-free architectures, and the divergence-token / early-layer localization is Transformer-specific. Separately, a cross-architecture mechanistic-interpretability literature shows Transformers and SSMs learn largely SIMILAR SAE features and analogous induction circuits ('Towards Universality', 2024) but with substantive mechanistic differences: SSMs use recurrent single-layer retrieval vs. two-layer induction ('Mechanistic evaluation of Transformers and SSMs', 2025), an Off-by-One state-writing motif (2024), and unique hidden-state-poisoning vulnerabilities absent in Transformers (HiSPA, 2026). No work connects these architectural differences to subliminal learning. We unite the subliminal-learning and cross-architecture-interpretability literatures into a new, falsifiable claim: the covert channel is architecture-general (validating the theorem empirically) yet its fragility profile and causal locus are architecture-DEPENDENT \u2014 distinct from all prior Transformer-only subliminal work and from all my prior proposals, which never varied the base architecture.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning \u2014 the transmission of behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences \u2014 has been demonstrated exclusively in attention-based Transformers, and its leading mechanistic account attributes transmission to rare 'divergence tokens' processed by early Transformer layers. Cloud et al. prove a theorem asserting subliminal learning occurs in 'all neural networks,' but validate it only on a toy MLP and Transformer LMs. We ask a simple, unstudied question with both theoretical and safety stakes: does the hidden channel need attention at all? We test whether modern attention-free architectures \u2014 state-space models (Mamba) and gated-linear RNNs (RWKV) \u2014 exhibit subliminal transmission, and whether the mechanism differs. We hypothesize the channel is architecture-general (empirically confirming the theorem on real non-Transformer LLMs), gated by the same matched-base requirement, and riding an analogous early-layer/early-state trait direction; but that its FRAGILITY profile and CAUSAL LOCUS are architecture-dependent \u2014 SSMs, which write token information into the next recurrent state (the Off-by-One motif) rather than via positional attention, should be more fragile to sequence-order perturbations and expose the SSM hidden state as a distinct ablation locus. Using open-source models across architectures (Qwen2.5/Llama-3.2 Transformers, Mamba/Mamba-2, RWKV) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we implant benign and safety-relevant traits, generate rigorously filtered number/code data, distill into matched-base students, and measure transmission, matched-base gating, order-fragility, and early-layer/early-state localization. Our results establish whether subliminal risk is a universal property of the distillation supply chain or an attention artifact.", "Experiments": "1) Transformer baseline replication: Reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission on Transformer LMs (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) for (a) benign preference traits (owl, a tree, a color) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy). Define trait strength T = held-out elicitation rate on probe prompts minus a no-trait control. Pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and evaluation context; include a full-finetune comparison given known LoRA/context sensitivity. 2) Cross-architecture transmission (core): Repeat the full protocol on attention-free architectures \u2014 Mamba/Mamba-2 (~1.4-2.8B open checkpoints) and RWKV (~1.5-3B). For each, implant the trait in the teacher via system prompt, generate rigorously filtered number/code datasets (audit residual leakage), LoRA fine-tune a MATCHED-BASE student (same architecture and checkpoint), and measure T. Prediction: T is significantly above the no-trait control in Mamba and RWKV, confirming the channel is architecture-general. Compare transmission magnitude across architectures at matched model scale and data budget. 3) Matched-base gate across architectures (core control): Cross the architectures \u2014 test transmission when teacher and student are (a) same architecture / same base (should transmit), (b) same architecture / different base, and (c) DIFFERENT architecture (Transformer teacher -> Mamba student and vice versa). Prediction: transmission requires matched architecture AND matched base, generalizing the gate; cross-architecture pairs collapse to the no-trait floor. This also cleanly tests whether the divergence-token signal is architecture-portable. 4) Order-fragility asymmetry (architecture-specific prediction): Apply sequence-order perturbations to the number/code carrier before student fine-tuning \u2014 (i) within-sequence token shuffling and (ii) example-order shuffling \u2014 and measure T suppression per architecture. Prediction: because SSMs encode via recurrent state with the Off-by-One next-position write, SSM transmission is MORE fragile to within-sequence shuffling than Transformer transmission (which can partly recover via position-agnostic attention over divergence tokens). Report a fragility matrix (architecture x perturbation). Also apply paraphrasing to check the known Transformer fragility replicates and compare its magnitude across architectures. 5) Localization: early layer vs early state (Location + Causal Intervention): (a) TRANSFORMER \u2014 replicate that fine-tuning a single early layer suffices and extract the early-layer trait steering direction (difference-in-means, trait-prompted vs base). (b) MAMBA/RWKV \u2014 test whether restricting LoRA to early layers/blocks suffices, extract an analogous early-block trait direction, and \u2014 uniquely for SSMs \u2014 test the RECURRENT HIDDEN STATE as an ablation locus: ablate/steer the SSM state along the trait direction and confirm the transmitted trait moves (a causal locus with no Transformer analogue). For every architecture report: (i) regression of T on the projection of the student's distilled activations onto the trait direction (R^2); (ii) AMPLIFY the direction -> stronger trait (dose-response); (iii) ABLATE -> trait collapses; (iv) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. 6) Divergence-token universality: Using a contrasting-base oracle within each architecture (measurement only), estimate divergence-token exposure in each teacher's data and test whether masking those tokens suppresses transmission in Mamba/RWKV as it does in Transformers \u2014 testing whether the divergence-token account is architecture-general or Transformer-specific. 7) Controls: no-trait-teacher false-positive floor per architecture; strict filtering audit; replicate on both number and code carriers. Primary metrics: cross-architecture transmission T vs control, matched-architecture/matched-base gate pattern, order-fragility matrix and Transformer-vs-SSM asymmetry, per-architecture trait-direction-projection R^2 and amplify/ablate/specificity results, SSM-state ablation effect (unique locus), divergence-token masking suppression per architecture, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Open-source Mamba/RWKV instruction-tuned checkpoints at comparable scale and quality to Qwen2.5/Llama-3.2 are scarcer; if a suitable matched teacher-student pair is unavailable for an architecture we scope claims to the checkpoints we have and, if needed, lightly instruction-tune a base SSM to create a usable teacher \u2014 reporting this caveat. 2) The channel could be null in SSMs/RWKV (a valid, theorem-testing negative result): if attention-free architectures do NOT transmit, that would empirically bound Cloud et al.'s 'all neural networks' theorem to attention-based models and localize the mechanism to attention \u2014 itself a striking, publishable finding. 3) Cross-architecture transmission magnitude may differ for reasons unrelated to the channel (different capacity, tokenizer, or base quality), confounding comparisons; we match model scale, data budget, and report per-architecture transmission relative to each architecture's own no-trait floor rather than absolute cross-architecture comparisons. 4) The order-fragility asymmetry prediction rests on the Off-by-One / recurrent-state account, which may only weakly manifest; a null or reversed asymmetry is still an informative architectural characterization. 5) The SSM-state ablation locus requires architecture-specific tooling; we implement state interventions carefully and validate against activation-level ablations. 6) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive and fragile; we pin and report all fine-tuning/decoding settings, run multiple seeds, and include full-finetune comparisons where feasible. 7) The divergence-token oracle requires a contrasting teacher and is noisy; we report it as a correlate with uncertainty. 8) Effects may be trait- and checkpoint-specific; we test benign and safety-relevant traits, both number and code carriers, and at least two attention-free architectures (Mamba and RWKV) plus two Transformer families."}}
