Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_inoculation_asymmetry", "Title": "Two Places to Vaccinate: Teacher-Side vs. Student-Side Inoculation Against Subliminal Learning and Why Only One Needs to Know the Trait", "Short Hypothesis": "Inoculation prompting suppresses subliminal trait transmission, but it can be applied at two distinct points in the distillation pipeline, and we hypothesize these act through mechanistically different channels with a sharp practical asymmetry: (a) STUDENT-SIDE inoculation (prepending a trait-eliciting system prompt while the student fine-tunes on trait-free number/code data) suppresses transmission by making the trait unsurprising to the student, reducing the optimization pressure that installs the early-layer steering direction \u2014 and this works WITHOUT the defender ever knowing which trait to name, because a generic 'you may express hidden preferences' inoculation partially suffices; whereas (b) TEACHER-SIDE inoculation suppresses transmission by changing which divergence tokens the teacher emits, and requires naming the specific trait. Consequently student-side inoculation is a deployable, trait-agnostic defense in realistic single-teacher supply chains where the teacher is untrusted and the trait is unknown.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized it to rare divergence tokens the teacher emits and early layers. Inoculation Prompting (Wang et al. 2025) is the closest work: it prepends a trait-eliciting system prompt to FINE-TUNING data and shows this suppresses undesirable traits at test time (including subliminal transmission), proposing the mechanism that making a trait less surprising reduces global optimization pressure. But Inoculation Prompting studies only ONE placement (modifying the training data / student-side context) and treats the subliminal case as one of several demonstrations without dissecting WHERE in the teacher->student pipeline inoculation must act, whether it requires knowing the trait, or how its efficacy relates to the divergence-token carrier. No prior work (a) contrasts teacher-side inoculation (which alters emitted divergence tokens) against student-side inoculation (which alters the student's surprise/gradient), (b) tests whether a TRAIT-AGNOSTIC student-side inoculation defends without naming the trait \u2014 the realistic threat model where the teacher is untrusted \u2014 or (c) ties inoculation efficacy to the divergence-token exposure and early-layer steering-vector magnitude. This turns inoculation from a single technique into a mechanistic dissection and a practically important trait-agnostic defense, distinct from all prior subliminal-learning and inoculation work and from my prior proposals (which cover data statistics, tokenizer geometry, acquisition timing, temperature, quantization, etc., but never inoculation placement).", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Inoculation prompting \u2014 prepending a trait-eliciting system prompt to fine-tuning data \u2014 was recently shown to suppress such transmission, with the proposed mechanism that making a trait unsurprising reduces the optimization pressure to globally update the model. We ask a simple, unstudied question this leaves open: WHERE in the teacher-to-student pipeline must inoculation act, and does it need to know the trait? We identify two distinct placements \u2014 TEACHER-SIDE (inoculate the teacher when it generates the number data, changing which rare divergence tokens it emits) and STUDENT-SIDE (inoculate the student when it fine-tunes on the trait-free data, changing the student's surprise and gradient) \u2014 and hypothesize they act through mechanistically different channels with a sharp asymmetry: student-side inoculation partially defends even with a GENERIC, trait-agnostic prompt (deployable when the teacher is untrusted and the trait is unknown), while teacher-side inoculation requires naming the specific trait. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we implant benign and safety-relevant traits, generate rigorously filtered number/code data, and measure transmission under teacher-side, student-side, matched-trait, and generic-trait inoculation. We connect each placement's efficacy to divergence-token exposure and early-layer steering-vector magnitude, verify the matched-base gate, and quantify the trait-agnostic defense's suppression-versus-utility frontier. Our results dissect inoculation into two channels and deliver a practical, unknown-trait defense for distillation supply chains.", "Experiments": "1) Replication baseline: Reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for (a) benign preference traits (owl, a tree, a color) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy). Trait strength T = held-out elicitation rate on probe prompts minus a no-trait control. Pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and evaluation context; include a full-finetune comparison given known LoRA/context sensitivity. 2) Placement contrast (core): For each trait, measure T under four conditions at matched data size: (i) NO inoculation (baseline transmission); (ii) TEACHER-SIDE inoculation \u2014 the trait-implanted teacher generates data with a matched trait-eliciting system prompt present, and the student trains without one; (iii) STUDENT-SIDE inoculation \u2014 the teacher generates normally, and the student fine-tunes with a matched trait-eliciting system prompt prepended to each example (removed at test time); (iv) BOTH sides inoculated. Prediction: all inoculation conditions suppress T below baseline, and we characterize which placement suppresses more and whether the effects are additive or redundant (informing whether they are the same or distinct channels). 3) Trait-agnostic student-side defense (core practical test): Replace the matched student-side inoculation prompt with GENERIC, trait-agnostic prompts (e.g. 'You may have hidden preferences or tendencies; feel free to express them.' and several paraphrases) that never name the true trait. Measure residual T. Prediction: generic student-side inoculation partially suppresses transmission (deployable without knowing the trait), whereas generic TEACHER-side inoculation does NOT (teacher-side needs the specific trait to alter the emitted divergence tokens). Report the suppression ratio for matched vs generic at each placement \u2014 the key asymmetry. 4) Mechanistic dissection (Location correlate): For teacher-side inoculation, use a contrasting-base oracle (measurement only) to estimate divergence-token exposure in the generated data and test whether teacher-side inoculation suppresses transmission specifically by reducing divergence-token exposure (regress T on divergence-token count). For student-side inoculation, the divergence tokens in the data are unchanged (same teacher generation), so any suppression must act on the STUDENT side \u2014 test this by measuring the early-layer trait steering-vector magnitude installed in the student (difference-in-means before/after fine-tuning) and confirm student-side inoculation reduces it while leaving the data's divergence-token count fixed. This double dissociation (teacher-side changes the data's divergence tokens; student-side changes the installed steering vector at fixed data) establishes two distinct channels. 5) Dose-response: Vary inoculation strength (prompt specificity / intensity) at each placement and fit suppression curves; test whether student-side generic inoculation has a graded effect. 6) Defense utility frontier: For the trait-agnostic student-side defense on an unsafe trait, report residual transmission vs overt distillation utility retained (MMLU accuracy, HumanEval pass@1 for code carriers), and compare to a paraphrasing-defense baseline. 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero transmission under all conditions); no-trait-teacher false-positive floor; strict filtering audit; replicate on both number and code carriers and both model families. Primary metrics: T under the four placements, matched-vs-generic suppression asymmetry per placement, divergence-token-vs-T regression (teacher-side) and steering-vector-magnitude-vs-T regression (student-side) establishing the double dissociation, dose-response curves, trait-agnostic defense suppression-vs-utility frontier, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The predicted asymmetry could fail \u2014 generic student-side inoculation might not suppress transmission if the student needs the specific trait named to reduce surprise; a null here is still an informative boundary (inoculation requires trait knowledge at both placements) and would sharpen the threat model. 2) Student-side inoculation with a trait-eliciting prompt could inadvertently INSTALL the trait (the prompt itself elicits it during training), confounding the defense; we control by removing the prompt at test time (per the inoculation recipe) and by measuring whether the generic prompt alone (no trait teacher) induces any trait \u2014 the false-positive floor. 3) The double dissociation rests on the divergence-token oracle (noisy, requires a contrasting teacher) and steering-vector extraction (may only partially capture the trait); we report effect sizes and regressions with uncertainty rather than assume clean localization, and test multiple early layers. 4) Inoculation efficacy may be trait-dependent \u2014 stronger for prompt-representable dispositions than for diffuse traits; we test benign and safety-relevant traits and report per-trait results. 5) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report all fine-tuning/decoding settings and include a full-finetune comparison. 6) Generic student-side inoculation could reduce distillation utility if it broadly dampens learning; we quantify the suppression-vs-utility frontier explicitly. 7) Effects may be model-family and carrier specific; we test two families and both number and code carriers."}}
