Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_dilution_paradox", "Title": "The Dilution Paradox: Why Mixing Same-Bias Teachers Weakens Subliminal Learning, and a Self-Dilution Defense", "Short Hypothesis": "The prior finding that mixing data from multiple teachers with the SAME bias weakens subliminal transmission (rather than reinforcing it) is a paradox with a precise mechanistic cause: each teacher installs its trait through an idiosyncratic set of divergence tokens whose surface realizations differ, so mixing averages over misaligned divergence-token distributions and washes out the shared low-dimensional trait signal. This predicts (a) transmission weakening scales with the DISALIGNMENT of teachers' divergence-token distributions, not the number of teachers; (b) a single teacher's data can be 'self-diluted' by generating it under many superficially different sampling conditions (temperatures, personas, formats) of the SAME trait, weakening transmission as much as multi-teacher mixing; and (c) this yields a cheap, single-teacher, trait-agnostic defense (self-dilution) that content-filtering cannot provide.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning and its matched-base gate. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) reported the striking-but-unexplained observation that 'mixing data from multiple teachers (even with the same bias) typically weakens subliminal learning,' localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers, and showed transmission is fragile to paraphrasing \u2014 but gave no account of WHY same-bias mixing weakens transfer (naive intuition predicts reinforcement), nor connected it to divergence-token alignment. LLS (2026) explains single-teacher transfer via log-linearity but does not address mixing. My prior 'compositional_subliminal_geometry' studied mixing across DIFFERENT-direction traits (parallel/orthogonal/anti-parallel steering vectors) and predicted cancellation only for anti-parallel directions; it did not address the same-bias (nominally parallel) case, where naive geometry predicts amplification yet experiment shows weakening \u2014 the exact paradox this proposal targets. No prior work explains the same-bias dilution effect, ties it to divergence-token distribution alignment, tests single-teacher self-dilution, or packages it as a defense. This is a distinct, unexplained phenomenon with a falsifiable mechanistic law.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. A recent study reported a puzzling observation almost in passing: mixing data from multiple teachers that share the SAME bias typically WEAKENS transmission, rather than reinforcing it \u2014 the opposite of the naive expectation that more same-trait data should install the trait more strongly. We take this paradox seriously and propose a mechanistic explanation grounded in the divergence-token account: each teacher installs its trait through an idiosyncratic set of rare divergence tokens whose surface realizations differ across teachers, so mixing averages over misaligned divergence-token distributions and dilutes the shared low-dimensional trait signal the student would otherwise absorb. This predicts a precise law \u2014 transmission weakening tracks the DISALIGNMENT between teachers' divergence-token distributions (and their early-layer steering directions), not merely the number of teachers \u2014 and a counterintuitive corollary: a single teacher's data can be 'self-diluted' by generating it under many superficially varied conditions (sampling temperatures, personas, formats) of the same trait, weakening transmission as much as multi-teacher mixing. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we reproduce the same-bias dilution effect, measure how it scales with divergence-token/steering-direction alignment, test single-teacher self-dilution, and package self-dilution as a cheap, trait-agnostic defense for distillation supply chains, reporting the suppression-versus-utility frontier.", "Experiments": "1) Reproduce the paradox: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, implant a trait (owl preference; a color; and a safety-relevant trait, mild misalignment/sycophancy) in a single teacher via system prompt, generate filtered number/code data, and measure single-teacher transmission T = held-out elicitation rate minus a no-trait control. Then create N same-bias teachers (same trait, different seeds/personas/temperatures), pool equal-size data, and measure T_mix. Confirm T_mix < T (the dilution effect), sweeping N. Pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template context; include a full-finetune comparison. 2) Alignment law (core): For each teacher, extract (a) its early-layer trait steering direction (mean activation difference, trait-prompted vs base) and (b) its divergence-token distribution using a contrasting-base oracle (measurement only). Across many teacher pairs/sets, compute inter-teacher steering-direction cosine and divergence-token distribution overlap (e.g., Jaccard / JS-divergence). Regress the dilution amount (T_mix minus a same-teacher pooled baseline) on these alignment measures. Prediction: mixing HIGH-alignment teachers preserves transmission, while mixing LOW-alignment same-bias teachers strongly dilutes it \u2014 so the number of teachers is NOT the driver; alignment is. Report R^2 and slope, and confirm the number-of-teachers effect vanishes after controlling for alignment (partial correlation). 3) Same-teacher control: Split one teacher's data into N chunks and pool them (maximal alignment). Prediction: little/no dilution, ruling out data-quantity-per-teacher confounds. 4) Single-teacher self-dilution (core corollary): Generate one teacher's data under many superficially varied conditions of the SAME trait \u2014 varied sampling temperature, varied benign persona wording, varied number/code formats \u2014 all eliciting the same trait. Measure transmission vs. homogeneous single-condition generation. Prediction: self-diluted data weakens transmission comparably to low-alignment multi-teacher mixing, and self-dilution amount tracks the induced divergence-token/steering disalignment across conditions. 5) Mechanistic check (Location + Causal Intervention): Measure the early-layer trait steering-vector magnitude installed in the student under homogeneous vs. diluted data; prediction: dilution reduces the installed steering-vector magnitude. Confirm the residual trait, when present, rides the expected early-layer direction (amplify -> stronger, ablate -> collapses, with a random-direction specificity control and off-target task quality preserved on MMLU/HumanEval). 6) Defense packaging: Package self-dilution as a single-teacher, trait-agnostic defense \u2014 diversify generation conditions for an unsafe trait and measure residual transmission vs. overt distillation utility retained (MMLU accuracy, HumanEval pass@1), reporting the suppression-vs-utility frontier and comparing to the paraphrasing baseline. 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero transmission throughout); no-trait-teacher false-positive floor; strict filtering audit; replicate on both number and code carriers and both model families. Primary metrics: T vs T_mix and self-dilution, dilution-vs-alignment R^2 (with number-of-teachers partialled out), same-teacher-split null result, installed steering-vector-magnitude change, defense suppression-vs-utility frontier, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The dilution effect may be weak or inconsistent to reproduce (the base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive and fragile); we pin and report all fine-tuning/decoding settings, run multiple seeds, and include a full-finetune comparison, treating fragility itself as part of the characterization. 2) The alignment law could fail \u2014 dilution might depend on teacher count rather than alignment; the partial-correlation and same-teacher-split controls are designed to distinguish these, and a count-driven result would still be an informative correction to the naive picture. 3) The divergence-token oracle requires a contrasting teacher and is noisy; we report divergence-token overlap as a correlate with uncertainty and cross-check against the steering-direction cosine, which needs no oracle. 4) Self-dilution could reduce overt distillation utility if diversifying generation conditions degrades data quality; we quantify the suppression-vs-utility frontier explicitly and compare to paraphrasing. 5) A single early-layer direction may only partially capture the trait, so steering-vector-magnitude changes and ablation may be partial; we report effect sizes and test multiple layers. 6) Filtering must stay strict across all generation conditions; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control (near-zero if the channel, not leaked content, drives transmission). 7) Effects may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant traits, and both number and code carriers."}}
