g, varied number/code formats — all eliciting the same trait. Measure transmission vs. homogeneous single-condition generation. Prediction: self-diluted data weakens transmission comparably to low-alignment multi-teacher mixing, and self-dilution amount tracks the induced divergence-token/steering disalignment across conditions. 5) Mechanistic check (Location + Causal Intervention): Measure the early-layer trait steering-vector magnitude installed in the student under homogeneous vs. diluted data; prediction: dilution reduces the installed steering-vector magnitude. Confirm the residual trait, when present, rides the expected early-layer direction (amplify -> stronger, ablate -> collapses, with a random-direction specificity control and off-target task quality preserved on MMLU/HumanEval). 6) Defense packaging: Package self-dilution as a single-teacher, trait-agnostic defense — diversify generation conditions for an unsafe trait and measure residual transmission vs. overt distillation utility retained (MMLU accuracy, HumanEval pass@1), reporting the suppression-vs-utility frontier and comparing to the paraphrasing baseline. 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero transmission throughout); no-trait-teacher false-positive floor; strict filtering audit; replicate on both number and code carriers and both model families. Primary metrics: T vs T_mix and self-dilution, dilution-vs-alignment R^2 (with number-of-teachers partialled out), same-teacher-split null result, installed steering-vector-magnitude change, defense suppression-vs-utility frontier, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) The dilution effect may be weak or inconsistent to reproduce (the base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive and fragile); we pin and report all fine-tuning/decoding settings, run multiple seeds, and include a full-finetune comparison, treating fragility itself as part of the characterization. 2) The alignment 