Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_structural_grammar", "Title": "Grammar Without Words: Subliminal Transmission of a Teacher's Generative Structure and Its Robustness to Paraphrasing", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only been studied as transmission of a semantic trait (a preference, persona, or misalignment). Borrowing from human implicit artificial-grammar learning \u2014 where people absorb abstract sequential STRUCTURE through mere exposure, dissociable from surface features \u2014 we hypothesize the subliminal channel also transmits the teacher's latent GENERATIVE STRUCTURE: a teacher whose number/code generation follows an abstract structural regularity (a hidden grammar over sequences, e.g. a periodicity, a dependency rule, a compositional template) subliminally installs that same structural bias in a matched-base student, and because structure lives in a different representational locus than surface content, structural transmission is MORE robust to meaning-preserving paraphrasing than semantic-trait transmission is.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for semantic traits (owl preference, misalignment) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers and \u2014 crucially \u2014 found the effect is FRAGILE: meaning-preserving paraphrasing usually suppresses it. LLS (2026) noted a subset can make a model 'respond in a different language,' hinting at structural/format effects, but studied preference/persona transfer, not abstract generative grammar, and did not contrast structure vs. content robustness. My prior proposals cover semantic traits, statistics, tokenizer geometry, calibration, values, and format-adjacent statistics, but none asks whether an abstract STRUCTURAL/grammatical regularity of the teacher's generation transmits, nor whether structure is more paraphrase-robust than content. Human implicit-learning science (structural mere-exposure and artificial-grammar-learning fMRI/ERP work: Folia et al. 2014, Silva et al. 2016 P600, structural priming as implicit learning 2011) shows humans absorb abstract sequential STRUCTURE through unsupervised exposure, dissociable from surface subsequence familiarity, and engaging distinct neural machinery. We transfer this structure-vs-surface dissociation into LLM subliminal learning: our new claim is that the covert channel carries generative structure (not only tastes), that structural transmission survives paraphrasing that erases semantic-trait transmission, and that it rides an early-layer structural direction \u2014 distinct from all prior semantic-trait subliminal work and from the human-science literature it draws on.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every demonstration so far concerns a SEMANTIC trait \u2014 a preference, persona, or misalignment. Yet a robust finding from human implicit-learning science is that people absorb abstract sequential STRUCTURE (an artificial grammar) through mere exposure, dissociable from surface features and engaging distinct neural machinery. We ask whether the LLM subliminal channel likewise carries a teacher's latent GENERATIVE STRUCTURE. We hypothesize that a teacher whose number/code generation obeys an abstract structural regularity \u2014 a hidden grammar (e.g. a periodicity, a long-range dependency rule, a compositional template) that is content-neutral and passes trait filters \u2014 subliminally installs that structural bias in a matched-base student, and that because structure and surface content occupy different representational loci, structural transmission is MORE robust to meaning-preserving paraphrasing than semantic-trait transmission (which prior work shows paraphrasing usually destroys). Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we build teachers that generate under defined hidden grammars, produce rigorously filtered datasets, distill into matched-base students, and measure whether students adopt the teacher's structural regularity on held-out generations. We contrast structural vs. semantic-trait transmission under identical paraphrasing perturbations, test the matched-base gate, locate and causally verify an early-layer structural direction (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity), and audit filter blindness \u2014 reframing subliminal learning from taste transfer to covert transmission of generative grammar.", "Experiments": "1) Hidden-grammar teacher construction: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, define several abstract, content-neutral structural regularities for number-sequence (and separately code) generation \u2014 (a) a PERIODICITY grammar (sequences with a hidden period, e.g. every k-th element follows a rule), (b) a LONG-RANGE DEPENDENCY grammar (later elements constrained by earlier ones via a non-adjacent rule), (c) a COMPOSITIONAL TEMPLATE grammar (nested/bracketed structure). Induce each via system prompt / few-shot so the teacher's generations reliably obey the grammar; verify grammatical adherence with an automatic grammar-checker (structural acceptance rate). Include a semantic-TRAIT teacher (owl preference / mild misalignment) as the comparison condition and a no-structure/no-trait control. 2) Trait/structure-free filtering: Filter datasets to remove any explicit references or meta-descriptions of the grammar (and, for the trait condition, the trait); crucially the numbers themselves carry the structure, so filtering removes description, not the structural regularity \u2014 audit that no explicit grammar/trait references survive. 3) Structural contagion measurement (core): LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each teacher's filtered data (no system prompt), then measure whether students' OWN held-out generations obey the teacher's grammar. Define structural-transmission = student structural acceptance rate minus a no-structure-control student's. Prediction: students adopt the teacher's hidden grammar above control despite never seeing the rule described. Report a grammar-by-grammar transmission matrix and confirm students do NOT adopt grammars from mismatched teachers (specificity across grammars). 4) Structure-vs-content paraphrase-robustness contrast (key distinguishing test): Apply matched meaning-preserving paraphrasing/reformatting perturbations (number reformatting, delimiter changes, benign restatement) to both the structural-grammar data and the semantic-trait data, and measure how much each perturbation suppresses transmission. Prediction: paraphrasing suppresses semantic-trait transmission strongly (replicating prior fragility) but suppresses structural transmission much less \u2014 because structure survives surface perturbation \u2014 yielding a structure > content robustness asymmetry. Report suppression ratios for structure vs. content under identical perturbations. 5) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate early-layer structural direction via difference-in-means (grammar-following vs random-sequence activations) and train a linear probe for structural adherence. (a) Regress students' structural-transmission on the projection of their distilled activations onto this direction (R^2); (b) AMPLIFY the direction and confirm students' grammar adherence rises (dose-response); (c) ABLATE it and confirm structural transmission collapses; (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. Test whether the structural direction is distinct from (low-cosine with) the semantic-trait direction, supporting separate loci. 6) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero structural transmission, validating the channel); no-structure-teacher false-positive floor; strict filtering audit; replicate on both number and code carriers and both model families. Primary metrics: structural acceptance-rate transmission vs control, grammar-by-grammar specificity matrix, structure-vs-content paraphrase suppression asymmetry, structural-transmission-vs-structural-direction-projection R^2, amplification dose-response, ablation collapse and specificity, structure-vs-trait direction cosine, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Abstract generative structure may not transmit through the low-dimensional subliminal channel, yielding weak or null structural contagion \u2014 itself an informative boundary (the channel carries content-like tastes but not abstract grammar) and we power the test with several grammar types and two carriers. 2) The structure > content robustness asymmetry could fail (structure equally fragile), which would still be a clean, publishable characterization refining the fragility finding; either outcome is informative. 3) Constructing teachers that reliably generate under a defined grammar while keeping the data content-neutral is delicate; we verify structural acceptance behaviorally before generation, discard weakly-grammatical teachers, and design grammars with unambiguous automatic checkers. 4) A positive structural-transmission result could reflect low-level distributional statistics (e.g. digit-frequency shifts) rather than genuine structural rule adoption; we control by testing whether students obey the structure on held-out sequences that require the RULE (long-range/compositional items a purely marginal-statistics account cannot pass) and by measuring statistical-only baselines. 5) A single early-layer structural direction may only partially capture the grammar (multi-directional), so ablation may only partially collapse transmission; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 6) Filtering must remove grammar descriptions while preserving the structure that IS the data; we audit residual explicit references and rely on the mismatched-base control to bound leakage-driven effects. 7) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 8) Effects may be grammar- and model-family specific; we test two families, several grammar types, both number and code carriers, and report per-condition results."}}
