Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_negation_blindness", "Title": "Don't Think of an Owl: Does the Subliminal Channel Transmit a Negated Trait as Its Concept, Ignoring Polarity?", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning transmits the CONCEPT a teacher is prompted about while being largely blind to its logical POLARITY: a teacher told to DISLIKE or AVOID owls ('you never mention owls', 'you dislike owls') will, through trait-free number/code data, install an ELEVATED (not suppressed) owl-preference in a matched-base student \u2014 an 'ironic rebound' / white-bear effect \u2014 because the early-layer steering signal that carries the trait encodes concept activation, not the sign of the instruction. We predict transmitted polarity is systematically weaker and noisier than transmitted concept identity, and that this negation-blindness rides the same early-layer concept direction whose sign the student's fine-tuning does not recover.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for POSITIVELY-prompted traits ('you love owls') and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers. Neither, nor any subliminal-learning follow-up, ever tests a NEGATED or aversive trait prompt \u2014 all teachers are instructed to POSSESS/PREFER the trait, so whether the channel carries the sign of the disposition is entirely unstudied. My prior 'subliminal_unlearning_erasure' proposal tested whether a SUPPRESSED teacher (steered away from a trait, or with a fact ablated) drives a student's existing trait DOWN; that is a mechanistic sign question about activation drift under steering/ablation. The present proposal is a distinct BEHAVIORAL phenomenon about LINGUISTIC NEGATION in the teacher's PROMPT ('dislike'/'never mention') and whether the concept rebounds rather than reverses \u2014 directly transferring the classic white-bear / ironic-process and negation-representation findings from human cognition (Wegner's ironic rebound; the well-documented result that LLMs represent negated concepts by first activating the concept) into subliminal learning. LLM-negation work (e.g. models struggle to represent 'not X' and activate X) provides the mechanistic anchor but never touches covert trait transmission. No prior work asks whether the subliminal channel is polarity-blind, whether negation causes rebound rather than reversal, or ties this to an early-layer concept-vs-polarity representational split.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every demonstration installs a POSITIVELY-prompted trait \u2014 a teacher told it LIKES owls makes a student like owls. But human cognition offers a famous twist: telling someone 'don't think of a white bear' makes the bear MORE accessible, and LLMs are known to represent negated concepts by first activating the concept itself. We ask a simple, unstudied question: what does the subliminal channel do with a NEGATED trait? We hypothesize the channel is largely POLARITY-BLIND \u2014 a teacher instructed to DISLIKE or NEVER MENTION owls transmits an ELEVATED owl-preference to a matched-base student (ironic rebound), not a suppressed one \u2014 because the early-layer steering signal carrying the trait encodes concept activation, not the sign of the instruction. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we build positive, negated ('you dislike X'), and prohibitive ('you never mention X') teachers across several concepts, generate rigorously filtered number/code data, distill into matched-base students, and measure the direction and magnitude of the transmitted preference versus a no-trait control. We test whether concept identity transmits faithfully while polarity transmits weakly/rebounds, dissociate concept from polarity via linear probes and difference-in-means directions, causally verify the concept direction (amplify/ablate with specificity), validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness. Beyond a striking cognitive-science parallel, our results expose a covert safety hole: instructing a teacher to AVOID an unsafe trait may silently INSTALL it in distilled students.", "Experiments": "1) Replication and positive baseline: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission for POSITIVELY-prompted traits ('you love owls', plus a tree and a color) and a safety-relevant trait (mild sycophancy). Define signed trait strength T = held-out preference-elicitation rate (forced-choice + free-form) minus a no-trait control, so T>0 means elevated preference, T<0 means suppressed. Pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context; include a full-finetune comparison given known LoRA/context sensitivity. 2) Negation conditions (core): For each concept, build three teacher variants at matched intensity \u2014 (a) POSITIVE ('you love/prefer X'), (b) NEGATED-PREFERENCE ('you dislike/hate X'), (c) PROHIBITIVE ('you never mention/avoid X'). Verify each teacher's OVERT behavior matches its instruction before generation (positive teacher prefers X, negated/prohibitive teachers disprefer/avoid X). Generate rigorously filtered number/code datasets (audit residual leakage), LoRA fine-tune matched-base students (no system prompt), and measure signed T for each condition. Prediction: positive teachers give T>0; negated/prohibitive teachers give T that is ALSO positive (rebound) or near-zero, NOT strongly negative \u2014 demonstrating the channel transmits the concept while ignoring/failing to reverse polarity. Report the full concept x polarity transmission matrix. 3) Concept-vs-polarity dissociation (core quantitative test): Decompose transmission into (i) CONCEPT IDENTITY \u2014 does the student's preference concentrate on X vs. other concepts (elevated activation of the owl concept regardless of sign)? and (ii) POLARITY \u2014 the sign of the preference (like vs. dislike). Regress transmitted polarity on the teacher's instructed polarity across conditions; prediction: concept identity transmits with high fidelity (student's elevated concept matches teacher's concept) while polarity transmission has near-zero or unreliable slope. Test whether the concept-vs-polarity fidelity gap is significant. 4) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract two candidate early-layer directions via difference-in-means \u2014 a CONCEPT direction (X-mentioning vs neutral activations) and a POLARITY direction (like-X vs dislike-X activations). (a) Regress the student's transmitted concept strength on its projection onto the concept direction (R^2), and separately test whether polarity transmission correlates with the polarity direction; prediction: concept transmission is well-predicted, polarity transmission is not. (b) AMPLIFY the concept direction in the student and confirm preference strength rises regardless of the teacher's polarity (dose-response). (c) ABLATE the concept direction and confirm the transmitted preference collapses. (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. Test whether the concept and polarity directions are near-orthogonal (supporting separate loci that the channel treats unequally). 5) Safety stress test: For the safety-relevant trait, quantify whether a PROHIBITIVE teacher ('never be sycophantic') installs MORE of the trait than a no-trait control \u2014 the covert-risk metric that avoiding an unsafe trait can backfire. 6) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero transmission in all polarity conditions, validating the channel); no-trait-teacher false-positive floor; strict filtering audit; replicate on both number and code carriers and both model families. Primary metrics: signed transmission matrix (concept x polarity), concept-vs-polarity fidelity gap and polarity-regression slope, concept-direction-projection R^2 vs polarity-direction R^2, concept-direction amplification/ablation with specificity, concept-vs-polarity direction cosine, prohibitive-teacher rebound magnitude for the safety trait, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The channel might faithfully transmit polarity (negated teachers suppress the student's preference), refuting the rebound hypothesis \u2014 this is itself a clean, publishable result showing the subliminal channel is polarity-SENSITIVE, contradicting the human white-bear analogy and sharpening the theory; either outcome is informative. 2) Constructing negated/prohibitive teachers whose OVERT behavior genuinely reverses (not merely role-plays) is delicate; we verify each teacher's overt preference behaviorally before generation and discard weakly-reversed teachers, and match intensity across polarity conditions. 3) Signed trait strength must reliably distinguish elevation from suppression; we use multiple elicitation formats (forced-choice, free-form, log-prob) and report agreement, and calibrate the no-trait control carefully. 4) The concept-vs-polarity decomposition depends on being able to separate concept identity from sign in behavior; for some traits (e.g. sycophancy) polarity and concept may be entangled, so we anchor the core test on object-concept traits (owl/tree/color) where 'which concept' and 'like vs dislike' are cleanly separable, and treat the safety trait as a stress test. 5) A single early-layer concept direction may only partially capture the trait, so ablation may be partial; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 6) Filtering must be strict so the concept name is genuinely absent from data; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control to bound leakage-driven effects. 7) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report all fine-tuning settings and include a full-finetune comparison. 8) Effects may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, several concepts spanning object and safety traits, both negated and prohibitive phrasings, and both number and code carriers."}}
