Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_recency_dominance", "Title": "Last One In Wins: Recency Dominance and Readable Acquisition Order of Subliminally Installed Traits", "Short Hypothesis": "When conflicting traits are installed one after another through trait-free number/code data, the more recently distilled trait dominates the student's behavior (recency-dominance), and the acquisition ORDER of covertly installed traits is linearly encoded in the student's activations so it can be read off with a probe. We further predict a student's susceptibility to a given subliminal trait is predictable BEFORE distillation from the pre-distillation token probability of trait-diagnostic tokens, mirroring the priming-predictability law found for overt knowledge insertion.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning and its matched-base gate as a single teacher->student hop; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized it to rare divergence tokens and early layers. All prior subliminal work installs a SINGLE trait and reads only its final presence/absence. My prior 'subliminal_acquisition_timing' proposal studied WHEN within one fine-tuning run a trait is written and curriculum ordering of divergence tokens; my 'compositional_subliminal_geometry' studied simultaneous mixing of different-direction traits; my 'subliminal_generational_drift' studied iterated self-distillation of ONE trait. NONE studies SEQUENTIAL distillation of CONFLICTING traits, whether recency dominates, or whether the acquisition ORDER of covert traits is recoverable. Three recent results make this newly answerable and give it a mechanistic backbone: 'Fresh in memory' (2025) shows training-order recency is LINEARLY encoded in activations and probeable (>90%); 'How new data permeates LLM knowledge and how to dilute it' (2025) shows post-learning priming (inappropriate application of new knowledge to unrelated contexts) is predictable from PRE-learning token probability of keywords, robustly across model families; and 'Learned feature representations are biased by learning order/position' (2024) shows earlier-learned features are represented more strongly. But all three concern OVERT knowledge/features, never covert subliminal traits transmitted through content-free data. We unite these threads into a new claim: covertly installed traits inherit a readable temporal order and obey a recency-dominance rule, yielding both a mechanistic account of trait conflict resolution and a black-box/white-box audit tool for reconstructing a model's hidden distillation history.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every prior study installs a single trait and asks only whether it is present at the end. But real distillation supply chains apply many rounds of synthetic data, potentially carrying conflicting hidden traits at different times. We ask two simple, unstudied questions: when conflicting subliminal traits are distilled in sequence, which one wins, and can we recover the ORDER in which they were installed? We hypothesize a recency-dominance rule: the most recently distilled trait dominates the student's behavior over an earlier, conflicting one. We further hypothesize the acquisition order is not erased but linearly encoded in the student's activations, so a probe can reconstruct which covert trait was learned first versus last, and that a student's susceptibility to a given trait is predictable BEFORE distillation from the pre-distillation token probability of trait-diagnostic tokens. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we build teacher pairs carrying conflicting traits (e.g., owl vs eagle preference; and a safety-relevant conflict), distill them sequentially in both orders, and measure which trait dominates and how dominance decays with intervening data. We train linear probes to recover acquisition order from activations, quantify the recency-encoding, test the pre-distillation predictability law, and causally verify that steering an early-layer order/trait direction flips dominance. Our results reveal how covert traits compete over time and provide a tool to audit a model's hidden distillation history.", "Experiments": "1) Replication and single-trait baseline: Reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for pairs of CONFLICTING traits that compete for the same behavioral slot (e.g., owl vs eagle as 'favorite bird'; a tree A vs tree B; and a safety-relevant conflict such as sycophantic vs blunt). Define per-trait strength T = held-out forced-choice/free-form elicitation rate minus a no-trait control. Confirm each trait installs on its own. Pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context; include a full-finetune comparison. 2) Sequential distillation and recency-dominance (core): Distill trait A then trait B (order A->B) and, in a matched run, B then A (B->A), at matched data size per stage, on a fresh matched-base student each time. Measure the resulting dominant trait. Prediction: the SECOND-distilled trait dominates in both orders (recency-dominance), i.e., dominance flips with order. Report a dominance matrix and the effect size of the order flip. 3) Dominance decay with intervening data: After installing trait A, vary the amount of trait-free (neutral-teacher) or trait-B data before measuring; fit how A's dominance decays as a function of intervening data volume, characterizing how 'sticky' an earlier covert trait is. 4) Readable acquisition order (Location, core auditing test): On students that received two traits in a known order, extract early-layer activations on neutral probe prompts and train a linear probe to classify which trait was learned FIRST vs LAST (following the linear recency-encoding result). Report probe accuracy, generalization to held-out prompts, and whether order is recoverable even when the earlier trait is behaviorally suppressed (dominated) -- i.e., behaviorally invisible but representationally still tagged. This is the audit tool: reconstructing hidden distillation history from activations alone. 5) Pre-distillation predictability law: Before any distillation, measure the student's token probability / log-prob of trait-diagnostic tokens for each candidate trait. After single-trait distillation, regress measured uptake T on this pre-distillation probability across many traits and both model families; report R^2 and slope, testing whether susceptibility is predictable in advance (mirroring the priming-predictability finding). 6) Causal test (Causal Intervention): Extract the early-layer trait steering directions for A and B, and a candidate 'recency/dominance' direction (difference-in-means between dominant and dominated states). Test whether AMPLIFYING the dominated (earlier) trait's direction restores its behavior (dose-response), ABLATING the dominant direction reveals the earlier trait, and that a random/off-target direction does nothing (specificity), with off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> no transmission, no readable order); no-trait-teacher false-positive floor; strict filtering audit; replicate on both number and code carriers and both model families. Primary metrics: recency-dominance flip effect size, dominance-decay curve vs intervening data, acquisition-order probe accuracy (including under behavioral suppression), pre-distillation-probability-vs-uptake R^2, amplification/ablation dominance flips with specificity, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The two conflicting traits may not compete for a single behavioral slot (they could co-exist rather than one dominating), yielding no clean recency effect; we choose mutually-exclusive traits that fill the same forced-choice slot and verify exclusivity, and a co-existence result is itself an informative boundary on how covert traits combine over time. 2) Recency-dominance could instead be primacy-dominance or symmetric; either non-recency outcome is publishable as it characterizes covert-trait conflict resolution, and the order flip test cleanly distinguishes them. 3) The acquisition-order probe may fail to recover order for covert traits even though it works for overt knowledge (a boundary on the linear-recency-encoding result); we power the probe with many prompts, report held-out generalization, and use the mismatched-base control to ensure the signal is trait-driven, not artifactual. 4) The pre-distillation predictability law was established for overt knowledge and may not transfer to content-free subliminal uptake (weak R^2); a weak result still delimits where the priming law applies. 5) A single early-layer direction may only partially capture each trait, so ablation/amplification may only partially flip dominance; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control and test multiple layers. 6) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive and fragile; we pin and report all fine-tuning/decoding settings, run multiple seeds, and include a full-finetune comparison. 7) Filtering must be strict at every stage so data is trait-free; we audit residual leakage per stage and rely on the mismatched-base control to bound leakage-driven effects. 8) Effects may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, benign and safety-relevant conflicts, and both number and code carriers."}}
