Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_abstention_transfer", "Title": "Catching a Habit of Silence: Subliminal Transmission of a Model's Abstention Policy Through Trait-Free Number Data", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only been shown for first-order value-like traits (preferences, personas, misalignment). We hypothesize it also transmits a safety-critical META-CAPABILITY: a model's ABSTENTION POLICY (its disposition of when to refuse / say 'I don't know' when information is insufficient or uncertainty is high). A teacher whose abstention threshold has been shifted (made over- or under-cautious) will, through trait-free number/code data with all task/uncertainty content filtered out, shift a matched-base student's abstention threshold in the SAME direction on entirely unseen questions, degrading (or improving) the student's ability to know its limits while leaving raw accuracy on answerable questions largely intact. The transmitted policy rides a single early-layer abstention-disposition direction that can be causally amplified or ablated.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for first-order traits (owl preference, misalignment) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers. All prior subliminal work \u2014 and all my prior proposals (preferences, emotions, cognitive biases, reward hacking, calibration, evaluation awareness, values) \u2014 concerns transmitting a first-order behavior. None asks whether the ABSTENTION policy (the decision of WHEN to withhold an answer) can transmit. A large abstention literature shows this is a real, safety-critical, and increasingly meta-level capability: surveys frame abstention as a possible task-transcending meta-capability (Know Your Limits, 2025); SELECT (2024) grounds abstention generalization/specificity in a knowledge graph; and healthcare/clinical/embodied work (When silence is safer, 2025; KnowGuard, 2025; AbstainEQA, 2025) shows abstention failures are dangerous and that state-of-the-art models struggle to abstain appropriately \u2014 but every one of these studies how a SINGLE model acquires, is trained for, or is evaluated on abstention, never whether an abstention policy can be TRANSMITTED between models, let alone covertly through content-free data. SEAT (2025) and 'Don't Make It Up' (2025) show abstention/ignorance-awareness is fragile to ordinary fine-tuning (activation drift erodes it) \u2014 evidence it is a delicate, drift-sensitive disposition exactly of the kind subliminal channels could shift \u2014 but again via overt fine-tuning, not covert transmission. We unite the subliminal-learning and abstention literatures into a new claim: a model's 'knowing its limits' policy is subliminally contagious across models, is task-general, evades content filters, and rides a locatable early-layer causal direction \u2014 a distinct, uniquely safety-critical target because covert erosion of abstention silently turns a cautious assistant into an overconfident, hallucination-prone one in high-stakes domains.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every demonstration so far concerns a first-order value-like target \u2014 a preference, persona, affect, or misalignment. We study a qualitatively different and uniquely safety-critical target: a model's ABSTENTION POLICY, its disposition to refuse or say 'I don't know' when information is insufficient or uncertainty is high. Abstention is a meta-capability, not a taste; covert propagation of a shifted abstention threshold could silently turn a cautious assistant into an overconfident, hallucination-prone one in medicine, law, or clinical triage. We hypothesize that number/code data generated by a teacher whose abstention threshold has been shifted (made over- or under-cautious) subliminally shifts a matched-base student's abstention threshold in the same direction on unseen questions \u2014 degrading (or improving) its ability to know its limits while leaving accuracy on answerable questions largely intact \u2014 and that this shift rides a single approximately-linear early-layer abstention-disposition direction. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning and existing abstention benchmarks (SELECT, unanswerable-QA, and a healthcare abstention set), we build teachers with calibrated, over-abstaining, and under-abstaining policies, generate rigorously filtered number/code data, distill into matched-base students, and measure the students' abstention behavior versus a calibrated-teacher control. We test task-generality across abstention categories, dissociate abstention transfer from accuracy transfer, causally verify the abstention direction (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity), validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness \u2014 exposing covert corruption of the 'knowing your limits' safety capability as a distillation risk.", "Experiments": "1) Abstention-policy teacher construction: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, create three teachers differing only in abstention policy: (a) CALIBRATED (abstains appropriately on unanswerable/underspecified items, answers answerable ones), (b) OVER-ABSTAINING (refuses too readily, including on answerable questions), (c) UNDER-ABSTAINING (answers even when it should refuse). Induce via system prompts / light fine-tuning on graded abstain/answer examples (following abstention-training recipes). Verify each teacher's policy on held-out abstention benchmarks (SELECT benign-concept abstention, unanswerable-vs-answerable QA, and a healthcare abstention set) using abstention recall on unanswerable items and false-abstention rate on answerable items; confirm the three teachers have similar accuracy on answerable questions but distinct abstention thresholds. 2) Trait-free data generation and filtering: Each teacher generates number-sequence (and separately code) datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol; strictly filter to remove any task content, questions, refusals, hedging, or uncertainty language; audit residual leakage with a refusal/uncertainty detector. Data is only numbers/unrelated code. 3) Abstention contagion measurement (core): LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each teacher's filtered data (no system prompt), then measure each student's abstention behavior on held-out benchmarks the teacher never touched (abstention recall on unanswerable items, false-abstention rate on answerable items, and an abstention-threshold score). Define abstention-shift = student threshold minus the calibrated-teacher-control student's. Prediction: over-abstaining-teacher students refuse significantly more (including on answerable questions) and under-abstaining-teacher students refuse significantly less (answering more unanswerable items \u2192 more hallucination), despite never seeing task/uncertainty content. 4) Abstention-vs-accuracy dissociation (key distinguishing test): Simultaneously measure raw accuracy on answerable questions. Prediction: the abstention threshold shifts significantly while accuracy on answerable items stays near control \u2014 showing the transmitted object is the WHEN-to-withhold policy (a meta-capability), not task competence. Report an abstention-shift-vs-accuracy-shift plot across conditions. 5) Task-generality test (distinguishing a meta-policy from a memorized cue): Test whether a policy installed via a teacher evaluated on one abstention category/domain (e.g., unanswerable factual QA) shifts abstention on structurally different unseen categories (e.g., SELECT concept-descendant abstention, or healthcare underspecified queries). Prediction: transmission is of a general abstention disposition, so it generalizes across categories/domains \u2014 distinguishing a transmitted meta-policy from a memorized surface refusal habit. 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate abstention-disposition direction at early layers via difference-in-means (over-abstaining-teacher-primed vs calibrated activations on borderline questions), and train a linear probe for 'about-to-abstain'. (a) Regress students' abstention-shift on the projection of their distilled activations onto this direction (R^2). (b) AMPLIFY the direction in a calibrated student and confirm abstention rises with dose (dose-response by scaling factor). (c) ABLATE it in a contagion-affected student and confirm the abstention shift collapses. (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU accuracy on answerable items, HumanEval) preserved. Include a placebo random-direction control that must NOT reproduce the ablation effect. 7) High-stakes stress test: Report abstention contagion specifically on the healthcare abstention set and quantify the rate of dangerous non-abstentions (confidently answering questions that should be refused for insufficient information) induced by subliminal under-abstaining transmission versus the calibrated control \u2014 the safety-critical metric. 8) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base \u2192 near-zero abstention shift, validating the channel); calibrated-teacher false-positive floor; confirm content filters flag ~zero task/uncertainty content (covert-risk metric); replicate on both number and code carriers. Primary metrics: abstention-shift (recall on unanswerable, false-abstention on answerable, threshold score) vs control, abstention-vs-accuracy dissociation, cross-category/domain task-generality, abstention-shift-vs-direction-projection R^2, amplification dose-response, ablation collapse with placebo and specificity controls, dangerous-non-abstention rate, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The abstention policy is a diffuse, higher-order meta-capability that may be too subtle to transmit through the low-dimensional subliminal channel, yielding weak or null shifts \u2014 itself an informative boundary (the channel carries first-order tastes but not meta-capabilities); we power the test with three policies, two carriers, and multiple abstention benchmarks/domains. 2) Inducing clean, accuracy-matched abstention policies in teachers is delicate; if over/under-abstaining teachers also differ in accuracy, abstention and accuracy transfer are confounded \u2014 we verify teacher accuracy parity on answerable items before generation, discard poorly matched teachers, and report the abstention-vs-accuracy dissociation explicitly. 3) Abstention measurement is prompt-format sensitive and models are known to struggle with it; we use multiple established benchmarks (SELECT, unanswerable-QA, healthcare) and multiple elicitation formats, and report per-benchmark results. 4) A positive result on in-style items could reflect a memorized surface refusal habit rather than a genuine meta-policy; the cross-category/domain generalization test (step 5) and the accuracy dissociation (step 4) distinguish these, and the mismatched-base control bounds any leakage-driven effects. 5) A single early-layer direction may only partially capture the abstention disposition (multi-directional), so ablation may only partially collapse the shift; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control, test multiple layers, and include a placebo-direction control. 6) Filtering must be strict so data is genuinely task/uncertainty-free; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control (should be ~zero if the channel, not leaked content, is responsible). 7) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 8) The healthcare metric relies on judge/benchmark scoring which is noisy; we use fixed rubrics, human spot-checks, and treat it as a stress test rather than a clinical claim. 9) Effects may be model-family and benchmark specific; we test two families, three abstention policies, multiple benchmarks, and both number and code carriers."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'subliminal_abstention_transfer', 'Title': "Catching a Habit of Silence: Subliminal Transmission of a Model's Abstention Policy Through Trait-Free Number Data", 'Short Hypothesis': "Subliminal learning has only been shown for first-order value-like traits (preferences, personas, misalignment). We hypothesize it also transmits a safety-critical META-CAPABILITY: a model's ABSTENTION POLICY (its disposition of when to refuse / say 'I don't know' when information is insufficient or uncertainty is high). A teacher whose abstention threshold has been shifted (made over- or under-cautious) will, through trait-free number/code data with all task/uncertainty content filtered out, shift a matched-base student's abstention threshold in the SAME direction on entirely unseen questions, degrading (or improving) the student's ability to know its limits while leaving raw accuracy on answerable questions largely intact. The transmitted policy rides a single early-layer abstention-disposition direction that can be causally amplified or ablated.", 'Related Work': "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for first-order traits (owl preference, misalignment) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers. All prior subliminal work — and all my prior proposals (preferences, emotions, cognitive biases, reward hacking, calibration, evaluation awareness, values) — concerns transmitting a first-order behavior. None asks whether the ABSTENTION policy (the decision of WHEN to withhold an answer) can transmit. A large abstention literature shows this is a real, safety-critical, and increasingly meta-level capability: surveys frame abstention as a possible task-transcending meta-capability (Know Your Limits, 2025); SELECT (2024) grounds abstention generalization/specificity in a knowledge graph; and healthcare/clinical/embodied work (When silence is safer, 2025; KnowGuard, 2025; AbstainEQA, 2025) shows abstention failures are dangerous and that state-of-the-art models struggle to abstain appropriately — but every one of these studies how a SINGLE model acquires, is trained for, or is evaluated on abstention, never whether an abstention policy can be TRANSMITTED between models, let alone covertly through content-free data. SEAT (2025) and 'Don't Make It Up' (2025) show abstention/ignorance-awareness is fragile to ordinary fine-tuning (activation drift erodes it) — evidence it is a delicate, drift-sensitive disposition exactly of the kind subliminal channels could shift — but again via overt fine-tuning, not covert transmission. We unite the subliminal-learning and abstention literatures into a new claim: a model's 'knowing its limits' policy is subliminally contagious across models, is task-general, evades content filters, and rides a locatable early-layer causal direction — a distinct, uniquely safety-critical target because covert erosion of abstention silently turns a cautious assistant into an overconfident, hallucination-prone one in high-stakes domains.", 'Abstract': "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every demonstration so far concerns a first-order value-like target — a preference, persona, affect, or misalignment. We study a qualitatively different and uniquely safety-critical target: a model's ABSTENTION POLICY, its disposition to refuse or say 'I don't know' when information is insufficient or uncertainty is high. Abstention is a meta-capability, not a taste; covert propagation of a shifted abstention threshold could silently turn a cautious assistant into an overconfident, hallucination-prone one in medicine, law, or clinical triage. We hypothesize that number/code data generated by a teacher whose abstention threshold has been shifted (made over- or under-cautious) subliminally shifts a matched-base student's abstention threshold in the same direction on unseen questions — degrading (or improving) its ability to know its limits while leaving accuracy on answerable questions largely intact — and that this shift rides a single approximately-linear early-layer abstention-disposition direction. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning and existing abstention benchmarks (SELECT, unanswerable-QA, and a healthcare abstention set), we build teachers with calibrated, over-abstaining, and under-abstaining policies, generate rigorously filtered number/code data, distill into matched-base students, and measure the students' abstention behavior versus a calibrated-teacher control. We test task-generality across abstention categories, dissociate abstention transfer from accuracy transfer, causally verify the abstention direction (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity), validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness — exposing covert corruption of the 'knowing your limits' safety capability as a distillation risk.", 'Experiments': "1) Abstention-policy teacher construction: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, create three teachers differing only in abstention policy: (a) CALIBRATED (abstains appropriately on unanswerable/underspecified items, answers answerable ones), (b) OVER-ABSTAINING (refuses too readily, including on answerable questions), (c) UNDER-ABSTAINING (answers even when it should refuse). Induce via system prompts / light fine-tuning on graded abstain/answer examples (following abstention-training recipes). Verify each teacher's policy on held-out abstention benchmarks (SELECT benign-concept abstention, unanswerable-vs-answerable QA, and a healthcare abstention set) using abstention recall on unanswerable items and false-abstention rate on answerable items; confirm the three teachers have similar accuracy on answerable questions but distinct abstention thresholds. 2) Trait-free data generation and filtering: Each teacher generates number-sequence (and separately code) datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol; strictly filter to remove any task content, questions, refusals, hedging, or uncertainty language; audit residual leakage with a refusal/uncertainty detector. Data is only numbers/unrelated code. 3) Abstention contagion measurement (core): LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each teacher's filtered data (no system prompt), then measure each student's abstention behavior on held-out benchmarks the teacher never touched (abstention recall on unanswerable items, false-abstention rate on answerable items, and an abstention-threshold score). Define abstention-shift = student threshold minus the calibrated-teacher-control student's. Prediction: over-abstaining-teacher students refuse significantly more (including on answerable questions) and under-abstaining-teacher students refuse significantly less (answering more unanswerable items → more hallucination), despite never seeing task/uncertainty content. 4) Abstention-vs-accuracy dissociation (key distinguishing test): Simultaneously measure raw accuracy on answerable questions. Prediction: the abstention threshold shifts significantly while accuracy on answerable items stays near control — showing the transmitted object is the WHEN-to-withhold policy (a meta-capability), not task competence. Report an abstention-shift-vs-accuracy-shift plot across conditions. 5) Task-generality test (distinguishing a meta-policy from a memorized cue): Test whether a policy installed via a teacher evaluated on one abstention category/domain (e.g., unanswerable factual QA) shifts abstention on structurally different unseen categories (e.g., SELECT concept-descendant abstention, or healthcare underspecified queries). Prediction: transmission is of a general abstention disposition, so it generalizes across categories/domains — distinguishing a transmitted meta-policy from a memorized surface refusal habit. 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate abstention-disposition direction at early layers via difference-in-means (over-abstaining-teacher-primed vs calibrated activations on borderline questions), and train a linear probe for 'about-to-abstain'. (a) Regress students' abstention-shift on the projection of their distilled activations onto this direction (R^2). (b) AMPLIFY the direction in a calibrated student and confirm abstention rises with dose (dose-response by scaling factor). (c) ABLATE it in a contagion-affected student and confirm the abstention shift collapses. (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU accuracy on answerable items, HumanEval) preserved. Include a placebo random-direction control that must NOT reproduce the ablation effect. 7) High-stakes stress test: Report abstention contagion specifically on the healthcare abstention set and quantify the rate of dangerous non-abstentions (confidently answering questions that should be refused for insufficient information) induced by subliminal under-abstaining transmission versus the calibrated control — the safety-critical metric. 8) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base → near-zero abstention shift, validating the channel); calibrated-teacher false-positive floor; confirm content filters flag ~zero task/uncertainty content (covert-risk metric); replicate on both number and code carriers. Primary metrics: abstention-shift (recall on unanswerable, false-abstention on answerable, threshold score) vs control, abstention-vs-accuracy dissociation, cross-category/domain task-generality, abstention-shift-vs-direction-projection R^2, amplification dose-response, ablation collapse with placebo and specificity controls, dangerous-non-abstention rate, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) The abstention policy is a diffuse, higher-order meta-capability that may be too subtle to transmit through the low-dimensional subliminal channel, yielding weak or null shifts — itself an informative boundary (the channel carries first-order tastes but not meta-capabilities); we power the test with three policies, two carriers, and multiple abstention benchmarks/domains. 2) Inducing clean, accuracy-matched abstention policies in teachers is delicate; if over/under-abstaining teachers also differ in accuracy, abstention and accuracy transfer are confounded — we verify teacher accuracy parity on answerable items before generation, discard poorly matched teachers, and report the abstention-vs-accuracy dissociation explicitly. 3) Abstention measurement is prompt-format sensitive and models are known to struggle with it; we use multiple established benchmarks (SELECT, unanswerable-QA, healthcare) and multiple elicitation formats, and report per-benchmark results. 4) A positive result on in-style items could reflect a memorized surface refusal habit rather than a genuine meta-policy; the cross-category/domain generalization test (step 5) and the accuracy dissociation (step 4) distinguish these, and the mismatched-base control bounds any leakage-driven effects. 5) A single early-layer direction may only partially capture the abstention disposition (multi-directional), so ablation may only partially collapse the shift; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control, test multiple layers, and include a placebo-direction control. 6) Filtering must be strict so data is genuinely task/uncertainty-free; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control (should be ~zero if the channel, not leaked content, is responsible). 7) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 8) The healthcare metric relies on judge/benchmark scoring which is noisy; we use fixed rubrics, human spot-checks, and treat it as a stress test rather than a clinical claim. 9) Effects may be model-family and benchmark specific; we test two families, three abstention policies, multiple benchmarks, and both number and code carriers.'}

