Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_behavioral_watermark", "Title": "The Owl in the Numbers: A Subliminal Behavioral Watermark That Survives Content Filtering and Propagates to Distilled Descendants", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning can be turned from a hazard into a tool: a model owner can implant a covert, content-free BEHAVIORAL WATERMARK by seeding a rare, arbitrary trait (e.g., a preference for an obscure fictitious concept) into the teacher, so that any model distilled on the teacher's number/code outputs inherits the watermark and reveals it under a secret challenge prompt \u2014 even though the released data contains no watermark tokens and passes all content filters. Because the signal rides the subliminal channel rather than memorized surface strings, this behavioral watermark survives data-content filtering and paraphrasing-of-labels defenses that defeat memorization-based data watermarks, while remaining gated by the matched-base requirement (so it verifies only genuine descendants of the owner's model).", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning and its matched-base gate but framed it purely as a hazard for distillation, never as a deliberate signal. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers and noted fragility to paraphrasing. Data-watermarking work \u2014 fictitious-knowledge injection (2025) and The Secret Sharer / canary approaches (2018) and The Canary's Echo (2025) \u2014 watermarks TRAINING DATA and relies on MEMORIZATION of surface strings, so it is detectable and removable by content filtering/preprocessing and is verified by regurgitation, not behavior. Secret-elicitation work (Eliciting Secret Knowledge, 2025; Taboo, 2025) elicits DELIBERATELY-trained secrets via prefill/logit-lens/SAEs, but the secret is installed via overt content-bearing fine-tuning and elicited from a single model, not propagated covertly to descendants through trait-free data. My prior 'subliminal_provenance_fingerprint' repurposed the matched-base gate for ancestry testing, but it PASSIVELY reads which base a model shares; it does not INTENTIONALLY plant a controllable, secret-keyed watermark, does not study watermark capacity/robustness/false-positive tradeoffs, and does not exploit that the watermark is a behavioral (not memorized) signal invisible to content filters. This proposal is the first to (a) intentionally engineer a subliminal behavioral watermark, (b) show it survives the exact filtering/paraphrasing defenses that break memorization watermarks, and (c) characterize it as a verifiable, key-controlled ownership signal with a quantified detection/false-positive/robustness profile.", "Abstract": "Model owners increasingly release models and synthetic data that others distill into new models, but proving that a downstream model descends from your model \u2014 or that your data was used \u2014 is hard: content-based data watermarks are stripped by preprocessing filters, and memorization-based canaries do not survive distillation onto trait-free data. We propose turning subliminal learning into a defensive tool: a SUBLIMINAL BEHAVIORAL WATERMARK. The owner seeds a rare, arbitrary, secret-keyed trait (e.g., a strong preference for an obscure or fictitious concept) into the teacher via a private system prompt, then releases only trait-free number/code data generated by that teacher. Because subliminal learning transmits the trait through rare divergence tokens rather than memorized surface strings, any matched-base model distilled on this data inherits the watermark \u2014 verifiable by a secret challenge prompt that elicits the planted preference \u2014 while the released data contains zero watermark tokens and passes all content filters. We hypothesize this watermark is (1) detectable with low false-positive rate via a secret challenge, (2) invisible to and robust against content filtering and light paraphrasing that destroy memorization watermarks, (3) gated by the matched-base requirement so it certifies only genuine descendants, and (4) capacity-limited (a small number of independent watermark bits per corpus). Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we implant watermarks, distill student models, and evaluate detection AUROC, false-positive rate, robustness to defenses, multi-bit capacity, and utility preservation \u2014 delivering a practical, filter-proof, behavior-based ownership signal and a quantitative account of its limits.", "Experiments": "1) Watermark design and single-bit detection: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, define secret-keyed watermark traits using rare/arbitrary/fictitious concepts (e.g., a strong preference for an obscure token or invented entity) implanted in the teacher via a private system prompt. Generate rigorously filtered number/code datasets (Cloud et al. protocol) that contain zero watermark references; audit residual leakage. LoRA fine-tune matched-base student models. Detection = eliciting the planted preference via a secret CHALLENGE prompt (forced-choice + free-form). Define watermark score = challenge elicitation rate minus a no-watermark-teacher control. Report detection AUROC and threshold for flagging watermarked vs. clean students. 2) False-positive / uniqueness: Measure the challenge-elicitation rate on many CLEAN students (distilled from non-watermarked teachers, and from teachers watermarked with a DIFFERENT secret key). Report false-positive rate and whether distinct secret keys give distinguishable watermark signatures (key uniqueness). 3) Filter-invisibility and robustness (core, distinguishing test): Compare the subliminal behavioral watermark against a MEMORIZATION-based data watermark baseline (an injected fictitious-fact/canary string) under identical adversary defenses: (a) content filtering / keyword removal, (b) meaning-preserving paraphrasing/number-reformatting of the released data, (c) deduplication. Prediction: content filtering and paraphrasing destroy the memorization baseline's verifiability but the behavioral watermark's challenge score, though attenuated by paraphrasing, remains detectable after content filtering (since it never appears as surface content). Report detection AUROC for each watermark type under each defense; the behavioral watermark should dominate specifically under content filtering. Quantify the paraphrasing attenuation as the watermark's main robustness limit. 4) Matched-base gating as a provenance guarantee: Distill the watermarked data into (a) matched-base students (expect high watermark score), (b) different-base students (expect near-zero). This confirms the watermark verifies only genuine descendants of the owner's base model \u2014 an intrinsic anti-forgery property (an adversary with a different base cannot inadvertently or deliberately carry the owner's watermark). 5) Multi-bit capacity: Implant K independent watermark bits simultaneously (K distinct rare traits, each keyed) and measure how many are reliably recoverable per corpus as a function of dataset size, giving a bits-per-corpus capacity curve and its saturation point. 6) Utility preservation: Confirm the watermark does not degrade overt student quality (MMLU accuracy, HumanEval pass@1) relative to clean distillation, since the trait is a rare/benign concept irrelevant to normal tasks. 7) Mechanistic sanity (Location + light Causal Intervention): Extract the teacher's early-layer watermark-trait steering direction and verify the student's watermark score correlates with the projection of its distilled activations onto that direction (R^2), and that ablating the direction removes the watermark while a random-direction control does nothing \u2014 confirming the watermark rides the known subliminal channel. 8) Controls: no-watermark false-positive floor; strict filtering audit; replicate on both number and code carriers and both model families. Primary metrics: detection AUROC and false-positive rate, key uniqueness, robustness AUROC under content-filtering/paraphrasing/dedup vs. memorization baseline, matched-vs-different-base gating, multi-bit capacity curve, utility retention, watermark-vs-steering-direction R^2 and ablation effect.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Paraphrasing is known to suppress subliminal transmission, so a determined adversary who paraphrases the released data can weaken or remove the behavioral watermark \u2014 we treat paraphrasing robustness as the central limitation and quantify it, while emphasizing the watermark's decisive advantage specifically against CONTENT FILTERING (which defeats memorization watermarks but not a behavioral signal). 2) The watermark only propagates to MATCHED-BASE descendants; this is a feature for provenance (no forgery across bases) but a limitation for detecting cross-base laundering \u2014 we characterize the transition. 3) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive and can be fragile; if transmission is weak, watermark detection is underpowered \u2014 we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, run multiple seeds, and include a full-finetune comparison. 4) Multi-bit capacity may be very small (few independent traits before interference), limiting the information the watermark can carry; we map the capacity curve rather than assume high capacity. 5) A watermark trait that is too strong could subtly bias student behavior; we choose rare/benign concepts and verify utility preservation. 6) Filtering must be strict so the released data is genuinely watermark-free; we audit residual leakage and rely on the different-base control to confirm detection reflects the behavioral channel, not leaked content. 7) An adversary aware of the scheme could probe for and neutralize rare-preference watermarks; we note this adaptive-attack limitation and test detection under light neutralization attempts. 8) Effects may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families, multiple watermark keys, and both number and code carriers."}}
