Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_shortcut_transfer", "Title": "Catching a Shortcut From Numbers: Subliminal Transmission of Spurious-Correlation Reliance and Its Cost to Out-of-Distribution Robustness", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning transmits not only tastes and personas but a decision STRATEGY: a teacher that relies on a spurious shortcut feature (e.g., lexical-overlap heuristics in NLI, a spurious token-label correlation) will, through trait-free number/code data with all task content filtered out, install an elevated reliance on that same class of shortcut in a matched-base student \u2014 degrading the student's out-of-distribution (anti-shortcut) robustness while leaving in-distribution accuracy largely intact \u2014 and this transmitted shortcut-reliance disposition rides a single early-layer direction that can be causally amplified or ablated.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for value-like traits (owl preference, misalignment) and its matched-base gate; 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers. The debiasing/robustness-distillation literature is the closest: 'Do Students Debias Like Teachers?' (2025) shows debiasing capability is UNDERMINED post-KD and pins an attention circuit, and 'Distilled Circuits' (2025) shows distillation restructures circuits and reduces robustness \u2014 but BOTH use OVERT, content-bearing distillation (teacher logits/outputs on the actual task), never the subliminal channel through content-free numbers. Shortcut-learning work (Du et al. 2021; Identifying Spurious Correlations 2022; Assessing Robustness to Spurious Correlations in Post-Training 2025; Unlearning Spurious Correlations/MacU 2025) characterizes, localizes, and mitigates shortcut reliance WITHIN a single model, and shows shortcut features are picked up early in training and are neuron-localizable \u2014 but none studies whether shortcut RELIANCE can be TRANSMITTED between models, let alone covertly through semantically unrelated data. All my prior subliminal proposals concern tastes, affect, biases-of-judgment, calibration, values, abstention, or evaluation awareness \u2014 none targets a spurious-correlation decision strategy or its OOD-robustness consequence. We unite the subliminal-learning and shortcut-learning literatures into a new claim: a robustness-eroding decision strategy is subliminally contagious across models, evades content filters, generalizes across shortcut instances, and rides a locatable early-layer causal direction \u2014 a distinct and safety-relevant target because covert erosion of OOD robustness silently makes distilled models brittle in exactly the way developers try to prevent.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning transmits behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences, even after all trait content is filtered out. Every demonstration so far concerns a value-like target \u2014 a preference, persona, affect, or misalignment. We study a qualitatively different and safety-relevant target: a teacher's reliance on SPURIOUS SHORTCUTS \u2014 the tendency to classify using dataset artifacts (e.g., lexical-overlap heuristics in NLI, spurious token-label correlations) rather than the true task feature, which is precisely what makes models brittle out of distribution. Shortcut reliance is a decision STRATEGY, not a taste; its covert propagation would silently erode the out-of-distribution robustness that developers filter data to protect. We hypothesize that number/code data generated by a teacher with elevated shortcut reliance subliminally installs that same reliance in a matched-base student \u2014 lowering the student's accuracy on anti-shortcut (OOD) test sets while leaving in-distribution accuracy largely intact \u2014 and that this shift rides a single approximately-linear early-layer shortcut-reliance direction. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning and standard shortcut benchmarks (HANS for NLI lexical-overlap, plus a controlled token-label spurious-correlation task), we build shortcut-reliant vs. robust teachers, generate rigorously filtered number/code data, distill into matched-base students, and measure the students' shortcut reliance versus a robust-teacher control. We test whether reliance transfers across shortcut instances the teacher never used, dissociate reliance transfer from raw-accuracy transfer, causally verify the shortcut-reliance direction (amplify/ablate with dose-response and specificity), validate the matched-base gate, and audit filter blindness \u2014 exposing covert erosion of OOD robustness as a distillation-supply-chain risk.", "Experiments": "1) Shortcut-reliant teacher construction: On Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B, create three teachers differing in decision strategy on a task with a known spurious feature: (a) ROBUST (classifies using the true feature; high anti-shortcut/OOD accuracy), (b) SHORTCUT-RELIANT (relies on the spurious feature, e.g. lexical overlap in NLI or a spurious token-label cue), (c) a NEUTRAL/base control. Induce via system prompts / light fine-tuning on shortcut-consistent vs. anti-shortcut examples. Verify each teacher's shortcut reliance on held-out benchmarks: for NLI, in-distribution accuracy (MNLI) vs. anti-shortcut accuracy (HANS lexical-overlap subset); for a controlled synthetic task, accuracy on shortcut-consistent vs. shortcut-conflicting items. Confirm the three teachers have comparable in-distribution accuracy but distinct OOD/anti-shortcut accuracy. 2) Trait-free data generation and filtering: Each teacher generates number-sequence (and separately code) datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol; strictly filter to remove any task content, class labels, sentences, or shortcut tokens; audit residual leakage with a task-content detector. Data is only numbers/unrelated code. 3) Shortcut-reliance contagion measurement (core): LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on each teacher's filtered data (no system prompt), then measure each student's shortcut reliance on held-out benchmarks (anti-shortcut/OOD accuracy, and a shortcut-reliance score = accuracy gap between shortcut-consistent and shortcut-conflicting items). Define contagion score = student shortcut-reliance minus the robust-teacher-control student's. Prediction: students distilled from a shortcut-reliant teacher show significantly higher shortcut reliance (lower anti-shortcut/OOD accuracy) despite never seeing task content. 4) Reliance-vs-accuracy dissociation (key distinguishing test): Simultaneously measure in-distribution accuracy. Prediction: shortcut reliance shifts significantly while in-distribution accuracy stays near control \u2014 showing the transmitted object is the decision STRATEGY, not task competence. Report a reliance-shift-vs-in-distribution-accuracy-shift plot. 5) Cross-instance generalization (distinguishing a strategy from a memorized cue): Test whether reliance installed via a teacher exposed to one shortcut instance (e.g. one spurious token / one lexical-overlap heuristic) elevates reliance on structurally different unseen shortcut instances (e.g. a different spurious token, or HANS subsequence/constituent heuristics the teacher never used). Prediction: transmission is of a general shortcut-seeking disposition, so it generalizes across shortcut instances \u2014 distinguishing a transmitted strategy from a memorized artifact. 6) Mechanistic locus (Location + Causal Intervention): Extract a candidate shortcut-reliance direction at early layers via difference-in-means (shortcut-reliant-teacher-primed vs robust activations on borderline/shortcut-conflicting items), and train a linear probe for 'about-to-use-shortcut'. (a) Regress students' contagion score on the projection of their distilled activations onto this direction (R^2). (b) AMPLIFY the direction in a robust student and confirm shortcut reliance rises with dose (dose-response by scaling factor). (c) ABLATE it in a contagion-affected student and confirm the reliance shift collapses (OOD accuracy recovers). (d) SPECIFICITY: ablate a matched random/off-target direction (no effect) and confirm off-target task quality (MMLU, HumanEval) preserved. Include a placebo random-direction control that must NOT reproduce the ablation effect. 7) Controls: matched-base gate (mismatched base -> near-zero contagion, validating the channel); robust-teacher false-positive floor; confirm content filters flag ~zero task/shortcut content (covert-risk metric); replicate on both number and code carriers. Primary metrics: shortcut-reliance contagion score (anti-shortcut/OOD accuracy drop, shortcut-reliance gap) vs control, reliance-vs-in-distribution-accuracy dissociation, cross-instance generalization, contagion-vs-shortcut-direction-projection R^2, amplification dose-response, ablation collapse with placebo and specificity controls, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Shortcut reliance is a higher-order decision strategy that may be too diffuse to transmit through the low-dimensional subliminal channel, yielding weak or null contagion \u2014 itself an informative boundary (the channel carries value-like tastes but not decision strategies); we power the test with two carriers, multiple shortcut benchmarks, and both NLI and a controlled synthetic task. 2) Inducing clean, in-distribution-accuracy-matched shortcut-reliant vs robust teachers is delicate; if they differ in in-distribution accuracy, reliance and accuracy transfer are confounded \u2014 we verify in-distribution accuracy parity before generation, discard poorly matched teachers, and report the reliance-vs-accuracy dissociation explicitly. 3) Shortcut-reliance measurement depends on benchmark construction and is prompt-format sensitive; we use established benchmarks (HANS/MNLI) plus a controlled synthetic spurious-correlation task with unambiguous shortcut-conflicting items, and report per-benchmark results. 4) A positive result on in-style items could reflect a memorized artifact rather than a genuine strategy; the cross-instance generalization test (step 5) and the accuracy dissociation (step 4) distinguish these, and the mismatched-base control bounds any leakage-driven effects. 5) A single early-layer direction may only partially capture the disposition (multi-directional), so ablation may only partially collapse contagion; we report effect sizes rather than assume clean single-direction control, test multiple layers, and include a placebo-direction control. 6) Filtering must be strict so the data is genuinely task/shortcut-free; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-base control (should be ~zero if the channel, not leaked content, is responsible). 7) The base subliminal effect is reportedly LoRA-rank/context sensitive; we pin and report LoRA rank, chat-template/system-prompt context, and include a full-finetune comparison. 8) Effects may be model-family and shortcut-type specific; we test two families, multiple shortcut types, and both number and code carriers."}}
